// Scenario Lab asset library — The Social Animal - Elliot Aronson (12th Ed)
const _AL_social_animal = {"id":"social_animal","title":"The Social Animal - Elliot Aronson (12th Ed)","chapters":[{"id":"sa_ch01a","title":"Chapter 1: What Is Social Psychology? (Part 1)","pages":"19-30","assets":[{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Aronson First Law: People Who Do Crazy Things Are Not Necessarily Crazy","subject_baseline":"A rational, well-educated individual drives from North Carolina to Washington DC with an AR-15 rifle. He enters a family pizza restaurant and fires multiple shots. No one is hurt. He surrenders. The public narrative: he must be mentally ill or evil.","interrogation_transcript":"Edgar Welch believed the fake news story that Comet Ping Pong pizzeria housed a child sex trafficking ring run by Hillary Clinton. He was a volunteer firefighter, a concerned father, a churchgoer. His friends described him as normal and rational. He consumed hours of internet conspiracy content that reinforced the same narrative through repetition and social proof from online communities.","observed_nonverbal_log":["Subject consumed echo chamber content that reinforced conspiracy beliefs","Subject believed he was acting heroically to save children","Online community provided social proof that validated his mission","Dispositional attribution (crazy) is easier than acknowledging situational vulnerability"],"question":"Why do most people instinctively attribute Welch actions to mental illness rather than to the situational power of misinformation, social proof, and echo chambers?","correct_diagnosis":"The fundamental attribution error combined with the just-world hypothesis. Attributing behavior to disposition protects us from the uncomfortable truth that similar situational pressures could affect anyone. It is more comforting to believe he was crazy than that this could happen to anyone.","distractors":["Occam razor - the simplest explanation of mental illness is usually correct for extreme behavior","Clinical evidence - Welch had documented mental health issues that were the primary cause","Moral reasoning - normal people do not commit armed violence, so the actor must be abnormal"],"diagnostic_breakdown":"Stage 1 - Public instinctively labels the actor as deranged. Stage 2 - Situational evidence emerges: echo chamber, fake news ecosystem, social proof. Stage 3 - Cognitive dissonance: accepting situational influence means admitting personal vulnerability. Stage 4 - Most people default to dispositional attribution because it preserves the illusion of personal immunity. Aronson First Law reminds us that powerful situational forces can make rational people behave irrationally.","player_mission":"Why do most people instinctively attribute Welch actions to mental illness rather than to the situational power of misinformation, social proof, and echo chambers?","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A sports-broadcast studio desk buried in ledger sheets; each audited prediction gets stamped in red reading HINDSIGHT 20-20 or in green reading BEFORE THE FACT as a screen reveals the experiment outcomes.","key_concepts":["Social psychology is the scientific study of the influence of the real, imagined, or implied presence of others upon our thoughts, emotions, beliefs, and behavior, and of how we influence others","Aristotle was the first serious thinker to call our species 'the social animal'","As diverse as the chapter's opening examples seem, they all contain a common factor: social influence","The hindsight bias is our tendency to overestimate our powers of prediction once we know the outcome of a given event","Social psychological findings seem far more obvious to students who have been told what happened in a study than to students asked to predict what will happen","Aronson's first law states that people who do crazy things are not necessarily crazy","The dispositional view assumes that people who do crazy things have a personality disposition to be crazy, and that people who do stupid things must be stupid","Dispositionalism is often a mistake and an oversimplification, and we pay a price for it by feeling smug about our own invulnerability to pressures","The dispositional view moves the focus off improving situations and onto a narrow approach to fixing people","Most people, particularly those from Western cultures, assign personality traits like 'psychotic' or 'evil' spontaneously, without intention or conscious awareness","Some situations can cause a surprisingly large proportion of 'normal' adults to behave in unexpected, unappetizing, and sometimes abnormal ways","Helping us appreciate the situational view of human behavior is the central contribution of social psychology"]},{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Social Psychology | Hindsight Bias","topic":"The I-Knew-It-All-Along Effect: Hindsight Bias in Judgment","visual_frame_description":"Research participants predict the outcome of historical events. Another group is told the actual outcome and asked when they would have predicted it. The second group consistently claims they would have predicted the actual outcome, even though the first group showed no such predictive ability. The outcome knowledge contaminates their memory of their earlier mental state.","target_channel":"Outcome Knowledge Contamination | Bias Detection","question":"Why do people consistently overestimate what they would have predicted before an event occurred, claiming they knew it all along?","correct_cue":"Hindsight bias: once we know the outcome, our brain automatically reconstructs the prediction to fit the result. The knowledge contaminates our memory of our earlier mental state. We cannot unknow the outcome, so we wrongly believe we foresaw it.","distractors":["Overconfidence - people are naturally overconfident in their predictive abilities across all domains","Selective memory - we simply forget our incorrect predictions and remember only the correct ones","Pattern recognition - humans evolved to see causes, so we naturally construct post-hoc explanations"],"underlying_psychology":"Hindsight bias distorts legal judgments (jurors believe defendants should have foreseen harm), medical diagnoses (doctors believe the correct diagnosis was obvious after learning it), and historical analysis (historians see events as inevitable after they happen). The bias operates automatically. The cure: before learning the outcome, write down your prediction. Committing to a specific forecast makes the bias visible.","player_mission":"Why do people consistently overestimate what they would have predicted before an event occurred, claiming they knew it all along?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["Social psychology is the scientific study of the influence of the real, imagined, or implied presence of others upon our thoughts, emotions, beliefs, and behavior, and of how we influence others","Aristotle was the first serious thinker to call our species 'the social animal'","As diverse as the chapter's opening examples seem, they all contain a common factor: social influence","The hindsight bias is our tendency to overestimate our powers of prediction once we know the outcome of a given event","Social psychological findings seem far more obvious to students who have been told what happened in a study than to students asked to predict what will happen","Aronson's first law states that people who do crazy things are not necessarily crazy","The dispositional view assumes that people who do crazy things have a personality disposition to be crazy, and that people who do stupid things must be stupid","Dispositionalism is often a mistake and an oversimplification, and we pay a price for it by feeling smug about our own invulnerability to pressures","The dispositional view moves the focus off improving situations and onto a narrow approach to fixing people","Most people, particularly those from Western cultures, assign personality traits like 'psychotic' or 'evil' spontaneously, without intention or conscious awareness","Some situations can cause a surprisingly large proportion of 'normal' adults to behave in unexpected, unappetizing, and sometimes abnormal ways","Helping us appreciate the situational view of human behavior is the central contribution of social psychology"]}]},{"id":"sa_ch01b","title":"Chapter 1: What Is Social Psychology? (Part 2)","pages":"19-30","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Social Psychology | Founding Principles","topic":"Construal and Lewin's Equation: The Foundation of Social Psychology","visual_frame_description":"Lewin's formula: B = f(P, E). Behavior is a function of the Person and the Environment. But the environment is not objective — it is the environment as the person subjectively interprets it, which Lewin called the life space. Two people in the same objective situation will behave differently because they construe the situation differently. Social psychology is the study of how people subjectively interpret their social world.","target_channel":"Subjective Interpretation | The Life Space","question":"Why do two people in the same objective situation often behave so differently?","correct_cue":"Because behavior is driven by construal — the subjective interpretation of the situation, not the objective reality. Each person brings different schemas, past experiences, goals, and moods to the same situation. Lewin captured this with B = f(P, E): behavior emerges from the interaction between the person and their perceived environment. Two people at the same party have completely different experiences because one construes it as exciting and the other construes it as anxiety-provoking. Social psychology is the science of understanding these subjective construals.","distractors":["Personality is the sole driver of behavior — the situation matters very little once personality is accounted for","Objective reality determines behavior — people respond to the actual features of the situation, not their interpretation","Behavior is purely a function of reinforcement history — past rewards shape current behavior regardless of construal"],"underlying_psychology":"Lewin's formula is the founding identity statement of social psychology. The discipline distinguishes itself from personality psychology (which focuses on P) and behaviorism (which focuses on objective E). The social psychological contribution is that the E in the equation is the perceived environment — how the person construes it. This is why the same stimulus can produce radically different responses in different people. Understanding construal is essential for understanding conformity, persuasion, prejudice, and every other topic in the field.","player_mission":"Why do two people in the same objective situation often behave so differently?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["Social psychology is the scientific study of the influence of the real, imagined, or implied presence of others upon our thoughts, emotions, beliefs, and behavior, and of how we influence others","Aristotle was the first serious thinker to call our species 'the social animal'","As diverse as the chapter's opening examples seem, they all contain a common factor: social influence","The hindsight bias is our tendency to overestimate our powers of prediction once we know the outcome of a given event","Social psychological findings seem far more obvious to students who have been told what happened in a study than to students asked to predict what will happen","Aronson's first law states that people who do crazy things are not necessarily crazy","The dispositional view assumes that people who do crazy things have a personality disposition to be crazy, and that people who do stupid things must be stupid","Dispositionalism is often a mistake and an oversimplification, and we pay a price for it by feeling smug about our own invulnerability to pressures","The dispositional view moves the focus off improving situations and onto a narrow approach to fixing people","Most people, particularly those from Western cultures, assign personality traits like 'psychotic' or 'evil' spontaneously, without intention or conscious awareness","Some situations can cause a surprisingly large proportion of 'normal' adults to behave in unexpected, unappetizing, and sometimes abnormal ways","Helping us appreciate the situational view of human behavior is the central contribution of social psychology"]}]},{"id":"sa_ch02a","title":"Chapter 2: Social Cognition (Part 1)","pages":"31-74","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Social Psychology | Confirmation Bias","topic":"The Extrovert Trap: Biased Questioning That Self-Confirms","visual_frame_description":"Two research assistants interview the same person with different hypotheses. Assistant A tests for extroversion and asks: What would you do to liven up a party? Assistant B tests for introversion and asks: What do you dislike about loud parties? Both find evidence confirming their hypothesis because the questions manufacture the confirming evidence.","target_channel":"Question Framing | Self-Fulfilling Hypothesis Testing","question":"Why do both interviewers confidently conclude their hypothesis was correct, despite interviewing the same person?","correct_cue":"Confirmation bias in question design: people ask questions likely to yield confirming evidence, not disconfirming evidence. The questions shape the answers, creating a self-fulfilling prophecy. The extrovert question can be answered by anyone, as can the introvert question - neither genuinely tests the hypothesis.","distractors":["Interviewer incompetence - both lacked training in neutral interviewing techniques","Subject inconsistency - the same person behaves differently with different interviewers","Social desirability - subjects tell each interviewer what they want to hear"],"underlying_psychology":"Snyder and Swann 1978 study demonstrates a subtle confirmation bias. When testing for extroversion, people naturally ask: What do you do to liven up a party? When testing for introversion: What do you dislike about parties? Anyone can answer both. The interviewer always finds supporting evidence, but it is manufactured by the question format. The cure: ask questions that could genuinely disconfirm your hypothesis.","player_mission":"Why do both interviewers confidently conclude their hypothesis was correct, despite interviewing the same person?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["Social cognition is the study of how people come to believe what they do and why these processes so frequently produce errors","Much of the time we use reason, facts, and critical analysis not to form our opinions but to confirm what we already see, feel, or believe","Most of us are cognitive misers who seek ways to conserve cognitive energy and simplify complexity, taking shortcuts and using rules of thumb","When we focus on surface similarities to make inferences, we are using the representativeness heuristic, so the expensive wine is assumed to be better","Consumer Reports found that young rats thrived on a diet of Lucky Charms, whereas a diet of 100 Percent Natural Granola actually stunted their growth","The availability heuristic predicts the likelihood of an event based on how easy it is to bring examples to mind","The overwhelming majority of people wrongly believe more Americans die from shark attacks and fires than from falling airplane parts and drowning","People who consume crime dramas and television news tend to vastly overestimate the crime rate, and the more they watch, the more fearful they become","Statements that rhyme or roll off the tongue are judged to be truer than statements that say the same thing less felicitously","The affect heuristic taps our feelings to shape evaluations, so a bad mood makes you likely to evaluate a job candidate more negatively","The mad cow study showed beef consumption fell when the alarming label was used but stayed the same when the technical disease names were used","We are more likely to rely on heuristics when we don't have time, are overloaded with information, the issue is unimportant, we lack knowledge, or emotions get in the way"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Social Psychology | Attribution Error","topic":"The Quiz Show Study: Roles Create Illusions of Superiority","subject_baseline":"Three participants. Roles randomly assigned: Questioner, Contestant, Observer. The Questioner creates difficult questions from their own knowledge. The Contestant tries to answer. The Observer watches.","interrogation_transcript":"The Questioner asks obscure trivia from their own expertise. The Contestant gets most wrong. The Observer rates the Questioner as significantly more intelligent than the Contestant - despite witnessing the random role assignment. Even the Contestant makes this error.","observed_nonverbal_log":["All participants knew roles were randomly assigned","Questioners chose their best subjects - an unfair advantage","Observers and contestants ignored the situational constraint","Even Questioners became uncomfortable with perceived superiority"],"question":"Why did observers ignore the obvious situational advantage and attribute the outcome to intelligence?","correct_diagnosis":"The Fundamental Attribution Error: we underestimate situational factors and overestimate dispositional traits. The observer sees the Questioner as brilliant and the Contestant as dim, attributing this to intelligence rather than role advantage - even though assignment was random and witnessed.","distractors":["The Questioner genuinely was more intelligent - the random draw happened to select smarter participants","Creating questions demonstrates superior intelligence regardless of role, so the attribution was accurate","Observers were not paying attention and did not realize the assignment was random"],"diagnostic_breakdown":"Stage 1 - Random Role Assignment. Stage 2 - Unequal Performance due to role privilege. Stage 3 - Attribution Error: despite knowing roles were random, observers attribute to disposition. Stage 4 - Real-World Parallel: we see CEOs as brilliant and janitors as less capable, ignoring the role advantages that shaped their paths.","player_mission":"Why did observers ignore the obvious situational advantage and attribute the outcome to intelligence?","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A banker's ledger floats above a crowded quad where every student wears a glowing spotlight; each audit entry the player files stamps the ledger with the bias's name in a different colored ink.","key_concepts":["Social cognition is the study of how people come to believe what they do and why these processes so frequently produce errors","Much of the time we use reason, facts, and critical analysis not to form our opinions but to confirm what we already see, feel, or believe","Most of us are cognitive misers who seek ways to conserve cognitive energy and simplify complexity, taking shortcuts and using rules of thumb","When we focus on surface similarities to make inferences, we are using the representativeness heuristic, so the expensive wine is assumed to be better","Consumer Reports found that young rats thrived on a diet of Lucky Charms, whereas a diet of 100 Percent Natural Granola actually stunted their growth","The availability heuristic predicts the likelihood of an event based on how easy it is to bring examples to mind","The overwhelming majority of people wrongly believe more Americans die from shark attacks and fires than from falling airplane parts and drowning","People who consume crime dramas and television news tend to vastly overestimate the crime rate, and the more they watch, the more fearful they become","Statements that rhyme or roll off the tongue are judged to be truer than statements that say the same thing less felicitously","The affect heuristic taps our feelings to shape evaluations, so a bad mood makes you likely to evaluate a job candidate more negatively","The mad cow study showed beef consumption fell when the alarming label was used but stayed the same when the technical disease names were used","We are more likely to rely on heuristics when we don't have time, are overloaded with information, the issue is unimportant, we lack knowledge, or emotions get in the way"]},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Social Psychology | Heuristics","topic":"Three Mental Shortcuts: Representativeness, Availability, Affect","question":"Which heuristic is at work in each scenario? (1) People avoid mad cow beef but eat beef described as bovine spongiform encephalopathy - same risk. (2) People overestimate shark attack deaths and underestimate stroke deaths. (3) People prefer a candidate whose name they recognize over one they do not.","correct_answer":"(1) Affect heuristic - the emotional reaction to mad cow drives judgment more than the scientific label. (2) Availability heuristic - dramatic vivid deaths are more mentally available than common ones. (3) Representativeness heuristic - familiarity is mistaken for quality.","distractors":["All are availability heuristics since they all involve information accessibility","All are anchoring since they all involve a reference point distorting judgment","(1) Representativeness, (2) Affect, (3) Availability"],"explanation":"Heuristics are mental shortcuts that usually work but produce predictable errors. The affect heuristic replaces difficult questions with easy ones. The availability heuristic judges probability by ease of recall. The representativeness heuristic judges category membership by similarity. Compliance professionals exploit these by making desired options feel good, look familiar, and appear typical.","player_mission":"Which heuristic is at work in each scenario? (1) People avoid mad cow beef but eat beef described as bovine spongiform encephalopathy - same risk. (2) People overestimate shark attack deaths and underestimate stroke deaths. (3) People prefer a candidate whose name they recognize over one they do not.","difficulty_level":"Advanced","visual_frame_description":"A courtroom-style grid stretches across the floor; each behavior card drops into either the PERSONALITY column glowing red or the SITUATION column glowing blue, and two policy maps unfurl beneath the winning explanations.","key_concepts":["Social cognition is the study of how people come to believe what they do and why these processes so frequently produce errors","Much of the time we use reason, facts, and critical analysis not to form our opinions but to confirm what we already see, feel, or believe","Most of us are cognitive misers who seek ways to conserve cognitive energy and simplify complexity, taking shortcuts and using rules of thumb","When we focus on surface similarities to make inferences, we are using the representativeness heuristic, so the expensive wine is assumed to be better","Consumer Reports found that young rats thrived on a diet of Lucky Charms, whereas a diet of 100 Percent Natural Granola actually stunted their growth","The availability heuristic predicts the likelihood of an event based on how easy it is to bring examples to mind","The overwhelming majority of people wrongly believe more Americans die from shark attacks and fires than from falling airplane parts and drowning","People who consume crime dramas and television news tend to vastly overestimate the crime rate, and the more they watch, the more fearful they become","Statements that rhyme or roll off the tongue are judged to be truer than statements that say the same thing less felicitously","The affect heuristic taps our feelings to shape evaluations, so a bad mood makes you likely to evaluate a job candidate more negatively","The mad cow study showed beef consumption fell when the alarming label was used but stayed the same when the technical disease names were used","We are more likely to rely on heuristics when we don't have time, are overloaded with information, the issue is unimportant, we lack knowledge, or emotions get in the way"]}]},{"id":"sa_ch02b","title":"Chapter 2: Social Cognition (Part 2)","pages":"31-74","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Social Psychology | Social Cognition | Schemas","topic":"Schemas and Priming: The Mental Frameworks That Shape Perception","visual_frame_description":"Participants are shown a list of words containing: bread, butter, milk, cheese, eggs. Later they are asked whether the word butter was on the list — they correctly say yes. But they also false alarm to the word loaf, which was not on the list. The schema food has been activated, and the mind fills in related information that may not have been present.","target_channel":"Schema Activation | Priming Effects","question":"Why do people confidently remember information that was never presented?","correct_cue":"Schemas are mental frameworks that organize knowledge about concepts, people, and situations. Once a schema is activated (through priming), it influences perception, memory, and judgment automatically. Priming: exposure to one stimulus activates related concepts in the associative network, making them more accessible. This happens without awareness. The food schema includes bread, milk, cheese, and loaf. When the schema is active, loaf feels like it was on the list because it is schema-consistent. This is why eyewitness testimony is unreliable — post-event information can prime schemas that alter what witnesses remember seeing.","distractors":["Participants are simply guessing and the false alarm rate is within normal memory error ranges","The word loaf is phonetically similar to loaf sounds like, causing a simple sound-based memory confusion","Priming only affects memory for words, not for complex social information like people and events"],"underlying_psychology":"Bartlett 1932 introduced schema theory. Anderson 1983 spreading activation model. In social psychology, schemas for people (person schemas), groups (stereotypes), events (scripts), and the self (self-schemas) all operate through the same mechanism. Priming studies show that even subliminal exposure to words related to elderly causes people to walk slower. The implications: schemas are not neutral — they systematically bias perception, memory, and behavior in ways that support existing beliefs and stereotypes.","player_mission":"Why do people confidently remember information that was never presented?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["Social cognition is the study of how people come to believe what they do and why these processes so frequently produce errors","Much of the time we use reason, facts, and critical analysis not to form our opinions but to confirm what we already see, feel, or believe","Most of us are cognitive misers who seek ways to conserve cognitive energy and simplify complexity, taking shortcuts and using rules of thumb","When we focus on surface similarities to make inferences, we are using the representativeness heuristic, so the expensive wine is assumed to be better","Consumer Reports found that young rats thrived on a diet of Lucky Charms, whereas a diet of 100 Percent Natural Granola actually stunted their growth","The availability heuristic predicts the likelihood of an event based on how easy it is to bring examples to mind","The overwhelming majority of people wrongly believe more Americans die from shark attacks and fires than from falling airplane parts and drowning","People who consume crime dramas and television news tend to vastly overestimate the crime rate, and the more they watch, the more fearful they become","Statements that rhyme or roll off the tongue are judged to be truer than statements that say the same thing less felicitously","The affect heuristic taps our feelings to shape evaluations, so a bad mood makes you likely to evaluate a job candidate more negatively","The mad cow study showed beef consumption fell when the alarming label was used but stayed the same when the technical disease names were used","We are more likely to rely on heuristics when we don't have time, are overloaded with information, the issue is unimportant, we lack knowledge, or emotions get in the way"]},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Social Psychology | Attribution Biases","topic":"The Self-Serving Bias and the Actor-Observer Difference","column_a_label":"Self-Serving Bias - Attribute successes to internal factors (I earned it, my skill). Attribute failures to external factors (the test was unfair, I was tired). Protects self-esteem.","column_b_label":"Actor-Observer Difference - As an actor, I attribute my behavior to the situation (I was late because traffic was bad). As an observer, I attribute others behavior to disposition (he is late because he is rude).","rows":[{"scenario":"A student gets an A on an exam and says I studied hard and I am smart","correct_column":"A","reason":"Self-serving bias — internal attribution for success protects self-esteem"},{"scenario":"The same student gets a D and says The test was unfair and the teacher graded too harshly","correct_column":"A","reason":"Self-serving bias — external attribution for failure preserves the self-concept that I am smart"},{"scenario":"You cut someone off in traffic because you are rushing to the hospital","correct_column":"B","reason":"Actor-observer — you attribute your own behavior to the situational urgency"},{"scenario":"Someone else cuts you off and you think what a reckless, selfish driver","correct_column":"B","reason":"Actor-observer — you attribute the other drivers behavior to their bad character, not to any situational pressure they might have"},{"scenario":"A politician wins an election and credits their policy vision and hard work","correct_column":"A","reason":"Self-serving bias — internal attribution for success. The loser blames the media bias and voter ignorance"}],"explanation":"Both biases are systematic errors in attribution that serve different functions. The self-serving bias protects and enhances self-esteem. The actor-observer difference reflects the different information available to each perspective: actors have rich contextual information about their own situation; observers see only behavior. Both biases have real-world consequences: self-serving bias causes conflict in relationships (each partner believes they contribute more), and the actor-observer difference causes us to underestimate situational pressures on others while overestimating them for ourselves.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Advanced","visual_frame_description":"A courtroom-style grid stretches across the floor; each behavior card drops into either the PERSONALITY column glowing red or the SITUATION column glowing blue, and two policy maps unfurl beneath the winning explanations.","key_concepts":["Social cognition is the study of how people come to believe what they do and why these processes so frequently produce errors","Much of the time we use reason, facts, and critical analysis not to form our opinions but to confirm what we already see, feel, or believe","Most of us are cognitive misers who seek ways to conserve cognitive energy and simplify complexity, taking shortcuts and using rules of thumb","When we focus on surface similarities to make inferences, we are using the representativeness heuristic, so the expensive wine is assumed to be better","Consumer Reports found that young rats thrived on a diet of Lucky Charms, whereas a diet of 100 Percent Natural Granola actually stunted their growth","The availability heuristic predicts the likelihood of an event based on how easy it is to bring examples to mind","The overwhelming majority of people wrongly believe more Americans die from shark attacks and fires than from falling airplane parts and drowning","People who consume crime dramas and television news tend to vastly overestimate the crime rate, and the more they watch, the more fearful they become","Statements that rhyme or roll off the tongue are judged to be truer than statements that say the same thing less felicitously","The affect heuristic taps our feelings to shape evaluations, so a bad mood makes you likely to evaluate a job candidate more negatively","The mad cow study showed beef consumption fell when the alarming label was used but stayed the same when the technical disease names were used","We are more likely to rely on heuristics when we don't have time, are overloaded with information, the issue is unimportant, we lack knowledge, or emotions get in the way"]}]},{"id":"sa_ch03a","title":"Chapter 3: Self-Justification - Cognitive Dissonance (Part 1)","pages":"75-126","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Social Psychology | Cognitive Dissonance","topic":"The 20 Dollar Study: Insufficient Justification and Attitude Change","visual_frame_description":"Participants perform an incredibly boring task - turning pegs for an hour. They are then asked to tell the next participant the task was interesting. Some are paid $1, others $20. The $1 liars later rate the task as genuinely enjoyable. The $20 liars rate it as boring. The smaller bribe produced more attitude change.","target_channel":"Insufficient Justification | Dissonance-Driven Attitude Shift","question":"Why did participants paid $1 convince themselves a boring task was enjoyable, while those paid $20 honestly rated it as boring?","correct_cue":"Cognitive dissonance: telling a lie with insufficient external justification creates psychological discomfort. The $1 liars could not justify the lie externally (one dollar is trivial), so they created internal justification by changing their belief - I did not lie, the task actually was interesting. The $20 liars had sufficient external justification (it was worth lying for twenty dollars), so no attitude change was needed.","distractors":["The $20 participants felt guilty about being overpaid and rated the task honestly to restore fairness","The $1 participants were less intelligent and genuinely could not distinguish boring from interesting tasks","The $20 participants suspected the experimenter was testing their honesty and answered truthfully"],"underlying_psychology":"Festinger and Carlsmith 1959 classic study demonstrates induced compliance: when people perform a behavior that contradicts their attitude with insufficient external justification, they change their attitude to match the behavior. The key insight: the less external justification, the more internal attitude change. Large rewards or punishments provide external justification and prevent internalization. Small incentives produce lasting attitude change because the person must justify the behavior internally. This principle explains why harsh punishments fail long-term (children obey to avoid punishment, not because they believe) while minimal sufficient pressure creates genuine value internalization.","player_mission":"Why did participants paid $1 convince themselves a boring task was enjoyable, while those paid $20 honestly rated it as boring?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["People are motivated to justify their own actions, beliefs, and feelings; that is the essence of self-justification","Sam, hypnotized into buying bourbon and wearing rain gear on a sunny day, invented sensible-sounding reasons because it was difficult for him to accept that he had behaved nonsensically","Leon Festinger described cognitive dissonance as a state of tension that occurs when an individual simultaneously holds two cognitions that are psychologically inconsistent","Two cognitions are dissonant if the opposite of one follows from the other, and because the experience is unpleasant, people are motivated to reduce it","We reduce dissonance by changing one or both cognitions to make them more compatible or by adding cognitions that help bridge the gap","In the 1964 survey after the Surgeon General's report, 40 percent of heavy smokers doubted a link between smoking and cancer had been proven, while only 10 percent of nonsmokers doubted it","Smokers who smoked one to two packs a day reduced dissonance by convincing themselves that they were 'moderate' smokers","Heavy smokers who relapsed after a smoking cessation clinic reduced dissonance by convincing themselves that smoking isn't as bad as they originally thought","At the 1994 congressional hearings, the top executives of every major tobacco company admitted they were smokers and argued cigarettes are no more harmful than eating Twinkies","James J. Morgan of Philip Morris said cigarettes are not pharmacologically addictive, comparing his habit to liking gummy bears","People like Morgan begin to believe their own lies, in an effort to deceive themselves","The process of reducing dissonance is largely unconscious; people don't sit down and say, 'I guess I will reduce some dissonance now'"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Integrated Influence Analysis | Dissonance","topic":"Effort Justification: The Severe Initiation Effect","stage_1_observation":"Aronson and Mills 1959 study: female volunteers underwent a screening test to join a discussion group about sex. One group read sex-related words aloud (embarrassing). A second group read obscene words and graphic passages (severe initiation). A third group had no initiation. All then listened to the same incredibly boring discussion. Those who underwent the severe initiation rated the discussion as far more interesting than the other groups.","stage_1_question":"Why did women who endured the most embarrassing initiation rate the same boring discussion as most interesting?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Effort justification: when people voluntarily undergo unpleasant effort to join a group, they experience dissonance. The cognition I suffered for this group conflicts with the group is boring. To resolve dissonance, they change their evaluation of the group - if I suffered for it, it must have been worth it. The greater the effort, the greater the justification needed.","stage_2_intervention":"You are a new employee at a company with hazing rituals. You have endured a humiliating initiation week. Now you realize the job is mediocre and the culture is toxic. Your brain wants to justify your suffering by convincing yourself the company is great. How do you resist this automatic self-justification?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Use the Would I choose this again test. Ask yourself: Knowing what I now know about the job and the initiation, if I could go back, would I still choose to join? If the answer is no, your current desire to defend the company is effort justification, not genuine satisfaction. Separate your evaluation of the company from your need to justify your past suffering. The decision to leave or change is a separate choice from the past choice to endure the initiation.","boss_battle_explanation":"Layer 1 - Dissonance: I voluntarily suffered for this goal/group. Layer 2 - Reality: The goal/group is disappointing. Layer 3 - Dissonance Resolution: Change perception of the goal/group to match the effort. I suffered for it, so it must be valuable. This explains fraternity hazing brutal initiations, cult members increased commitment after public sacrifice, and why expensive purchases are rated as more satisfying than cheap ones. The defense: separate past investment from present evaluation.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A vaulted initiation hall where three gated corridors glow, NO ORDEAL, MILD WORDS, and SEVERE ORDEAL, while a one-way mirror shows the dull group discussion that every new member must rate.","key_concepts":["People are motivated to justify their own actions, beliefs, and feelings; that is the essence of self-justification","Sam, hypnotized into buying bourbon and wearing rain gear on a sunny day, invented sensible-sounding reasons because it was difficult for him to accept that he had behaved nonsensically","Leon Festinger described cognitive dissonance as a state of tension that occurs when an individual simultaneously holds two cognitions that are psychologically inconsistent","Two cognitions are dissonant if the opposite of one follows from the other, and because the experience is unpleasant, people are motivated to reduce it","We reduce dissonance by changing one or both cognitions to make them more compatible or by adding cognitions that help bridge the gap","In the 1964 survey after the Surgeon General's report, 40 percent of heavy smokers doubted a link between smoking and cancer had been proven, while only 10 percent of nonsmokers doubted it","Smokers who smoked one to two packs a day reduced dissonance by convincing themselves that they were 'moderate' smokers","Heavy smokers who relapsed after a smoking cessation clinic reduced dissonance by convincing themselves that smoking isn't as bad as they originally thought","At the 1994 congressional hearings, the top executives of every major tobacco company admitted they were smokers and argued cigarettes are no more harmful than eating Twinkies","James J. Morgan of Philip Morris said cigarettes are not pharmacologically addictive, comparing his habit to liking gummy bears","People like Morgan begin to believe their own lies, in an effort to deceive themselves","The process of reducing dissonance is largely unconscious; people don't sit down and say, 'I guess I will reduce some dissonance now'"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Social Psychology | Victim Blaming | Dissonance","topic":"The Insult Study: Why We Blame Victims","subject_baseline":"Participants were manipulated into insulting another person. They were asked to tell a waiting participant that he was shallow, untrustworthy, and boring. After delivering the insult, participants were surveyed about their true opinion of the victim. Result: participants convinced themselves the victim deserved the insult.","interrogation_transcript":"Participants were told the study was about how people communicate negative feedback. After delivering prepared insulting statements, they rated the victim negatively on personality traits - despite having zero information about the victim before the study. The victims were innocent strangers.","observed_nonverbal_log":["Before insulting: neutral toward the stranger","After insulting: negative attitude toward the stranger","The insult caused dissonance: I am a good person but I harmed an innocent person","Dissonance resolution: the victim must have deserved it - he is a bad person"],"question":"Why did participants who were forced to insult a stranger subsequently convince themselves the victim was a bad person who deserved the treatment?","correct_diagnosis":"Cognitive dissonance and the just-world hypothesis combined. The cognition I am a good person conflicts with I harmed an innocent person. To resolve dissonance, the participant changes the belief about the victim from innocent to deserving. The just-world hypothesis makes this easier - we want to believe people get what they deserve.","distractors":["Accurate perception - participants were actually insightful about the victim negative traits after a brief interaction","Emotional contagion - the act of insulting made participants angry, and they projected that anger onto the victim","Social proof - hearing themselves say negative things convinced them the victim must be bad"],"diagnostic_breakdown":"Stage 1 - Harm: Participant insults innocent person. Stage 2 - Dissonance: I am good but I harmed someone. Stage 3 - Resolution Option A: Accept I am capable of harming innocents (painful, threatens self-concept). Stage 4 - Resolution Option B: The victim deserved it (preserves self-concept as good person). Most people choose Option B automatically. This is the psychological root of victim blaming: we convince ourselves that victims of assault brought it on themselves, that poor people deserve poverty, that those who suffer are somehow at fault. The belief protects us from the disturbing reality that suffering can happen to innocent people - including ourselves.","player_mission":"Why did participants who were forced to insult a stranger subsequently convince themselves the victim was a bad person who deserved the treatment?","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A betting-window counter at a racetrack where bettors stream past holding tickets; the audit ledger lights up each entry with IRREVOCABLE stamps and a comparison meter showing chosen versus rejected appeal.","key_concepts":["People are motivated to justify their own actions, beliefs, and feelings; that is the essence of self-justification","Sam, hypnotized into buying bourbon and wearing rain gear on a sunny day, invented sensible-sounding reasons because it was difficult for him to accept that he had behaved nonsensically","Leon Festinger described cognitive dissonance as a state of tension that occurs when an individual simultaneously holds two cognitions that are psychologically inconsistent","Two cognitions are dissonant if the opposite of one follows from the other, and because the experience is unpleasant, people are motivated to reduce it","We reduce dissonance by changing one or both cognitions to make them more compatible or by adding cognitions that help bridge the gap","In the 1964 survey after the Surgeon General's report, 40 percent of heavy smokers doubted a link between smoking and cancer had been proven, while only 10 percent of nonsmokers doubted it","Smokers who smoked one to two packs a day reduced dissonance by convincing themselves that they were 'moderate' smokers","Heavy smokers who relapsed after a smoking cessation clinic reduced dissonance by convincing themselves that smoking isn't as bad as they originally thought","At the 1994 congressional hearings, the top executives of every major tobacco company admitted they were smokers and argued cigarettes are no more harmful than eating Twinkies","James J. Morgan of Philip Morris said cigarettes are not pharmacologically addictive, comparing his habit to liking gummy bears","People like Morgan begin to believe their own lies, in an effort to deceive themselves","The process of reducing dissonance is largely unconscious; people don't sit down and say, 'I guess I will reduce some dissonance now'"]}]},{"id":"sa_ch03b","title":"Chapter 3: Self-Justification - Cognitive Dissonance (Part 2)","pages":"75-126","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Social Psychology | Dissonance | Everyday Life","topic":"The Ben Franklin Effect: Doing a Favor Makes You Like Someone More","visual_frame_description":"Benjamin Franklin, through a mutual acquaintance, asked a political rival who disliked him to borrow a rare book from his library. The rival lent the book. Franklin returned it with a polite thank-you note. After this interaction, the rival became friendly and cooperative. Franklin observed: He that has once done you a kindness will be more ready to do you another than he whom you yourself have obliged.","target_channel":"Dissonance in Reverse | Favor-Liking Connection","question":"Why does doing a favor for someone increase your liking for them, rather than the other way around?","correct_cue":"Cognitive dissonance: the thought I did a favor for that person conflicts with I do not like that person. To resolve the dissonance, the mind changes its evaluation: I must like him more than I thought, since I did him a favor. This is the reverse of reciprocity — it is not that they owe you, but that you justify your own behavior by changing your attitude. The Ben Franklin effect works best when the favor is small and voluntary. Large or coerced favors trigger reciprocity, not dissonance reduction. The practical implication: if you want to build rapport with someone, ask them for a small favor.","distractors":["The favor creates a sense of obligation in the recipient, who reciprocates by being friendlier","Franklin was an exceptionally charming person who would have won over the rival regardless of the favor","The favor provided an opportunity for interaction that revealed common interests and built natural rapport"],"underlying_psychology":"Jecker and Landy 1969 experimentally confirmed the Ben Franklin effect. Participants who were asked by the experimenter to return the money (a favor) rated the experimenter more favorably than participants who were paid. Doing a favor creates dissonance when the person is not already liked. The resolution: increase liking for the person who received the favor. The effect is limited to small, voluntary favors. Large favors create reactance (why is this person doing so much for me — what do they want?) and paid favors are just transactions.","player_mission":"Why does doing a favor for someone increase your liking for them, rather than the other way around?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["People are motivated to justify their own actions, beliefs, and feelings; that is the essence of self-justification","Sam, hypnotized into buying bourbon and wearing rain gear on a sunny day, invented sensible-sounding reasons because it was difficult for him to accept that he had behaved nonsensically","Leon Festinger described cognitive dissonance as a state of tension that occurs when an individual simultaneously holds two cognitions that are psychologically inconsistent","Two cognitions are dissonant if the opposite of one follows from the other, and because the experience is unpleasant, people are motivated to reduce it","We reduce dissonance by changing one or both cognitions to make them more compatible or by adding cognitions that help bridge the gap","In the 1964 survey after the Surgeon General's report, 40 percent of heavy smokers doubted a link between smoking and cancer had been proven, while only 10 percent of nonsmokers doubted it","Smokers who smoked one to two packs a day reduced dissonance by convincing themselves that they were 'moderate' smokers","Heavy smokers who relapsed after a smoking cessation clinic reduced dissonance by convincing themselves that smoking isn't as bad as they originally thought","At the 1994 congressional hearings, the top executives of every major tobacco company admitted they were smokers and argued cigarettes are no more harmful than eating Twinkies","James J. Morgan of Philip Morris said cigarettes are not pharmacologically addictive, comparing his habit to liking gummy bears","People like Morgan begin to believe their own lies, in an effort to deceive themselves","The process of reducing dissonance is largely unconscious; people don't sit down and say, 'I guess I will reduce some dissonance now'"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Social Psychology | Dissonance | Behavior Change","topic":"The Hypocrisy Paradigm: Aronson's Most Powerful Dissonance Intervention","stage_1_observation":"Aronson and colleagues 1991: College students advocated for condom use (made a video or speech). Then they were made mindful of their own failures to use condoms (listed times they had not used one). This hypocrisy condition produced dramatically more condom purchasing and reported use than either condition alone. Simply advocating (consistency condition) or simply being made aware of failures (mindfulness condition) had minimal effects.","stage_1_question":"Why did combining advocacy with mindfulness of failures produce so much more behavior change than either alone?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The hypocrisy paradigm creates the strongest possible cognitive dissonance by combining two elements: (1) a public commitment to a position (I advocate safe sex — I am a responsible person) and (2) undeniable evidence of failure to live up to that commitment (I have not been using condoms). The dissonance between I am responsible and I act irresponsibly is intolerable. Unlike the $1/$20 study where participants changed their attitude, hypocrisy forces behavior change because the attitude is already publicly committed to and cannot be easily changed. The only way to reduce dissonance is to change behavior to match the advocacy.","stage_2_intervention":"You run a workplace safety program. Posters and training sessions have not reduced accident rates. Workers know safety is important (attitude) but do not follow procedures (behavior). Using the hypocrisy paradigm, design an intervention.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Two-step intervention: (1) Advocacy — have each worker make a public video or written commitment to safety procedures, explaining why safety matters. Post these publicly. (2) Mindfulness — in a follow-up session, have each worker privately list every time this week they did NOT follow the safety procedure. Crucially, the list is for the workers own reflection, not for public shaming. The dissonance between their public safety advocacy and their private safety failures will drive genuine behavior change. Do not mix the two — advocacy first, then separate private mindfulness. The hypocrisy paradigm works because it preserves face while creating internal pressure to change.","boss_battle_explanation":"Layer 1 - Dissonance: Conflict between self-concept (I am responsible) and behavior (I act irresponsibly). Layer 2 - Hypocrisy Lock: Advocacy makes the attitude public and hard to change. Layer 3 - Mindfulness Trigger: Awareness of failure blocks the easy out of denying the behavior. Layer 4 - Resolution: Behavior must change to match the advocacy. This is far more powerful than standard persuasion because it does not tell people what to do — it makes them discover their own inconsistency. The discovery is self-motivating.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A vaulted initiation hall where three gated corridors glow, NO ORDEAL, MILD WORDS, and SEVERE ORDEAL, while a one-way mirror shows the dull group discussion that every new member must rate.","key_concepts":["People are motivated to justify their own actions, beliefs, and feelings; that is the essence of self-justification","Sam, hypnotized into buying bourbon and wearing rain gear on a sunny day, invented sensible-sounding reasons because it was difficult for him to accept that he had behaved nonsensically","Leon Festinger described cognitive dissonance as a state of tension that occurs when an individual simultaneously holds two cognitions that are psychologically inconsistent","Two cognitions are dissonant if the opposite of one follows from the other, and because the experience is unpleasant, people are motivated to reduce it","We reduce dissonance by changing one or both cognitions to make them more compatible or by adding cognitions that help bridge the gap","In the 1964 survey after the Surgeon General's report, 40 percent of heavy smokers doubted a link between smoking and cancer had been proven, while only 10 percent of nonsmokers doubted it","Smokers who smoked one to two packs a day reduced dissonance by convincing themselves that they were 'moderate' smokers","Heavy smokers who relapsed after a smoking cessation clinic reduced dissonance by convincing themselves that smoking isn't as bad as they originally thought","At the 1994 congressional hearings, the top executives of every major tobacco company admitted they were smokers and argued cigarettes are no more harmful than eating Twinkies","James J. Morgan of Philip Morris said cigarettes are not pharmacologically addictive, comparing his habit to liking gummy bears","People like Morgan begin to believe their own lies, in an effort to deceive themselves","The process of reducing dissonance is largely unconscious; people don't sit down and say, 'I guess I will reduce some dissonance now'"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Social Psychology | Dissonance | Internalization","topic":"The Forbidden-Toy Paradigm: Why Mild Threats Produce Lasting Attitude Change","subject_baseline":"Aronson and Carlsmith 1963: Children were told not to play with an attractive toy. One group received a mild threat: I would not want you to play with it. Another group received a severe threat: If you play with it, I will be very angry and will take your toys away. Both groups avoided the toy. Later, when the threat was removed, the mild-threat children still avoided the toy and rated it less attractive. The severe-threat children immediately played with it.","interrogation_transcript":"The mild-threat children experienced dissonance: I am a good child who obeys, but I chose not to play with that toy even though I wanted to. The justification for not playing was weak (just a mild request), so they created internal justification: I did not play with it because I do not actually like it. They genuinely changed their attitude toward the toy. The severe-threat children had an external justification (I will be punished), so no dissonance occurred. They did not need to change their attitude — they simply avoided punishment. When the threat was removed, the original desire returned.","observed_nonverbal_log":["Mild threat: insufficient external justification triggers internal attitude change","Severe threat: sufficient external justification prevents attitude change","The attitude change from mild threat persists after the threat is removed","Severe threat produces compliance only under surveillance"],"question":"Why does a mild threat produce more lasting behavior change than a severe threat?","correct_diagnosis":"The insufficient justification principle. The milder the external pressure, the more internal justification is required. This is called the forbidden-toy paradigm and it has profound implications for parenting, education, and social change. Harsh punishments produce compliance only while the punisher is present. Minimal sufficient pressure produces genuine internalization that persists without surveillance. Children who are harshly punished for lying lie less when punishment is likely — but they lie MORE when they think they can get away with it. Children who are given reasons why lying is wrong (minimal pressure) internalize honesty as a value.","distractors":["Children simply forgot about the toy in the mild-threat condition by the time the threat was removed","Mild threats create fear that generalizes to the toy, while severe threats are specific to the punisher","The children in the severe condition played with the toy to rebel against the adult who threatened them"],"diagnostic_breakdown":"Stage 1 - Restriction: Child is told not to play with a desired toy. Stage 2 - Compliance: Child obeys the restriction. Stage 3 - Dissonance: I am a good child, but I want a toy that I am not playing with, and the reason I am not playing with it is weak (mild threat only). Stage 4 - Resolution: I do not actually want the toy — it is not that attractive. Stage 5 - Internalization: The attitude persists beyond the restriction. The lesson for parents and teachers: use the minimal force necessary to achieve compliance. The less external pressure, the more the child internalizes the value.","player_mission":"Why does a mild threat produce more lasting behavior change than a severe threat?","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A betting-window counter at a racetrack where bettors stream past holding tickets; the audit ledger lights up each entry with IRREVOCABLE stamps and a comparison meter showing chosen versus rejected appeal.","key_concepts":["People are motivated to justify their own actions, beliefs, and feelings; that is the essence of self-justification","Sam, hypnotized into buying bourbon and wearing rain gear on a sunny day, invented sensible-sounding reasons because it was difficult for him to accept that he had behaved nonsensically","Leon Festinger described cognitive dissonance as a state of tension that occurs when an individual simultaneously holds two cognitions that are psychologically inconsistent","Two cognitions are dissonant if the opposite of one follows from the other, and because the experience is unpleasant, people are motivated to reduce it","We reduce dissonance by changing one or both cognitions to make them more compatible or by adding cognitions that help bridge the gap","In the 1964 survey after the Surgeon General's report, 40 percent of heavy smokers doubted a link between smoking and cancer had been proven, while only 10 percent of nonsmokers doubted it","Smokers who smoked one to two packs a day reduced dissonance by convincing themselves that they were 'moderate' smokers","Heavy smokers who relapsed after a smoking cessation clinic reduced dissonance by convincing themselves that smoking isn't as bad as they originally thought","At the 1994 congressional hearings, the top executives of every major tobacco company admitted they were smokers and argued cigarettes are no more harmful than eating Twinkies","James J. Morgan of Philip Morris said cigarettes are not pharmacologically addictive, comparing his habit to liking gummy bears","People like Morgan begin to believe their own lies, in an effort to deceive themselves","The process of reducing dissonance is largely unconscious; people don't sit down and say, 'I guess I will reduce some dissonance now'"]}]},{"id":"sa_ch04a","title":"Chapter 4: Conformity (Part 1)","pages":"127-166","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Social Psychology | Conformity Types","topic":"Three Shades of Conformity: Compliance, Identification, Internalization","column_a_label":"Compliance - Public conformity, private disagreement. Motivated by reward/punishment.","column_b_label":"Identification - Conform to maintain relationship with admired person/group.","column_c_label":"Internalization - Genuine belief change. Accept influence because it matches personal values.","rows":[{"scenario":"You wear a tie to a casual office because the boss enforces dress code, but you hate ties","correct_column":"A","reason":"Public behavior without private belief change, solely for reward avoidance"},{"scenario":"A teenager adopts the same band preferences and slang as their popular friend group","correct_column":"B","reason":"Conformity driven by desire for belonging and relationship maintenance"},{"scenario":"After years in a peace organization, you genuinely believe nonviolence is the most effective political strategy","correct_column":"C","reason":"The belief has become part of your personal value system, independent of external pressure"},{"scenario":"An employee attends optional diversity training but rolls their eyes in private","correct_column":"A","reason":"Surface compliance without acceptance, will reverse when training ends"},{"scenario":"A convert to a religion genuinely embraces its teachings as personally meaningful truth","correct_column":"C","reason":"The belief is now internally owned and integrated into the self"}],"explanation":"Kelman 1961 distinguished three levels of social influence. Compliance is the shallowest, least lasting, and depends on surveillance. Identification is deeper, depends on relationship maintenance, and ends if the relationship ends. Internalization is the deepest, most permanent, and becomes part of the self. Attempts at social change that only produce compliance will fail when surveillance ends.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Advanced","visual_frame_description":"A three-tiered temple grid rises in the room, its tiers labeled COMPLIANCE, IDENTIFICATION, and INTERNALIZATION; each scenario card drops onto a tier and its permanence meter spins up or down.","key_concepts":["Conformity is a change in a person's behavior or opinions as a result of real or imagined pressure from another person or group of people","Asch's line judgment task was so easy that when people judged the lines alone, they made almost no errors","When faced with their fellow students agreeing on incorrect responses, approximately three-quarters of Asch's participants conformed at least once","Over the entire spectrum of judgments, an average of 35 percent of responses conformed to the incorrect judgments","Asch firmly believed there would be little, if any, yielding to group pressure, but his prediction was wrong","Anthony Pratkanis repeated the experiment on national television with college students who considered themselves nonconformists, and the results were almost identical to Asch's","Bond and Smith's analysis of 133 experiments in 17 countries found conformity is more prevalent in collectivist societies than in individualistic ones","Berns's fMRI replication showed participants who resisted group pressure displayed heightened activity in the amygdala, a region associated with pain, fear, and emotional discomfort","People conform to group pressure about as often when the unanimous group consists of only 3 other people as when it consists of 16","When unanimity is broken, so is the pressure to conform; even a single ally who sees it your way releases you from that pressure","Even a dissenter who gives a different wrong answer sharply reduces conformity, and the participant is likely to give the correct response","It only takes one dissenter to seriously diminish the power of the group to induce conformity"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Social Psychology | Conformity Pressure","topic":"The Asch Line Paradigm: Resisting Unanimous Majority Pressure","stage_1_observation":"Asch 1956: Participants in groups of 7-9 judged which of three comparison lines matched a standard line. The answer was obvious. But all confederates gave the same wrong answer on critical trials. The real participant answered last. Under unanimous wrong majority pressure, 76% of participants conformed at least once. Average conformity rate: 37%.","stage_1_question":"Why did participants deny their own clear perception and agree with the wrong majority?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Two forces: normative influence (fear of being the lone dissenter, desire to avoid social ridicule) and informational influence (assuming the majority might be right despite clear evidence). Most participants privately knew the correct answer but publicly conformed to avoid standing out.","stage_2_intervention":"You are in a jury deliberation. All 11 other jurors want to convict based on what you see as weak circumstantial evidence. You believe the defendant is innocent. The foreperson asks each juror to state their verdict publicly. You are the last to speak. Everyone before you says guilty. The pressure to conform is immense. How do you resist?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Deploy three strategies simultaneously: (1) Find one ally - Asch showed that if even one person breaks unanimity, conformity drops sharply. Ask a clarifying question that signals doubt without directly opposing anyone yet. (2) Use private written ballots instead of public declarations - removes the surveillance that fuels compliance. (3) Reframe the situation from I am the odd one out to I am fulfilling a different role: the careful guardian protecting the standard of reasonable doubt. This reframing reduces normative pressure by giving your dissent purpose.","boss_battle_explanation":"Layer 1 - Unanimous Majority creates extreme conformity pressure. Layer 2 - Breaking Unanimity with even one ally dramatically reduces conformity (from 37% to 6%). Layer 3 - Privacy removes the social surveillance that drives compliance. Layer 4 - Self-Reframing turns dissent from social deviance into principled duty. Asch found that participants who conformed reported severe distress - they were not mindless sheep but people caught between trusting their own eyes and trusting the group. The tragedy is that many knew the group was wrong but still conformed.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A laboratory table bears the 30-switch Shock Generator, its dial glowing from 'Slight Shock' to 'Danger: Severe Shock' to 'XXX'; a technician in a lab coat stands behind you repeating, 'The experiment requires that you continue.'","key_concepts":["Conformity is a change in a person's behavior or opinions as a result of real or imagined pressure from another person or group of people","Asch's line judgment task was so easy that when people judged the lines alone, they made almost no errors","When faced with their fellow students agreeing on incorrect responses, approximately three-quarters of Asch's participants conformed at least once","Over the entire spectrum of judgments, an average of 35 percent of responses conformed to the incorrect judgments","Asch firmly believed there would be little, if any, yielding to group pressure, but his prediction was wrong","Anthony Pratkanis repeated the experiment on national television with college students who considered themselves nonconformists, and the results were almost identical to Asch's","Bond and Smith's analysis of 133 experiments in 17 countries found conformity is more prevalent in collectivist societies than in individualistic ones","Berns's fMRI replication showed participants who resisted group pressure displayed heightened activity in the amygdala, a region associated with pain, fear, and emotional discomfort","People conform to group pressure about as often when the unanimous group consists of only 3 other people as when it consists of 16","When unanimity is broken, so is the pressure to conform; even a single ally who sees it your way releases you from that pressure","Even a dissenter who gives a different wrong answer sharply reduces conformity, and the participant is likely to give the correct response","It only takes one dissenter to seriously diminish the power of the group to induce conformity"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Social Psychology | Obedience to Authority","topic":"Milgram Obedience: The Agentic State and Destructive Obedience","subject_baseline":"Yale University, early 1960s. A participant is instructed to administer increasingly severe electric shocks to a learner (confederate) for wrong answers. The learner screams, pounds on the wall, then falls silent. The experimenter prods: Please continue. The experiment requires that you continue. You have no choice. You must go on.","interrogation_transcript":"65% of participants administered the maximum 450-volt shock, despite hearing agonized screams from the next room. Participants sweated, trembled, and laughed nervously. Many protested verbally. Few defied. When asked why they continued, participants said they felt responsible to the experimenter, not for the learner. They shifted responsibility upward to authority.","observed_nonverbal_log":["Participants showed extreme distress - sweating, trembling, nervous laughter","Many protested verbally but still pressed the shock button","No one who stopped was punished - resistance was always available","Participants used the experimenter presence as moral cover for their actions"],"question":"Why did 65% of normal, ethical participants continue delivering what they believed were dangerous shocks to an innocent victim?","correct_diagnosis":"The agentic state: Milgram proposed that when people enter an authority system, they shift from autonomous to agentic mode. They become instruments of the authority will, feeling responsible to the authority rather than for the consequences. Combined with incremental commitment (shocks escalate 15V at a time - each step is only a little worse than the last) and the binding factors (politeness, keeping promises, not ruining the experiment), the situation overwhelmed participants moral boundaries.","distractors":["Participants were selected for sadistic personality traits and enjoyed administering shocks","Participants did not believe the learner was actually being shocked despite the screams","The experimenter threatened to physically restrain participants who wanted to leave"],"diagnostic_breakdown":"Stage 1 - Entry: Participant volunteers for a learning study. Stage 2 - Incremental Commitment: Each shock is 15V more. The jump from 15 to 30 is trivial; the jump from 0 to 450 is impossible. Stage 3 - Agentic Shift: I am not responsible, the experimenter is. Stage 4 - Binding Factors: Politeness, contractual obligation, embarrassment about stopping. Stage 5 - Outcome: Ordinary people commit destructive acts. The real lesson: evil is not carried by monsters but by ordinary people following orders in a situation that strips them of felt responsibility.","player_mission":"Why did 65% of normal, ethical participants continue delivering what they believed were dangerous shocks to an innocent victim?","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A split-screen field logbook: on one side a shower room with a soaping silhouette, on the other a parking lot where a model picks up a fast-food bag while a ticker counts the fliers dropped on the ground.","key_concepts":["Conformity is a change in a person's behavior or opinions as a result of real or imagined pressure from another person or group of people","Asch's line judgment task was so easy that when people judged the lines alone, they made almost no errors","When faced with their fellow students agreeing on incorrect responses, approximately three-quarters of Asch's participants conformed at least once","Over the entire spectrum of judgments, an average of 35 percent of responses conformed to the incorrect judgments","Asch firmly believed there would be little, if any, yielding to group pressure, but his prediction was wrong","Anthony Pratkanis repeated the experiment on national television with college students who considered themselves nonconformists, and the results were almost identical to Asch's","Bond and Smith's analysis of 133 experiments in 17 countries found conformity is more prevalent in collectivist societies than in individualistic ones","Berns's fMRI replication showed participants who resisted group pressure displayed heightened activity in the amygdala, a region associated with pain, fear, and emotional discomfort","People conform to group pressure about as often when the unanimous group consists of only 3 other people as when it consists of 16","When unanimity is broken, so is the pressure to conform; even a single ally who sees it your way releases you from that pressure","Even a dissenter who gives a different wrong answer sharply reduces conformity, and the participant is likely to give the correct response","It only takes one dissenter to seriously diminish the power of the group to induce conformity"]},{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Social Psychology | Social Influence | Cialdini","topic":"Sherif Autokinetic Effect: Informational Influence Creates Social Norms","visual_frame_description":"Participants sit in a dark room staring at a single point of light. The light appears to move (autokinetic effect - the eye and brain create movement where there is none). Participants estimate how far the light moves. Alone, estimates vary wildly. In groups, estimates converge to a shared norm - and that norm persists even when participants are later tested alone.","target_channel":"Informational Social Influence | Norm Formation","question":"Why did individual estimates of a subjective experience converge so rapidly and persist after the group dissolved?","correct_cue":"Informational social influence: when the situation is ambiguous and objective reality is unavailable we turn to others for information about what is correct. The light seems to move, so far, but I am not sure how far - others estimates provide information about what the right answer is. This creates a group norm that becomes internalized and persists beyond the group presence.","distractors":["Participants were simply complying to avoid looking incompetent in front of others","The autokinetic effect is highly suggestible and participants estimates naturally drift toward the mean","Participants could not see the light clearly and guessed randomly"],"underlying_psychology":"Sherif 1936 study distinguishes informational influence (using others as evidence about reality) from normative influence (conforming to avoid rejection). Informational influence leads to genuine internalization. This explains why cultural norms are so durable - they are not just followed to avoid punishment but are believed to be correct. New members internalize the groups definition of reality.","player_mission":"Why did individual estimates of a subjective experience converge so rapidly and persist after the group dissolved?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["Conformity is a change in a person's behavior or opinions as a result of real or imagined pressure from another person or group of people","Asch's line judgment task was so easy that when people judged the lines alone, they made almost no errors","When faced with their fellow students agreeing on incorrect responses, approximately three-quarters of Asch's participants conformed at least once","Over the entire spectrum of judgments, an average of 35 percent of responses conformed to the incorrect judgments","Asch firmly believed there would be little, if any, yielding to group pressure, but his prediction was wrong","Anthony Pratkanis repeated the experiment on national television with college students who considered themselves nonconformists, and the results were almost identical to Asch's","Bond and Smith's analysis of 133 experiments in 17 countries found conformity is more prevalent in collectivist societies than in individualistic ones","Berns's fMRI replication showed participants who resisted group pressure displayed heightened activity in the amygdala, a region associated with pain, fear, and emotional discomfort","People conform to group pressure about as often when the unanimous group consists of only 3 other people as when it consists of 16","When unanimity is broken, so is the pressure to conform; even a single ally who sees it your way releases you from that pressure","Even a dissenter who gives a different wrong answer sharply reduces conformity, and the participant is likely to give the correct response","It only takes one dissenter to seriously diminish the power of the group to induce conformity"]}]},{"id":"sa_ch04b","title":"Chapter 4: Conformity (Part 2)","pages":"127-166","assets":[{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Social Psychology | Group Dynamics | Groupthink","topic":"Groupthink: When the Drive for Consensus Overrides Critical Thinking","subject_baseline":"The Bay of Pigs invasion, 1961. President Kennedy and his advisors — some of the smartest people in America — approved a plan to invade Cuba with 1,400 exiles. The plan had so many flaws that it failed within 24 hours. The CIA had assessed it as having only a 30% chance of success. The group did not seriously question the plan because they were more focused on maintaining consensus and group harmony than on evaluating the plan critically.","interrogation_transcript":"Janis 1972 analyzed key foreign policy disasters and identified Groupthink: a mode of thinking in which the desire for unanimous agreement overrides realistic appraisal of alternatives. Symptoms include: (1) illusion of invulnerability (we cannot fail), (2) collective rationalization (we dismiss warnings), (3) belief in inherent group morality (we are good, they are evil), (4) stereotypes of outgroups, (5) direct pressure on dissenters, (6) self-censorship, (7) illusion of unanimity, and (8) self-appointed mindguards who protect the group from dissenting information.","observed_nonverbal_log":["Dissenters were pressured to agree with the majority","Members self-censored their doubts","The group felt invulnerable to failure","Warnings were collectively rationalized away"],"question":"How did extremely intelligent, experienced advisors approve a plan that was almost certain to fail?","correct_diagnosis":"Groupthink occurs when the conformity pressure within a cohesive group overrides realistic appraisal of alternatives. The group members are loyal, committed, and want to be good team players. The problem is that being a good team player means not challenging the consensus. The rider rationalizes: everyone else agrees, so I must be wrong to have doubts. The elephant wants harmony and belonging. Janis identified the cure: (1) leader should not state their position first, (2) assign a devil advocate, (3) bring in outside experts to challenge the group, (4) have a second-chance meeting after the initial decision.","distractors":["The plan was actually good but failed due to bad luck and unforeseeable circumstances","CIA intelligence was deliberately wrong, so even the best analysis would have produced the same decision","The advisors were not actually intelligent — they were political appointees with no relevant expertise"],"diagnostic_breakdown":"Stage 1 - Cohesive Group: Members like each other and want to get along. Stage 2 - Directive Leadership: Leader signals preference. Stage 3 - Isolation: Group cuts off outside information. Stage 4 - Pressure: Dissenters are pressured or marginalized. Stage 5 - Self-Censorship: Members stop raising objections. Stage 6 - Illusion of Unanimity: Silence is taken as agreement. Stage 7 - Flawed Decision: The plan is based on incomplete analysis and unchallenged assumptions. The prevention: institutionalize dissent. Make devil advocacy a required role, not a tolerated exception.","player_mission":"How did extremely intelligent, experienced advisors approve a plan that was almost certain to fail?","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A split-screen field logbook: on one side a shower room with a soaping silhouette, on the other a parking lot where a model picks up a fast-food bag while a ticker counts the fliers dropped on the ground.","key_concepts":["Conformity is a change in a person's behavior or opinions as a result of real or imagined pressure from another person or group of people","Asch's line judgment task was so easy that when people judged the lines alone, they made almost no errors","When faced with their fellow students agreeing on incorrect responses, approximately three-quarters of Asch's participants conformed at least once","Over the entire spectrum of judgments, an average of 35 percent of responses conformed to the incorrect judgments","Asch firmly believed there would be little, if any, yielding to group pressure, but his prediction was wrong","Anthony Pratkanis repeated the experiment on national television with college students who considered themselves nonconformists, and the results were almost identical to Asch's","Bond and Smith's analysis of 133 experiments in 17 countries found conformity is more prevalent in collectivist societies than in individualistic ones","Berns's fMRI replication showed participants who resisted group pressure displayed heightened activity in the amygdala, a region associated with pain, fear, and emotional discomfort","People conform to group pressure about as often when the unanimous group consists of only 3 other people as when it consists of 16","When unanimity is broken, so is the pressure to conform; even a single ally who sees it your way releases you from that pressure","Even a dissenter who gives a different wrong answer sharply reduces conformity, and the participant is likely to give the correct response","It only takes one dissenter to seriously diminish the power of the group to induce conformity"]},{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Social Psychology | Minority Influence | Social Change","topic":"Minority Influence: How Consistent Minorities Shift the Majority","visual_frame_description":"Moscovici 1969: Groups of 6 participants viewed blue slides that varied only in intensity. Confederates consistently called the slides green. When the confederates were consistent (always said green), 32% of participants reported seeing green at least once, and 8% conformed consistently. When confederates were inconsistent (sometimes green, sometimes blue), the effect disappeared. Consistency was the key.","target_channel":"Consistency as Influence | Innovation Through Minority","question":"Why do consistent minorities sometimes change majority opinion, while inconsistent ones have no effect?","correct_cue":"Consistency creates doubt. When a minority consistently maintains a position, it forces the majority to examine the issue more carefully. The majority assumes that people who hold a consistent position must have some basis for it. This triggers informational social influence — the majority looks at the issue from the minority perspective and may shift. Inconsistency allows the majority to dismiss the minority as confused or incompetent. Key factors for minority influence: (1) consistency over time and across individuals, (2) confidence in the position, (3) flexibility (sticking to the position while being willing to negotiate on peripheral details).","distractors":["Minorities influence through persuasion — their arguments are simply more convincing than the majority's","Minorities influence through intimidation — the majority goes along to avoid conflict","Minority influence only works in perceptual tasks like color judgment and does not apply to opinion change"],"underlying_psychology":"Moscovici distinguished majority influence (compliance, public conformity, superficial processing) from minority influence (conversion, private acceptance, deep processing). Minority influence works through informational influence: the minority causes the majority to think more deeply. When a majority disagrees, we conform publicly to avoid conflict (normative influence). When a minority disagrees consistently, we wonder if they might be right and reconsider our position (informational influence). This is how social change happens — small, consistent groups shift the majority over time. Civil rights movements, environmental activism, and scientific revolutions all follow the minority influence pattern.","player_mission":"Why do consistent minorities sometimes change majority opinion, while inconsistent ones have no effect?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["Conformity is a change in a person's behavior or opinions as a result of real or imagined pressure from another person or group of people","Asch's line judgment task was so easy that when people judged the lines alone, they made almost no errors","When faced with their fellow students agreeing on incorrect responses, approximately three-quarters of Asch's participants conformed at least once","Over the entire spectrum of judgments, an average of 35 percent of responses conformed to the incorrect judgments","Asch firmly believed there would be little, if any, yielding to group pressure, but his prediction was wrong","Anthony Pratkanis repeated the experiment on national television with college students who considered themselves nonconformists, and the results were almost identical to Asch's","Bond and Smith's analysis of 133 experiments in 17 countries found conformity is more prevalent in collectivist societies than in individualistic ones","Berns's fMRI replication showed participants who resisted group pressure displayed heightened activity in the amygdala, a region associated with pain, fear, and emotional discomfort","People conform to group pressure about as often when the unanimous group consists of only 3 other people as when it consists of 16","When unanimity is broken, so is the pressure to conform; even a single ally who sees it your way releases you from that pressure","Even a dissenter who gives a different wrong answer sharply reduces conformity, and the participant is likely to give the correct response","It only takes one dissenter to seriously diminish the power of the group to induce conformity"]}]},{"id":"sa_ch05a","title":"Chapter 5: Mass Communication, Propaganda, and Persuasion (Part 1)","pages":"167-214","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Social Psychology | Persuasion | Fear Appeals","topic":"Fear and Action: Why High-Fear Messages Often Fail","visual_frame_description":"Two anti-smoking posters side by side. Poster A: graphic image of diseased lungs with text Smoking Kills in large red letters. Poster B: simple text listing steps for quitting with a phone number for a helpline. Most people recall Poster A as more memorable. Most people acted on Poster B.","target_channel":"Fear-Arc Activation | Persuasion Component Timing","question":"Why do high-fear messages grab attention and get recalled but often fail to change behavior, while low-fear action-oriented messages succeed?","correct_cue":"Too much fear triggers defensive avoidance: people reject, minimize, or avoid the message to reduce their anxiety. Effective persuasion requires the right fear arc: Step 1 - Moderate fear to motivate attention. Step 2 - Specific efficacy information - here is exactly what you can do. Without Step 2, high fear alone causes people to tune out or rationalize (I am young and healthy, this does not apply to me).","distractors":["People are desensitized to fear appeals and no longer react to graphic images","Text-based messages are naturally processed more deeply than image-based messages","High-fear messages trigger the sympathetic nervous system which impairs memory encoding"],"underlying_psychology":"Leventhal 1970 danger control vs fear control framework. Fear appeals create two parallel processes. Fear control: managing the emotional fear response (tune out, deny). Danger control: managing the actual threat (take action). High fear activates fear control, not danger control. The persuasion formula: moderate fear to get attention + clear actionable steps to channel fear into danger control. This is why ads that show the problem then immediately show the product as the solution are effective.","player_mission":"Why do high-fear messages grab attention and get recalled but often fail to change behavior, while low-fear action-oriented messages succeed?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["Persuasion occurs when communication from one person changes the opinions, attitudes, or behavior of another person","An opinion is what a person believes to be true and is primarily cognitive and transient, while an attitude is an opinion that includes an emotional and evaluative component and is extremely difficult to change","The three key factors in any persuasive attempt are the source of the communication, the nature of the communication, and the characteristics and mindset of the audience","The news media influence opinions simply by selecting which events get exposure and how much exposure they get, based largely on entertainment value, so 'if it bleeds, it leads'","A Gallup poll found 60 percent of Americans believed crime was on the rise even though crimes of all kinds had been steadily declining for 25 years, a distortion fed by violent media coverage","Selective emphasis puts the media in a position not only of reporting events but of determining subsequent ones, as when heavy news coverage of suicides increased teen suicides (David Phillips's studies)","More than 90 percent of preschool children asked for toys or food they saw advertised on television, and by the tenth grade only 4 percent of children believed ads told the truth most of the time","Robert Zajonc showed that, all other things being equal, the more familiar an item is, the more attractive it is, even a silly nonsense word","Northwestern Mutual jumped from thirty-fourth to third in name familiarity after two weeks of $1 million worth of advertising, and A&W Root Beer's market share rose from 15 to 50 percent after six months of TV ads","The Willie Horton ads of the 1988 Bush-Dukakis campaign showed that when people are scared and angry, facts alone (like Dukakis's furlough statistics) are neither reassuring nor convincing","Eli Pariser calls the personalized universe of information created by algorithms and social networks the filter bubble, which reinforces our beliefs invisibly so we think we are seeing reality","The more educated and skeptical viewers believe they are immune to persuasion, but our sense of immunity can make us more susceptible to persuasion of all kinds"]},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Social Psychology | Persuasion | Central vs Peripheral Routes","topic":"The Case for Reason: Elaboration Likelihood Model in Action","dialogue":[{"speaker":"You","line":"Judge, the prosecution case rests entirely on circumstantial evidence. There are no eyewitnesses, no forensic match, no confession. The alibi witness is the defendants mother, whom the prosecution dismisses as biased. But if we examined the timeline carefully, the defendant was 40 miles away at the time of the crime."},{"speaker":"Jury Foreperson","line":"That mother is just trying to protect her son. Of course she would say that. And the defendant has a prior record - that tells you something about his character."},{"speaker":"You","line":"Lets set aside character and focus on evidence. The traffic camera 40 miles away captured a vehicle matching the defendants description at 8:47 PM. The coroner places the death at approximately 8:30 PM. Physics alone makes it impossible to travel 40 miles in 17 minutes on these roads. That is not opinion - it is geometry."},{"speaker":"Commentator","line":"Persuasion research reveals that the foreperson is using the peripheral route: relying on surface cues like family relationship bias and criminal record. These simple heuristics require little mental effort. You are appealing to the central route: asking for systematic processing of evidence. The central route produces more durable attitude change when people have the motivation and ability to process. Your evidence-based approach works when the audience is analytical, but you need to address the peripheral cues too before people will engage centrally."}],"psychological_breakdown":"ELM Petty and Cacioppo 1986. Central route: high elaboration, thoughtful consideration of arguments, produces lasting change. Peripheral route: low elaboration, uses heuristics (expertise, attractiveness, consensus), produces temporary change. People take the peripheral route when they lack motivation or ability to process deeply. To persuade effectively: match your approach to your audiences processing mode, or increase their motivation to process centrally.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A courtroom splits down the middle: the left half shows scales weighing argument strength while the right half flashes peripheral cues - a glowing glove, a rhymed slogan, a celebrity smile - and a cursor labeled 'which route?' hovers between them.","key_concepts":["Persuasion occurs when communication from one person changes the opinions, attitudes, or behavior of another person","An opinion is what a person believes to be true and is primarily cognitive and transient, while an attitude is an opinion that includes an emotional and evaluative component and is extremely difficult to change","The three key factors in any persuasive attempt are the source of the communication, the nature of the communication, and the characteristics and mindset of the audience","The news media influence opinions simply by selecting which events get exposure and how much exposure they get, based largely on entertainment value, so 'if it bleeds, it leads'","A Gallup poll found 60 percent of Americans believed crime was on the rise even though crimes of all kinds had been steadily declining for 25 years, a distortion fed by violent media coverage","Selective emphasis puts the media in a position not only of reporting events but of determining subsequent ones, as when heavy news coverage of suicides increased teen suicides (David Phillips's studies)","More than 90 percent of preschool children asked for toys or food they saw advertised on television, and by the tenth grade only 4 percent of children believed ads told the truth most of the time","Robert Zajonc showed that, all other things being equal, the more familiar an item is, the more attractive it is, even a silly nonsense word","Northwestern Mutual jumped from thirty-fourth to third in name familiarity after two weeks of $1 million worth of advertising, and A&W Root Beer's market share rose from 15 to 50 percent after six months of TV ads","The Willie Horton ads of the 1988 Bush-Dukakis campaign showed that when people are scared and angry, facts alone (like Dukakis's furlough statistics) are neither reassuring nor convincing","Eli Pariser calls the personalized universe of information created by algorithms and social networks the filter bubble, which reinforces our beliefs invisibly so we think we are seeing reality","The more educated and skeptical viewers believe they are immune to persuasion, but our sense of immunity can make us more susceptible to persuasion of all kinds"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Social Psychology | Propaganda | Fear-Based Persuasion","topic":"The Means of Persuasion: Slippery Slope as Fear-Mongering Device","subject_baseline":"A political candidate warns: If we legalize same-sex marriage, the next step will be legalizing marriage with animals. The slides: same-sex marriage is a foot-in-the-door for total destruction of traditional values. The audience audience shifts from a specific policy debate to an abstract fear about social collapse.","interrogation_transcript":"The slippery slope argument diverts attention from the immediate issue (whether same-sex couples deserve equal rights) to a hypothetical extreme (marriage licences for dogs). There is no evidence for the causal chain proposed. Countries that legalized same-sex marriage have not subsequently legalized marriage with animals. The argument works by triggering disgust and threat responses that override rational analysis.","observed_nonverbal_log":["The immediate policy issue is replaced by an emotional fear image","No causal evidence links Step A to Step Z","The audience emotional response short-circuits critical evaluation","The arguer avoids defending the actual policy by changing the subject to a more extreme version"],"question":"Why is the slippery slope fallacy so effective as a persuasive device despite having no logical or evidentiary support?","correct_diagnosis":"The slippery slope works via fear conditioning and the affect heuristic. It replaces a complex policy debate with a vivid, disgusting image that triggers automatic avoidance. The emotional response (disgust, fear) contaminates the evaluation of the original policy through misattribution. Additionally, the foot-in-the-door principle (if you accept A, you have committed to the path toward Z) creates false commitment framing. The persuasive trick: make the opponent defend against Z while you avoid defending your position on A.","distractors":["The slippery slope is logically valid - historical evidence shows that small changes always lead to extreme outcomes","The audience is simply too uninformed to recognize the logical fallacy in the argument","The arguer genuinely believes in the causal chain and is not intentionally manipulating"],"diagnostic_breakdown":"Stage 1 - Issue Shifting: Move from debate about issue A to hypothetical extreme Z. Stage 2 - Emotional Contamination: Use disgust/fear to short-circuit analysis. Stage 3 - Burden Shifting: Force opponent to disprove Z while you never defend A. Stage 4 - Social Proof: If others fear Z, the fear seems validated. The defense: Reject the shift. When someone says Accepting A leads to Z, ask: Where is the evidence that A causes Z? If they provide none, recognize the tactic and redirect to the actual issue.","player_mission":"Why is the slippery slope fallacy so effective as a persuasive device despite having no logical or evidentiary support?","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A broadcast studio with a walk of fame: each communicator steps onto a pedestal as a credibility meter swings between expert, trustworthy, self-interested, and persuasive, while a ledger auto-fills with audience impact scores.","key_concepts":["Persuasion occurs when communication from one person changes the opinions, attitudes, or behavior of another person","An opinion is what a person believes to be true and is primarily cognitive and transient, while an attitude is an opinion that includes an emotional and evaluative component and is extremely difficult to change","The three key factors in any persuasive attempt are the source of the communication, the nature of the communication, and the characteristics and mindset of the audience","The news media influence opinions simply by selecting which events get exposure and how much exposure they get, based largely on entertainment value, so 'if it bleeds, it leads'","A Gallup poll found 60 percent of Americans believed crime was on the rise even though crimes of all kinds had been steadily declining for 25 years, a distortion fed by violent media coverage","Selective emphasis puts the media in a position not only of reporting events but of determining subsequent ones, as when heavy news coverage of suicides increased teen suicides (David Phillips's studies)","More than 90 percent of preschool children asked for toys or food they saw advertised on television, and by the tenth grade only 4 percent of children believed ads told the truth most of the time","Robert Zajonc showed that, all other things being equal, the more familiar an item is, the more attractive it is, even a silly nonsense word","Northwestern Mutual jumped from thirty-fourth to third in name familiarity after two weeks of $1 million worth of advertising, and A&W Root Beer's market share rose from 15 to 50 percent after six months of TV ads","The Willie Horton ads of the 1988 Bush-Dukakis campaign showed that when people are scared and angry, facts alone (like Dukakis's furlough statistics) are neither reassuring nor convincing","Eli Pariser calls the personalized universe of information created by algorithms and social networks the filter bubble, which reinforces our beliefs invisibly so we think we are seeing reality","The more educated and skeptical viewers believe they are immune to persuasion, but our sense of immunity can make us more susceptible to persuasion of all kinds"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Social Psychology | Persuasion | Dual Processing","topic":"Peripheral Persuasion: When Surface Cues Trump Substance","stage_1_observation":"Would you be more persuaded by a credit card commercial that features an attractive celebrity reading a complex analysis of interest rates and terms, or one that shows exciting travel montages with simple tagline Live the Life? Most people say the first is more informative. The second sells more credit cards.","stage_1_question":"Why does simple emotional peripheral advertising consistently outperform detailed rational argument in real-world persuasion?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Most real-world persuasion occurs under low elaboration conditions. People lack the motivation or cognitive resources to process complex arguments about mundane products. Peripheral cues (attractiveness, emotional imagery, simple slogans, celebrity endorsements) provide quick heuristics for decision-making. The central route would dominate only when the decision is personally important and the audience is motivated and able to process - like choosing a medical treatment or buying a house.","stage_2_intervention":"You are running a public health campaign to increase vaccination rates. Your research shows that detailed statistical evidence about vaccine safety (central route) is ineffective because most people lack the statistical literacy and motivation to process it. Yet you know emotional testimonials (peripheral route) might feel manipulative. Design a campaign that ethically combines both routes.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Use a two-step approach. Step 1 (Peripheral): A trusted community doctor or nurse - credible expert - delivers a simple emotional message. Vaccination protects what matters most - your child. Use the peripheral cue of expert credibility + emotional hook of caring for children. Step 2 (Central): The ad directs to a website or QR code where those motivated can access the detailed safety data. This respects both processing routes: those who follow peripheral cues get the key message from a trusted authority; those who want central processing can access the evidence. The ethical key: use peripheral cues to get attention, not to deceive, and always make the evidence available.","boss_battle_explanation":"Layer 1 - Low Elaboration: Most daily decisions are peripheral - we use shortcuts because we cannot deeply analyze everything. Layer 2 - High Elaboration: Important personal decisions trigger central processing. Layer 3 - Ethical Persuasion: Use peripheral cues to open the door, central evidence to sustain the change. Layer 4 - Audience Split: A single message can target both routes by providing simple cues for peripheral processors and links to evidence for central processors. The unethical persuader uses peripheral cues to sell bad products; the ethical persuader uses peripheral cues to sell good ones.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A walled city where citizens scroll glowing feeds; hostile banners - 'fake news', 'death panels', 'America Under Attack' - batter the gates while defenders arm a broadcast tower with inoculation speeches and a 'more speech, not enforced silence' banner unfurls.","key_concepts":["Persuasion occurs when communication from one person changes the opinions, attitudes, or behavior of another person","An opinion is what a person believes to be true and is primarily cognitive and transient, while an attitude is an opinion that includes an emotional and evaluative component and is extremely difficult to change","The three key factors in any persuasive attempt are the source of the communication, the nature of the communication, and the characteristics and mindset of the audience","The news media influence opinions simply by selecting which events get exposure and how much exposure they get, based largely on entertainment value, so 'if it bleeds, it leads'","A Gallup poll found 60 percent of Americans believed crime was on the rise even though crimes of all kinds had been steadily declining for 25 years, a distortion fed by violent media coverage","Selective emphasis puts the media in a position not only of reporting events but of determining subsequent ones, as when heavy news coverage of suicides increased teen suicides (David Phillips's studies)","More than 90 percent of preschool children asked for toys or food they saw advertised on television, and by the tenth grade only 4 percent of children believed ads told the truth most of the time","Robert Zajonc showed that, all other things being equal, the more familiar an item is, the more attractive it is, even a silly nonsense word","Northwestern Mutual jumped from thirty-fourth to third in name familiarity after two weeks of $1 million worth of advertising, and A&W Root Beer's market share rose from 15 to 50 percent after six months of TV ads","The Willie Horton ads of the 1988 Bush-Dukakis campaign showed that when people are scared and angry, facts alone (like Dukakis's furlough statistics) are neither reassuring nor convincing","Eli Pariser calls the personalized universe of information created by algorithms and social networks the filter bubble, which reinforces our beliefs invisibly so we think we are seeing reality","The more educated and skeptical viewers believe they are immune to persuasion, but our sense of immunity can make us more susceptible to persuasion of all kinds"]}]},{"id":"sa_ch05b","title":"Chapter 5: Mass Communication, Propaganda, and Persuasion (Part 2)","pages":"167-214","assets":[{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Social Psychology | Persuasion | Resistance","topic":"Inoculation Theory: Vaccinating People Against Persuasion","stage_1_observation":"McGuire 1964: Participants were exposed to weakened versions of arguments against cultural truisms (teeth should be brushed, it is good to get regular medical checkups). Later, when they faced strong versions of those arguments, the inoculated participants resisted persuasion significantly more than uninoculated participants.","stage_1_question":"Why does exposure to a weakened attack make people more resistant to a stronger attack later?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The biological analogy: just as a weakened virus builds antibodies against the full-strength virus, a weakened persuasive attack builds mental antibodies. The process works in two steps: (1) threat — the person realizes their belief is vulnerable (motivating them to defend it), and (2) pre-exposure — the person practices defending their belief against the weakened argument, building counterarguments they can use later. Without inoculation, people are defenseless because they have never considered that their belief could be challenged.","stage_2_intervention":"You work for a public health agency. A new misinformation campaign claims that vaccines cause autism. The campaign uses emotional testimonials and cherry-picked data. You know that refuting the claims after people have heard them is less effective than preventing acceptance. Design an inoculation intervention.","stage_2_correct_answer":"Create a pre-exposure campaign that (1) warns people that they will encounter misinformation about vaccines (threat), (2) presents a weakened version of the autism claim: Some people claim that vaccines cause autism because a celebrity said so and showed a graph, (3) provides the counterargument: that celebrity was referring to a fraudulent study that was retracted, and subsequent massive studies show no link. (4) Forewarns about specific rhetorical techniques: they will use emotional stories, not data. They will cite the fraudulent study as if it were still valid. They will appeal to your intuitions about natural vs artificial. This forewarning + weakened refutation makes people resistant to the full-strength misinformation when they encounter it.","boss_battle_explanation":"Layer 1 - The Gap: People have never considered that their belief might be challenged, so they have no defenses. Layer 2 - Threat Warning: Forewarning that someone will try to change your mind mobilizes defense. Layer 3 - Weakened Attack: Exposure to a mild version of the argument. Layer 4 - Refutation Practice: The person generates or receives counterarguments. Layer 5 - Resistance: When the full-strength attack comes, counterarguments are immediately available. Inoculation is much more effective than refutation-after-the-fact because once a belief is accepted, dislodging it requires overcoming the backfire effect (people resist evidence that contradicts their beliefs).","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A walled city where citizens scroll glowing feeds; hostile banners - 'fake news', 'death panels', 'America Under Attack' - batter the gates while defenders arm a broadcast tower with inoculation speeches and a 'more speech, not enforced silence' banner unfurls.","key_concepts":["Persuasion occurs when communication from one person changes the opinions, attitudes, or behavior of another person","An opinion is what a person believes to be true and is primarily cognitive and transient, while an attitude is an opinion that includes an emotional and evaluative component and is extremely difficult to change","The three key factors in any persuasive attempt are the source of the communication, the nature of the communication, and the characteristics and mindset of the audience","The news media influence opinions simply by selecting which events get exposure and how much exposure they get, based largely on entertainment value, so 'if it bleeds, it leads'","A Gallup poll found 60 percent of Americans believed crime was on the rise even though crimes of all kinds had been steadily declining for 25 years, a distortion fed by violent media coverage","Selective emphasis puts the media in a position not only of reporting events but of determining subsequent ones, as when heavy news coverage of suicides increased teen suicides (David Phillips's studies)","More than 90 percent of preschool children asked for toys or food they saw advertised on television, and by the tenth grade only 4 percent of children believed ads told the truth most of the time","Robert Zajonc showed that, all other things being equal, the more familiar an item is, the more attractive it is, even a silly nonsense word","Northwestern Mutual jumped from thirty-fourth to third in name familiarity after two weeks of $1 million worth of advertising, and A&W Root Beer's market share rose from 15 to 50 percent after six months of TV ads","The Willie Horton ads of the 1988 Bush-Dukakis campaign showed that when people are scared and angry, facts alone (like Dukakis's furlough statistics) are neither reassuring nor convincing","Eli Pariser calls the personalized universe of information created by algorithms and social networks the filter bubble, which reinforces our beliefs invisibly so we think we are seeing reality","The more educated and skeptical viewers believe they are immune to persuasion, but our sense of immunity can make us more susceptible to persuasion of all kinds"]}]},{"id":"sa_ch06a","title":"Chapter 6: Human Aggression (Part 1)","pages":"215-264","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Social Psychology | Aggression | Frustration-Aggression Theory","topic":"The Doll Study: Frustration as Aggression Fuel","visual_frame_description":"Children in a room full of attractive toys. Left alone, they are told these toys are for other children, so you cannot play with them. They are then sent to another room with less interesting toys. When later allowed to play with the attractive toys, they smash them, throw them, and show destructive behavior far beyond baseline.","target_channel":"Frustration-Fueled Hostility | Displaced Aggression","question":"Why did children denied the attractive toys later destroy those same toys when finally given access?","correct_cue":"Frustration creates an aggressive drive that seeks release. The children experienced goal blockage (they wanted the toys but were denied) which created frustration. This frustration built an aggressive energy that, when finally released in the context of the formerly forbidden toys, manifested as destruction rather than enjoyment. The aggression was displaced from the frustrating agent (the adult who denied them) to the trigger (the toys).","distractors":["Children naturally destroy toys - this is normal developmental behavior unrelated to frustration","The attractive toys were poorly made and broke easily during normal play","Children were simply bored with the waiting activity and took it out on the nearest objects"],"underlying_psychology":"Dollard et al 1939 frustration-aggression hypothesis: frustration always leads to some form of aggression, and aggression always stems from frustration. Berkowitz 1989 refined this: frustration produces anger (an emotional readiness for aggression), and aggressive cues in the environment determine whether aggression actually occurs. Guns dont just enable aggression - they prime it, a phenomenon called the weapons effect. The mere presence of a weapon increases aggressive thoughts and behavior.","player_mission":"Why did children denied the attractive toys later destroy those same toys when finally given access?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["Aronson defines aggression as an intentional action aimed at doing harm or causing physical or psychological pain, so an angry friend throwing a beer bottle that misses is still aggression but a drunk driver's accident is not","Thomas Hobbes in Leviathan argued humans are brutes in their natural state, while Rousseau's noble savage held that a restrictive society forces gentle creatures to become hostile","Freud theorized human beings are born with an instinctual drive toward life (eros) and an instinctual drive toward death (thanatos) leading to aggressive actions","Zing Yang Kuo raised a kitten in the same cage with a rat and found the cat refused to attack its buddy or any other rats, showing aggressive instincts can be curbed by early experience","Rats raised in isolation still attack a fellow rat introduced into the cage using the same pattern of threat and attack as experienced rats, so in some species aggression needn't be learned","Our closest genetic relatives are the aggressive chimpanzee and the peaceful bonobo, the 'make love not war' ape that defuses conflict with communal sex","The Iroquois lived in peace for hundreds of years but became ferocious warriors when trade with Europeans brought competition with the Hurons over furs, showing aggression is an optional strategy shaped by circumstances","Richard Nisbett showed homicide rates for white Southern males are substantially higher than for white Northern males, but only for honor-related killings and only in areas with a herding economy","In the culture of honor experiments by Dov Cohen and Richard Nisbett, Southern white males responded to an insult with more aggression, spiking cortisol and testosterone, while Northerners tended to shrug it off","Cultures of honor have rates of honor-related homicide five times higher than other regions, more than twice as many school shootings per capita, and higher rates of domestic violence","The number one predictor of violence is gender: boys engage in more rough-and-tumble play, and in a cross-cultural study by Dane Archer and Patricia McDaniel, young men in all 11 countries showed a greater tendency to end conflict stories with violence","James Dabbs found naturally occurring testosterone levels are significantly higher among prisoners convicted of violent crimes, but Robert Sapolsky argues testosterone does not cause aggression since aggression continues even after castration"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Social Psychology | Aggression Reduction | Catharsis Myth","topic":"The Catharsis Trap: Why Venting Anger Increases Aggression","stage_1_observation":"Employees who were criticized by their boss were given an opportunity to vent their anger: they could either (1) punch a punching bag while imagining the boss face, (2) write about their feelings, or (3) sit quietly for two minutes. The venting group actually became more aggressive afterward, not less.","stage_1_question":"Why does the common wisdom of blow off steam or get it out of your system backfire and increase aggression?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The catharsis hypothesis is false. Venting aggression does not release aggressive energy - it rehearses and reinforces aggressive responses. Punching a bag while imagining the boss face keeps aggressive thoughts active, rehearses aggressive motor patterns, and prevents the cognitive reappraisal that would reduce anger. The two-minute quiet sit allows the physiological arousal to subside, enabling cooler reflection.","stage_2_intervention":"You are a school counselor. A student comes to you furious after being humiliated by a peer in front of the class. The student wants to fight the peer after school. Your colleague suggests letting the student punch a pillow in your office to release the anger before discussing it. Based on the evidence, what should you do instead?","stage_2_correct_answer":"Do NOT let them vent aggressively. Instead, use the two-step reappraisal approach: (1) Time and distance - suggest a brief walk or a glass of water to allow the physiological fury to subside (the 20-minute rule for anger). (2) Cognitive reappraisal - help them reframe the situation. Was the humiliation intentional or accidental? Does the peers opinion actually matter? What would a calm person do? The goal is to reduce the perceived provocation, not to release an imagined aggressive energy. Studies show that reframing reduces future aggression more than any venting activity does.","boss_battle_explanation":"Layer 1 - Catharsis Fallacy: The belief that venting aggression purges it is appealing but wrong. Studies consistently show that playing violent sports, watching violent media, and expressing anger verbally all increase subsequent aggression. Layer 2 - Rehearsal Effect: Each aggressive act, thought, or image rehearses the neural pathways for aggression. Layer 3 - Cooling Off: Time alone reduces physiological arousal (the body cannot sustain high arousal indefinitely). Layer 4 - Reappraisal: Changing the meaning of the provocation is the most effective anger reduction strategy. The enemy hates me is more angering than The enemy is jealous of me. Change the interpretation, change the emotion.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A school auditorium summit table surrounded by glowing strategy cards - punishment severity dials, empathy training videos, mindfulness bells, and cooperative game designs - while a city skyline beyond the windows dims as each intervention succeeds.","key_concepts":["Aronson defines aggression as an intentional action aimed at doing harm or causing physical or psychological pain, so an angry friend throwing a beer bottle that misses is still aggression but a drunk driver's accident is not","Thomas Hobbes in Leviathan argued humans are brutes in their natural state, while Rousseau's noble savage held that a restrictive society forces gentle creatures to become hostile","Freud theorized human beings are born with an instinctual drive toward life (eros) and an instinctual drive toward death (thanatos) leading to aggressive actions","Zing Yang Kuo raised a kitten in the same cage with a rat and found the cat refused to attack its buddy or any other rats, showing aggressive instincts can be curbed by early experience","Rats raised in isolation still attack a fellow rat introduced into the cage using the same pattern of threat and attack as experienced rats, so in some species aggression needn't be learned","Our closest genetic relatives are the aggressive chimpanzee and the peaceful bonobo, the 'make love not war' ape that defuses conflict with communal sex","The Iroquois lived in peace for hundreds of years but became ferocious warriors when trade with Europeans brought competition with the Hurons over furs, showing aggression is an optional strategy shaped by circumstances","Richard Nisbett showed homicide rates for white Southern males are substantially higher than for white Northern males, but only for honor-related killings and only in areas with a herding economy","In the culture of honor experiments by Dov Cohen and Richard Nisbett, Southern white males responded to an insult with more aggression, spiking cortisol and testosterone, while Northerners tended to shrug it off","Cultures of honor have rates of honor-related homicide five times higher than other regions, more than twice as many school shootings per capita, and higher rates of domestic violence","The number one predictor of violence is gender: boys engage in more rough-and-tumble play, and in a cross-cultural study by Dane Archer and Patricia McDaniel, young men in all 11 countries showed a greater tendency to end conflict stories with violence","James Dabbs found naturally occurring testosterone levels are significantly higher among prisoners convicted of violent crimes, but Robert Sapolsky argues testosterone does not cause aggression since aggression continues even after castration"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Social Psychology | Aggression | Media Violence","topic":"The Television Experiment: Media Violence as Aggression Teacher","subject_baseline":"Nursery school children were randomly assigned to watch either aggressive cartoons (Batman and Superman cartoons with violent content) or prosocial cartoons (Mister Rogers Neighborhood). After viewing, their behavior was observed during free play.","interrogation_transcript":"Children who watched the aggressive cartoons immediately showed more aggressive behavior: hitting, pushing, breaking toys, and verbal aggression. Children who watched Mister Rogers showed more prosocial behavior: helping, sharing, and cooperating. The differences were not subtle - they were visible within minutes of viewing and persisted through the observation period.","observed_nonverbal_log":["The aggressive content did not just entertain - it taught specific aggressive behaviors","Children imitated the exact aggression styles they saw (punching, kicking)","The effect was immediate and measurable within minutes","Prosocial content had the opposite effect - increased cooperation and sharing"],"question":"How does media violence cause real-world aggression increases beyond simple imitation of specific punches and kicks?","correct_diagnosis":"Four mechanisms: (1) Disinhibition - watching violence lowers normal restraints against aggression (if Batman does it, it is okay). (2) Imitation - children learn specific aggressive techniques. (3) Desensitization - repeated exposure reduces emotional response to violence, making it easier to perpetrate. (4) Cognitive priming - aggressive content activates aggressive thoughts and scripts that then bias interpretation of neutral situations (an accidental bump is interpreted as a hostile act). The cumulative effect: heavy media consumers develop a hostile world view and lower thresholds for aggressive response.","distractors":["Children who watch violent media were already aggressive - the media did not cause the behavior","The observed effects are temporary and disappear within hours - no long-term harm is done","Aggressive play is normal childhood development and watching violence has no special effect"],"diagnostic_breakdown":"Stage 1 - Short-Term: Imitation and priming - child copies specific acts and interprets ambiguous events as hostile. Stage 2 - Medium-Term: Desensitization - child stops reacting emotionally to violence; aggressive behavior becomes easier. Stage 3 - Long-Term: Worldview shift - heavy media consumers overestimate real-world violence and see the world as a hostile place. Stage 4 - Cumulative: The child who consumed 6+ hours of media daily as a preschooler shows more aggression in adolescence. The correlation persists even controlling for initial aggression levels, parenting style, and socioeconomic status.","player_mission":"How does media violence cause real-world aggression increases beyond simple imitation of specific punches and kicks?","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A summer city street where every aggressive act is traced back to a glowing cause: thermometers over rooftops, a glowing rifle on a desk, cocktail glasses in a bar, and a voodoo doll bristling with pins in a kitchen window.","key_concepts":["Aronson defines aggression as an intentional action aimed at doing harm or causing physical or psychological pain, so an angry friend throwing a beer bottle that misses is still aggression but a drunk driver's accident is not","Thomas Hobbes in Leviathan argued humans are brutes in their natural state, while Rousseau's noble savage held that a restrictive society forces gentle creatures to become hostile","Freud theorized human beings are born with an instinctual drive toward life (eros) and an instinctual drive toward death (thanatos) leading to aggressive actions","Zing Yang Kuo raised a kitten in the same cage with a rat and found the cat refused to attack its buddy or any other rats, showing aggressive instincts can be curbed by early experience","Rats raised in isolation still attack a fellow rat introduced into the cage using the same pattern of threat and attack as experienced rats, so in some species aggression needn't be learned","Our closest genetic relatives are the aggressive chimpanzee and the peaceful bonobo, the 'make love not war' ape that defuses conflict with communal sex","The Iroquois lived in peace for hundreds of years but became ferocious warriors when trade with Europeans brought competition with the Hurons over furs, showing aggression is an optional strategy shaped by circumstances","Richard Nisbett showed homicide rates for white Southern males are substantially higher than for white Northern males, but only for honor-related killings and only in areas with a herding economy","In the culture of honor experiments by Dov Cohen and Richard Nisbett, Southern white males responded to an insult with more aggression, spiking cortisol and testosterone, while Northerners tended to shrug it off","Cultures of honor have rates of honor-related homicide five times higher than other regions, more than twice as many school shootings per capita, and higher rates of domestic violence","The number one predictor of violence is gender: boys engage in more rough-and-tumble play, and in a cross-cultural study by Dane Archer and Patricia McDaniel, young men in all 11 countries showed a greater tendency to end conflict stories with violence","James Dabbs found naturally occurring testosterone levels are significantly higher among prisoners convicted of violent crimes, but Robert Sapolsky argues testosterone does not cause aggression since aggression continues even after castration"]},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Social Psychology | Aggression Types","topic":"Instrumental vs Hostile Aggression: Means vs Ends","column_a_label":"Instrumental Aggression - Harm is a means to another goal. Premeditated, goal-oriented, cold.","column_b_label":"Hostile Aggression - Harm is the goal itself. Reactive, emotional, hot.","rows":[{"scenario":"A hitman kills a target for payment","correct_column":"A","reason":"Aggression is a transaction - harm is a tool for achieving financial gain"},{"scenario":"A jealous husband attacks his ex-wife lover in a rage","correct_column":"B","reason":"Emotional arousal drives aggression directly - the harm itself is the goal"},{"scenario":"A soldier shoots an enemy combatant in battle","correct_column":"A","reason":"Harm is a means to a military objective, not personally motivated rage"},{"scenario":"A student spreads cruel rumors about a classmate who won the position they wanted","correct_column":"B","reason":"Emotional retaliation intended to harm the rival, not to achieve a separate goal"},{"scenario":"A boxer punches their opponent in a championship match","correct_column":"A","reason":"Harm is instrumental to winning within agreed rules - not emotional rage"}],"explanation":"Berkowitz distinguished these types. Hostile aggression is hot, impulsive, and driven by anger - the goal is to make the victim suffer. Instrumental aggression is cold, calculated, and serves other goals - the victim is a means to an end. The same act (punching) can be either depending on motive. The distinction matters for intervention: hostile aggression responds to anger management and cooling-off periods; instrumental aggression requires incentive restructuring and consequence strengthening.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Advanced","visual_frame_description":"A courtroom evidence wall covered with cards and video stills: a child punching a Bobo doll, a man slugging a punching bag, a gory boxing match, and a violent video game screen; a verdict banner asks 'Does venting reduce or increase hostility?' while the pointer swings to increase.","key_concepts":["Aronson defines aggression as an intentional action aimed at doing harm or causing physical or psychological pain, so an angry friend throwing a beer bottle that misses is still aggression but a drunk driver's accident is not","Thomas Hobbes in Leviathan argued humans are brutes in their natural state, while Rousseau's noble savage held that a restrictive society forces gentle creatures to become hostile","Freud theorized human beings are born with an instinctual drive toward life (eros) and an instinctual drive toward death (thanatos) leading to aggressive actions","Zing Yang Kuo raised a kitten in the same cage with a rat and found the cat refused to attack its buddy or any other rats, showing aggressive instincts can be curbed by early experience","Rats raised in isolation still attack a fellow rat introduced into the cage using the same pattern of threat and attack as experienced rats, so in some species aggression needn't be learned","Our closest genetic relatives are the aggressive chimpanzee and the peaceful bonobo, the 'make love not war' ape that defuses conflict with communal sex","The Iroquois lived in peace for hundreds of years but became ferocious warriors when trade with Europeans brought competition with the Hurons over furs, showing aggression is an optional strategy shaped by circumstances","Richard Nisbett showed homicide rates for white Southern males are substantially higher than for white Northern males, but only for honor-related killings and only in areas with a herding economy","In the culture of honor experiments by Dov Cohen and Richard Nisbett, Southern white males responded to an insult with more aggression, spiking cortisol and testosterone, while Northerners tended to shrug it off","Cultures of honor have rates of honor-related homicide five times higher than other regions, more than twice as many school shootings per capita, and higher rates of domestic violence","The number one predictor of violence is gender: boys engage in more rough-and-tumble play, and in a cross-cultural study by Dane Archer and Patricia McDaniel, young men in all 11 countries showed a greater tendency to end conflict stories with violence","James Dabbs found naturally occurring testosterone levels are significantly higher among prisoners convicted of violent crimes, but Robert Sapolsky argues testosterone does not cause aggression since aggression continues even after castration"]}]},{"id":"sa_ch06b","title":"Chapter 6: Human Aggression (Part 2)","pages":"215-264","assets":[{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Social Psychology | Aggression | Social Learning","topic":"The Bobo Doll Study: Aggression Is Learned Through Observation","subject_baseline":"Bandura 1961: Children watched an adult model attack a large inflatable Bobo doll. The adult punched it, kicked it, hit it with a mallet, and shouted aggressive phrases: Sock him in the nose! Pow! Throw him in the air! Later, the children were placed in a room with the same Bobo doll. They imitated the adults aggressive behavior with remarkable precision — including the same specific actions and verbal phrases.","interrogation_transcript":"The children did not just become more aggressive in general — they specifically imitated the exact behaviors they observed: (1) using the same weapons (mallet, fist, feet), (2) striking the doll in the same body parts, (3) shouting the same aggressive phrases. A control group that did not see the aggressive model showed significantly less aggression. A third group that watched the model be punished for aggression showed less imitation — demonstrating that vicarious consequences also matter.","observed_nonverbal_log":["Children imitated specific aggressive behaviors, not just general arousal","The exact actions (mallet, punch, kick) and phrases (Pow!) were reproduced","Vicarious reinforcement (seeing the model punished) reduced imitation","The learning occurred through observation alone, without any direct reinforcement"],"question":"If the children were never rewarded for aggression, why did they learn and reproduce the aggressive behaviors?","correct_diagnosis":"Banduras social learning theory shows that learning does not require direct reinforcement. Observational learning (modeling) occurs when an observer watches a model and stores the behavior mentally, even if it is never performed. The children learned the aggressive behaviors through observation alone. They performed them because the situation permitted it and the behavior was disinhibited by the models example. Social learning theory explains how aggression spreads through media, peer groups, and families. Children who observe violent parents, violent media, or violent peers learn specific aggressive techniques and disinhibition toward using them.","distractors":["The children were frustrated before the study and the Bobo doll simply allowed them to release pre-existing aggression","The Bobo doll is designed to be hit — children of this age naturally hit inflatable toys regardless of observation","The learning was temporary and did not persist — the children forgot the behaviors within hours"],"diagnostic_breakdown":"Stage 1 - Observation: Child watches aggressive model. Stage 2 - Encoding: Child stores observed behaviors in memory (learning without performance). Stage 3 - Disinhibition: The models behavior signals that aggression is acceptable in this context. Stage 4 - Performance: When given the opportunity, the child reproduces the learned behaviors. Stage 5 - Reinforcement: If the child is rewarded for aggression (or sees the model rewarded), the behavior is reinforced and becomes more likely. Banduras four-step model: Attention, Retention, Reproduction, Motivation. All four are required for observational learning to result in behavior.","player_mission":"If the children were never rewarded for aggression, why did they learn and reproduce the aggressive behaviors?","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A summer city street where every aggressive act is traced back to a glowing cause: thermometers over rooftops, a glowing rifle on a desk, cocktail glasses in a bar, and a voodoo doll bristling with pins in a kitchen window.","key_concepts":["Aronson defines aggression as an intentional action aimed at doing harm or causing physical or psychological pain, so an angry friend throwing a beer bottle that misses is still aggression but a drunk driver's accident is not","Thomas Hobbes in Leviathan argued humans are brutes in their natural state, while Rousseau's noble savage held that a restrictive society forces gentle creatures to become hostile","Freud theorized human beings are born with an instinctual drive toward life (eros) and an instinctual drive toward death (thanatos) leading to aggressive actions","Zing Yang Kuo raised a kitten in the same cage with a rat and found the cat refused to attack its buddy or any other rats, showing aggressive instincts can be curbed by early experience","Rats raised in isolation still attack a fellow rat introduced into the cage using the same pattern of threat and attack as experienced rats, so in some species aggression needn't be learned","Our closest genetic relatives are the aggressive chimpanzee and the peaceful bonobo, the 'make love not war' ape that defuses conflict with communal sex","The Iroquois lived in peace for hundreds of years but became ferocious warriors when trade with Europeans brought competition with the Hurons over furs, showing aggression is an optional strategy shaped by circumstances","Richard Nisbett showed homicide rates for white Southern males are substantially higher than for white Northern males, but only for honor-related killings and only in areas with a herding economy","In the culture of honor experiments by Dov Cohen and Richard Nisbett, Southern white males responded to an insult with more aggression, spiking cortisol and testosterone, while Northerners tended to shrug it off","Cultures of honor have rates of honor-related homicide five times higher than other regions, more than twice as many school shootings per capita, and higher rates of domestic violence","The number one predictor of violence is gender: boys engage in more rough-and-tumble play, and in a cross-cultural study by Dane Archer and Patricia McDaniel, young men in all 11 countries showed a greater tendency to end conflict stories with violence","James Dabbs found naturally occurring testosterone levels are significantly higher among prisoners convicted of violent crimes, but Robert Sapolsky argues testosterone does not cause aggression since aggression continues even after castration"]}]},{"id":"sa_ch07a","title":"Chapter 7: Prejudice and Discrimination (Part 1)","pages":"265-314","assets":[{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Social Psychology | Prejudice | Realistic Conflict Theory","topic":"The Robbers Cave Experiment: Creating and Reversing Prejudice","subject_baseline":"Twenty-two 11-12-year-old boys at a summer camp. Randomly divided into two groups: Eagles and Rattlers. Each group developed their own norms, leaders, and identities. They did not know the other group existed until Phase 2.","interrogation_transcript":"Phase 2 introduced competitive activities (baseball, tug-of-war) with prizes for winners. Conflict escalated rapidly. Groups raided each others cabins, burned flags, called names, and fought. Negative stereotypes emerged. Each group saw themselves as brave and friendly, the others as sneaky and mean. Even though the groups were randomly created with no prior differences, intense intergroup hostility emerged within days.","observed_nonverbal_log":["Groups were randomly assigned but developed distinct cultures within days","Competition for scarce resources (prizes, trophies) triggered hostility","Stereotypes of the outgroup became uniformly negative","Ingroup favoritism intensified - Eagles were brave, Rattlers were sneaky"],"question":"What caused previously friendly boys to become hostile enemies, and what did it take to reverse the hostility?","correct_diagnosis":"Sherif 1961 Robbers Cave Experiment. Cause: realistic conflict over scarce resources (competitive tournament). The competition created zero-sum framing - their win is our loss. This produced ingroup solidarity + outgroup hostility. Cure: superordinate goals - shared objectives that required cooperation between groups (fixing a broken water supply, pulling a stuck truck). Contact alone was insufficient. But cooperative contact toward shared goals systematically reduced prejudice and created cross-group friendships.","distractors":["The boys were naturally aggressive and the camp simply revealed their true competitive natures","The counselors inadvertently favored one group, creating justified resentment in the other","The groups developed genuine cultural differences that naturally led to conflict"],"diagnostic_breakdown":"Stage 1 - Minimal Groups: Simply dividing people into groups creates ingroup bias (Tajfel minimal group paradigm - even random assignment to meaningless groups produces favoritism). Stage 2 - Competition: Scarce resources amplify ingroup/outgroup dynamics. Stage 3 - Dehumanization: Outgroup becomes stereotyped negatively, seen as less human. Stage 4 - Superordinate Goals: Only cooperative pursuit of shared goals reverses the hostility. This has profound implications for reducing real-world prejudice: integrated contact must be cooperative, equal-status, and supported by authority.","player_mission":"What caused previously friendly boys to become hostile enemies, and what did it take to reverse the hostility?","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A job market diorama: identical resumes float onto desks and get shuffled into a yes pile or a no pile, an orchestra auditions behind a screen, a woman in a fat suit drinks a milkshake in a department store, and an eye-tracking heat map shows teachers staring at a black boy at play.","key_concepts":["Aronson defines prejudice as a negative attitude toward all members of a distinguishable group, based solely on their membership in the group, and it is partly cognitive, partly emotional, and partly behavioral","Categorization, the grouping of people by key characteristics, is fundamental to cognition and can be the first step toward prejudice, and the fundamental category for social animals is us or them","Newborns show no preference for faces of one race or another, but by three months of age babies in a mono-racial setting prefer faces of their own race, showing we are predisposed to like people like us and be wary of people who are different","Walter Lippmann, the first to use the term stereotype, described the difference between reality - the 'world out there' - and the 'little pictures in our heads'","Contrary to conventional wisdom, stereotypes are often accurate and can be an adaptive, shorthand way of dealing with complexity; Lee Jussim showed stereotype accuracy is among the strongest and most reliable findings in social psychology","A stereotype that is accurate as a group average can be woefully incomplete and misleading when applied to an individual, as with the 'model minority' myth that torments a Chinese male who hates math","Jeff Stone's experiment: students who believed basketball player 'Mark Flick' was African American rated him as having more natural athletic ability and playing a better game, while those who thought he was white rated him as having greater hustle and basketball smarts","Birt Duncan showed people interpreted the same shove differently depending on who did it - aggression if a black man shoved a white man, playful if a white man shoved a black man - even when the interpreters were themselves black","James Pennebaker wired up men and women with voice recorders and found both sexes used about 16,000 words per day, so the girls-talk-more stereotype is simply false","Peter Glick and Susan Fiske found sexism worldwide takes two forms - hostile sexism, holding negative stereotypes of women, and benevolent sexism, holding positive but patronizing stereotypes that idealize women as needing protection","In the hurricane naming study, raters thought male-named hurricanes were more intense than female-named ones and said they'd be more likely to evacuate for Hurricane Christopher than for identically described Hurricane Christina","Oliver Wendell Holmes Jr. compared the mind of a bigot to the pupil of an eye: 'The more light you pour on it, the more it contracts', and as Allport put it, 'Defeated intellectually, prejudice lingers emotionally'"]},{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Social Psychology | Prejudice | Automatic vs Controlled Processing","topic":"The Unconscious Bigot: Automatic Stereotype Activation","visual_frame_description":"In a computer task, participants see a face (Black or White) flashed for 30 milliseconds - too fast for conscious awareness. Immediately after, a word appears on screen. Participants name the word color (Stroop task). After Black faces, participants are faster to name the color of aggression-related words. After White faces, they are faster to name the color of positive words. This happens regardless of the participants conscious racial attitudes.","target_channel":"Automatic Stereotype Activation | Implicit Bias","question":"Why do even explicitly egalitarian people show automatic stereotype activation in laboratory conditions?","correct_cue":"Stereotypes are cognitive schemas - mental structures that organize information. In a culture saturated with racial associations, everyone learns the cultural stereotype regardless of personal beliefs. Automatic activation is like a reflex: seeing a face triggers the learned stereotype before conscious thought can intervene. Controlled processing (I do not endorse this stereotype) can override the activation, but only if the person is motivated and has cognitive resources available. Under time pressure or cognitive load, the automatic response dominates.","distractors":["The test reveals true hidden racism that people are lying about on surveys","Stereotype activation only occurs in prejudiced people - egalitarians show no effect","The task measures reaction times, not prejudice, and has no reliability or validity"],"underlying_psychology":"Devine 1989 dual-process model of prejudice. Two levels: (1) Automatic activation - cultural stereotype knowledge is automatically triggered by relevant cues. This is a cognitive reflex, not a character flaw. (2) Controlled inhibition - personal beliefs can override the automatic response, but this requires awareness, motivation, and cognitive resources. Under fatigue, distraction, or time pressure, the automatic stereotype leaks through. This explains why well-intentioned people make prejudiced slip-ups. The remedy is not guilt but awareness and habit-building: practice overriding automatic associations until the override itself becomes automatic.","player_mission":"Why do even explicitly egalitarian people show automatic stereotype activation in laboratory conditions?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["Aronson defines prejudice as a negative attitude toward all members of a distinguishable group, based solely on their membership in the group, and it is partly cognitive, partly emotional, and partly behavioral","Categorization, the grouping of people by key characteristics, is fundamental to cognition and can be the first step toward prejudice, and the fundamental category for social animals is us or them","Newborns show no preference for faces of one race or another, but by three months of age babies in a mono-racial setting prefer faces of their own race, showing we are predisposed to like people like us and be wary of people who are different","Walter Lippmann, the first to use the term stereotype, described the difference between reality - the 'world out there' - and the 'little pictures in our heads'","Contrary to conventional wisdom, stereotypes are often accurate and can be an adaptive, shorthand way of dealing with complexity; Lee Jussim showed stereotype accuracy is among the strongest and most reliable findings in social psychology","A stereotype that is accurate as a group average can be woefully incomplete and misleading when applied to an individual, as with the 'model minority' myth that torments a Chinese male who hates math","Jeff Stone's experiment: students who believed basketball player 'Mark Flick' was African American rated him as having more natural athletic ability and playing a better game, while those who thought he was white rated him as having greater hustle and basketball smarts","Birt Duncan showed people interpreted the same shove differently depending on who did it - aggression if a black man shoved a white man, playful if a white man shoved a black man - even when the interpreters were themselves black","James Pennebaker wired up men and women with voice recorders and found both sexes used about 16,000 words per day, so the girls-talk-more stereotype is simply false","Peter Glick and Susan Fiske found sexism worldwide takes two forms - hostile sexism, holding negative stereotypes of women, and benevolent sexism, holding positive but patronizing stereotypes that idealize women as needing protection","In the hurricane naming study, raters thought male-named hurricanes were more intense than female-named ones and said they'd be more likely to evacuate for Hurricane Christopher than for identically described Hurricane Christina","Oliver Wendell Holmes Jr. compared the mind of a bigot to the pupil of an eye: 'The more light you pour on it, the more it contracts', and as Allport put it, 'Defeated intellectually, prejudice lingers emotionally'"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Social Psychology | Prejudice Reduction","topic":"The Contact Hypothesis: When Contact Reduces Prejudice and When It Backfires","stage_1_observation":"School desegregation in the United States after Brown v Board of Education had mixed results. Some schools saw reduced prejudice; others saw increased hostility. Simply putting different groups together was not enough.","stage_1_question":"Why did desegregation sometimes increase rather than decrease racial prejudice?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Contact alone is insufficient - and can backfire. Allport 1954 specified four necessary conditions for contact to reduce prejudice: (1) equal status within the contact situation, (2) cooperation toward shared goals, (3) institutional support, and (4) opportunity for personal acquaintance. When contact occurs under conditions of unequal status, competition, or no authority support, it confirms existing stereotypes. A Black principal visiting a White school as a guest speaker (high status, expert role) works. A Black janitor (unequal role) does not challenge stereotypes.","stage_2_intervention":"You are a manager at a company with segregated teams. Engineering is mostly Asian, Marketing is mostly White. Tensions are high. You arrange a monthly lunch mixer where teams mingle freely (contact). After three months, the tensions have increased. What went wrong and what should you do instead?","stage_2_correct_answer":"The lunch mixer violated Allport conditions: (1) unequal status - engineers have higher status at the company. (2) no cooperation - mingling is not cooperative work. (3) no shared goal - there is no superordinate objective. Instead: Create a cross-functional project team for a high-priority initiative that requires both teams to succeed. Equalize status by giving each member specific expertise that the project needs. Provide clear institutional support from leadership. Create opportunities for genuine acquaintance beyond stereotypes (shared meals during the project are fine as a supplement, not as the main intervention). The superordinate goal is the key that Sherif found at Robbers Cave.","boss_battle_explanation":"Layer 1 - Mere Contact: Insufficient and potentially harmful. Layer 2 - Allport Conditions: Equal status, cooperation, shared goals, institutional support, personal acquaintance. Layer 3 - Superordinate Goals: To borrow from chapter 6, cooperative interdependence toward shared objectives transforms us/them into we. Layer 4 - Friendship Potential: The contact must allow people to learn that outgroup members are individuals, not stereotypes. When these conditions are met, contact reduces prejudice not just toward the specific individuals encountered but toward the entire outgroup category.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A schoolyard where the lunchtime clusters of black, Latino, and white students slowly dissolve into mixed groups holding puzzle-piece cards; in a classroom, a nervous Mexican American boy recites his paragraph while other children learn to ask gentle, probing questions.","key_concepts":["Aronson defines prejudice as a negative attitude toward all members of a distinguishable group, based solely on their membership in the group, and it is partly cognitive, partly emotional, and partly behavioral","Categorization, the grouping of people by key characteristics, is fundamental to cognition and can be the first step toward prejudice, and the fundamental category for social animals is us or them","Newborns show no preference for faces of one race or another, but by three months of age babies in a mono-racial setting prefer faces of their own race, showing we are predisposed to like people like us and be wary of people who are different","Walter Lippmann, the first to use the term stereotype, described the difference between reality - the 'world out there' - and the 'little pictures in our heads'","Contrary to conventional wisdom, stereotypes are often accurate and can be an adaptive, shorthand way of dealing with complexity; Lee Jussim showed stereotype accuracy is among the strongest and most reliable findings in social psychology","A stereotype that is accurate as a group average can be woefully incomplete and misleading when applied to an individual, as with the 'model minority' myth that torments a Chinese male who hates math","Jeff Stone's experiment: students who believed basketball player 'Mark Flick' was African American rated him as having more natural athletic ability and playing a better game, while those who thought he was white rated him as having greater hustle and basketball smarts","Birt Duncan showed people interpreted the same shove differently depending on who did it - aggression if a black man shoved a white man, playful if a white man shoved a black man - even when the interpreters were themselves black","James Pennebaker wired up men and women with voice recorders and found both sexes used about 16,000 words per day, so the girls-talk-more stereotype is simply false","Peter Glick and Susan Fiske found sexism worldwide takes two forms - hostile sexism, holding negative stereotypes of women, and benevolent sexism, holding positive but patronizing stereotypes that idealize women as needing protection","In the hurricane naming study, raters thought male-named hurricanes were more intense than female-named ones and said they'd be more likely to evacuate for Hurricane Christopher than for identically described Hurricane Christina","Oliver Wendell Holmes Jr. compared the mind of a bigot to the pupil of an eye: 'The more light you pour on it, the more it contracts', and as Allport put it, 'Defeated intellectually, prejudice lingers emotionally'"]},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Social Psychology | Prejudice | Levels of Bias","topic":"Modern Racism: Overt vs Subtle Discrimination","column_a_label":"Overt / Old-Fashioned Racism - Explicit, conscious, open. I believe my group is superior and theirs is inferior.","column_b_label":"Modern / Symbolic Racism - Subtle, indirect, disguised as principled objection. I oppose busing because I believe in neighborhood schools.","rows":[{"scenario":"A landlord refuses to rent to a tenant because of their skin color","correct_column":"A","reason":"Direct discrimination based on explicit racial belief"},{"scenario":"A voter opposes affirmative action on the grounds of meritocracy and colorblindness","correct_column":"B","reason":"The opposition uses abstract principles that disproportionately harm minorities without explicitly mentioning race"},{"scenario":"A store security guard follows a Black customer more closely than White customers","correct_column":"B","reason":"Subtle behavioral discrimination that can be denied as safety procedure rather than racism"},{"scenario":"A club openly states Whites Only membership policy","correct_column":"A","reason":"Explicit, direct, and openly stated racial exclusion"},{"scenario":"A manager consistently hires White candidates citing better cultural fit without specific evidence","correct_column":"B","reason":"The discrimination is justified through a non-racial category that has a racial effect"}],"explanation":"Sears and McConahay identified modern racism as a post-civil-rights shift from overt bigotry to subtle bias. Modern racists endorse traditional values (hard work, self-reliance, merit) and oppose policies that would reduce inequality, framing their opposition in abstract principles rather than racial hostility. They deny personal prejudice while opposing almost all structural remedies for discrimination. This form is harder to detect and confront because the perpetrator sincerely does not see themselves as racist.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Advanced","visual_frame_description":"A testing hall where identical exams float under different banners - 'measuring your intellectual ability' versus 'testing the test' - while anxiety meters rise and fall; beneath them, essays about personal values glow green as buffers against the threat.","key_concepts":["Aronson defines prejudice as a negative attitude toward all members of a distinguishable group, based solely on their membership in the group, and it is partly cognitive, partly emotional, and partly behavioral","Categorization, the grouping of people by key characteristics, is fundamental to cognition and can be the first step toward prejudice, and the fundamental category for social animals is us or them","Newborns show no preference for faces of one race or another, but by three months of age babies in a mono-racial setting prefer faces of their own race, showing we are predisposed to like people like us and be wary of people who are different","Walter Lippmann, the first to use the term stereotype, described the difference between reality - the 'world out there' - and the 'little pictures in our heads'","Contrary to conventional wisdom, stereotypes are often accurate and can be an adaptive, shorthand way of dealing with complexity; Lee Jussim showed stereotype accuracy is among the strongest and most reliable findings in social psychology","A stereotype that is accurate as a group average can be woefully incomplete and misleading when applied to an individual, as with the 'model minority' myth that torments a Chinese male who hates math","Jeff Stone's experiment: students who believed basketball player 'Mark Flick' was African American rated him as having more natural athletic ability and playing a better game, while those who thought he was white rated him as having greater hustle and basketball smarts","Birt Duncan showed people interpreted the same shove differently depending on who did it - aggression if a black man shoved a white man, playful if a white man shoved a black man - even when the interpreters were themselves black","James Pennebaker wired up men and women with voice recorders and found both sexes used about 16,000 words per day, so the girls-talk-more stereotype is simply false","Peter Glick and Susan Fiske found sexism worldwide takes two forms - hostile sexism, holding negative stereotypes of women, and benevolent sexism, holding positive but patronizing stereotypes that idealize women as needing protection","In the hurricane naming study, raters thought male-named hurricanes were more intense than female-named ones and said they'd be more likely to evacuate for Hurricane Christopher than for identically described Hurricane Christina","Oliver Wendell Holmes Jr. compared the mind of a bigot to the pupil of an eye: 'The more light you pour on it, the more it contracts', and as Allport put it, 'Defeated intellectually, prejudice lingers emotionally'"]}]},{"id":"sa_ch07b","title":"Chapter 7: Prejudice and Discrimination (Part 2)","pages":"265-314","assets":[{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Social Psychology | Stereotype Threat | Performance","topic":"Stereotype Threat: The Burden of Confirming a Negative Stereotype About Your Group","subject_baseline":"Steele and Aronson 1995: Black and White Stanford students took a difficult verbal test. Half were told the test measured intellectual ability (activating the stereotype about Black intellectual inferiority). The other half were told it was a laboratory problem-solving task (no stereotype activation). When the stereotype was activated, Black students performed significantly worse than White students. When the stereotype was not activated, Black and White students performed equally.","interrogation_transcript":"Stereotype threat is the psychological burden of being at risk of confirming a negative stereotype about one's group. When the stereotype is activated, it creates: (1) physiological stress — increased heart rate, blood pressure, cortisol, (2) cognitive interference — monitoring oneself for signs of confirming the stereotype uses working memory capacity, (3) suppression of stereotype-relevant thoughts — which ironically makes them more accessible, (4) reduced performance expectations — leading to disidentification. The effect is not about internalized belief in the stereotype — it affects even highly confident, successful members of stereotyped groups.","observed_nonverbal_log":["The same students performed differently depending on whether the stereotype was activated","The effect was not due to ability differences — the students were matched on SAT scores","Stereotype threat affects any group when a relevant negative stereotype is activated","Women perform worse on math tests when told the test measures gender differences"],"question":"How can the mere awareness of a stereotype — which the person may not believe — cause a measurable drop in performance?","correct_diagnosis":"Stereotype threat works through cognitive load and physiological stress. The awareness of being judged through a negative stereotype creates: (1) anxiety about confirming the stereotype, (2) emotional regulation to manage the anxiety, (3) thought suppression (do not think about the stereotype), and (4) hypervigilance about ones performance. All of these consume working memory capacity that would otherwise be available for the task. The result: the very anxiety about confirming the stereotype makes confirmation more likely. The effect is not about ability or motivation — it is about situational pressure overwhelming cognitive resources.","distractors":["Black students who believe the stereotype are less confident and perform worse; Black students who reject the stereotype are unaffected","The effect is caused by test bias — standardized tests are culturally biased against minority groups","Stereotype threat only affects people with low self-esteem who are vulnerable to external judgments"],"diagnostic_breakdown":"Stage 1 - Stereotype Activation: A situational cue makes a negative stereotype relevant. Stage 2 - Threat Appraisal: The person perceives the risk of confirming the stereotype. Stage 3 - Physiological Stress: Increased cortisol, blood pressure. Stage 4 - Cognitive Interference: Working memory is consumed by monitoring, suppression, and anxiety. Stage 5 - Performance Decrement: Reduced cognitive resources lead to poorer performance. Stage 6 - Potential Long-Term Effect: Repeated experiences of stereotype threat can lead to disidentification with the domain (I do not care about math anyway). The implications: stereotype threat can be reduced by reframing tests as challenges rather than threats, emphasizing growth mindset, and providing role models.","player_mission":"How can the mere awareness of a stereotype — which the person may not believe — cause a measurable drop in performance?","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A job market diorama: identical resumes float onto desks and get shuffled into a yes pile or a no pile, an orchestra auditions behind a screen, a woman in a fat suit drinks a milkshake in a department store, and an eye-tracking heat map shows teachers staring at a black boy at play.","key_concepts":["Aronson defines prejudice as a negative attitude toward all members of a distinguishable group, based solely on their membership in the group, and it is partly cognitive, partly emotional, and partly behavioral","Categorization, the grouping of people by key characteristics, is fundamental to cognition and can be the first step toward prejudice, and the fundamental category for social animals is us or them","Newborns show no preference for faces of one race or another, but by three months of age babies in a mono-racial setting prefer faces of their own race, showing we are predisposed to like people like us and be wary of people who are different","Walter Lippmann, the first to use the term stereotype, described the difference between reality - the 'world out there' - and the 'little pictures in our heads'","Contrary to conventional wisdom, stereotypes are often accurate and can be an adaptive, shorthand way of dealing with complexity; Lee Jussim showed stereotype accuracy is among the strongest and most reliable findings in social psychology","A stereotype that is accurate as a group average can be woefully incomplete and misleading when applied to an individual, as with the 'model minority' myth that torments a Chinese male who hates math","Jeff Stone's experiment: students who believed basketball player 'Mark Flick' was African American rated him as having more natural athletic ability and playing a better game, while those who thought he was white rated him as having greater hustle and basketball smarts","Birt Duncan showed people interpreted the same shove differently depending on who did it - aggression if a black man shoved a white man, playful if a white man shoved a black man - even when the interpreters were themselves black","James Pennebaker wired up men and women with voice recorders and found both sexes used about 16,000 words per day, so the girls-talk-more stereotype is simply false","Peter Glick and Susan Fiske found sexism worldwide takes two forms - hostile sexism, holding negative stereotypes of women, and benevolent sexism, holding positive but patronizing stereotypes that idealize women as needing protection","In the hurricane naming study, raters thought male-named hurricanes were more intense than female-named ones and said they'd be more likely to evacuate for Hurricane Christopher than for identically described Hurricane Christina","Oliver Wendell Holmes Jr. compared the mind of a bigot to the pupil of an eye: 'The more light you pour on it, the more it contracts', and as Allport put it, 'Defeated intellectually, prejudice lingers emotionally'"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Social Psychology | Prejudice Reduction | Education","topic":"The Jigsaw Classroom: Aronson's Cooperative Learning Solution to Prejudice","stage_1_observation":"Austin, Texas, 1971. Schools were newly desegregated. White, Black, and Hispanic students were in the same classrooms but hostile and segregated within them. Competition was intense. Academic performance, especially among minority students, was declining. Intergroup tension was high. Aronson was called in to help.","stage_1_question":"Why did simply putting different racial groups in the same classroom increase hostility rather than reduce it?","stage_1_correct_answer":"The contact was competitive, not cooperative. Students competed for grades, teacher attention, and status. Competition between already-hostile groups confirms negative stereotypes. Aronson designed the Jigsaw Classroom as an alternative: students are divided into mixed-race groups, and each student is given a unique piece of information essential to the groups success — like a jigsaw puzzle where each person holds one piece. To complete the assignment, students must listen to and depend on each other. This creates interdependence, not competition.","stage_2_intervention":"You manage a team with two warring departments. Engineering and Sales refuse to cooperate. Engineering thinks Sales is lazy and ignorant. Sales thinks Engineering is arrogant and unhelpful. Standard team-building has failed. Design a Jigsaw intervention for this workplace.","stage_2_correct_answer":"(1) Cross-functional project teams: Create mixed teams with members from both departments. (2) Jigsaw the information: Give each member a unique piece of information needed to solve the problem. Engineering has the technical constraints. Sales has the customer requirements. Project management has the timeline. Each member becomes the expert on their piece. (3) Interdependent goal: The team cannot solve the problem without every member contributing their piece. This creates genuine interdependence. (4) Equal status: Each members contribution is visibly essential. (5) Superordinate goal: Team success depends on cooperation, not winning. The key difference from standard team-building: the task genuinely requires cooperation — it is not artificially created trust falls but real work that forces interdependence. The Jigsaw method reduces prejudice by creating positive interdependence, equal-status contact, and opportunities to discover that outgroup members are competent and valuable.","boss_battle_explanation":"Layer 1 - Competitive Contact: Contact without interdependence increases hostility. Layer 2 - Jigsaw Design: Create tasks that require everyone to contribute unique, essential information. Layer 3 - Interdependence: Each person holds one piece of the puzzle. Layer 4 - Equal Status: Each piece is equally necessary. Layer 5 - Outcome: Cooperation across group lines reduces prejudice by creating genuine appreciation for outgroup members contributions. Layer 6 - Evidence: Aronson found that Jigsaw classrooms showed increased academic performance, increased cross-racial friendships, and decreased prejudice. The method works because it changes the situation, not the attitudes directly.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A schoolyard where the lunchtime clusters of black, Latino, and white students slowly dissolve into mixed groups holding puzzle-piece cards; in a classroom, a nervous Mexican American boy recites his paragraph while other children learn to ask gentle, probing questions.","key_concepts":["Aronson defines prejudice as a negative attitude toward all members of a distinguishable group, based solely on their membership in the group, and it is partly cognitive, partly emotional, and partly behavioral","Categorization, the grouping of people by key characteristics, is fundamental to cognition and can be the first step toward prejudice, and the fundamental category for social animals is us or them","Newborns show no preference for faces of one race or another, but by three months of age babies in a mono-racial setting prefer faces of their own race, showing we are predisposed to like people like us and be wary of people who are different","Walter Lippmann, the first to use the term stereotype, described the difference between reality - the 'world out there' - and the 'little pictures in our heads'","Contrary to conventional wisdom, stereotypes are often accurate and can be an adaptive, shorthand way of dealing with complexity; Lee Jussim showed stereotype accuracy is among the strongest and most reliable findings in social psychology","A stereotype that is accurate as a group average can be woefully incomplete and misleading when applied to an individual, as with the 'model minority' myth that torments a Chinese male who hates math","Jeff Stone's experiment: students who believed basketball player 'Mark Flick' was African American rated him as having more natural athletic ability and playing a better game, while those who thought he was white rated him as having greater hustle and basketball smarts","Birt Duncan showed people interpreted the same shove differently depending on who did it - aggression if a black man shoved a white man, playful if a white man shoved a black man - even when the interpreters were themselves black","James Pennebaker wired up men and women with voice recorders and found both sexes used about 16,000 words per day, so the girls-talk-more stereotype is simply false","Peter Glick and Susan Fiske found sexism worldwide takes two forms - hostile sexism, holding negative stereotypes of women, and benevolent sexism, holding positive but patronizing stereotypes that idealize women as needing protection","In the hurricane naming study, raters thought male-named hurricanes were more intense than female-named ones and said they'd be more likely to evacuate for Hurricane Christopher than for identically described Hurricane Christina","Oliver Wendell Holmes Jr. compared the mind of a bigot to the pupil of an eye: 'The more light you pour on it, the more it contracts', and as Allport put it, 'Defeated intellectually, prejudice lingers emotionally'"]}]},{"id":"sa_ch08a","title":"Chapter 8: Liking, Love, and Interpersonal Sensitivity (Part 1)","pages":"315-366","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Social Psychology | Attraction | Proximity","topic":"The Mere Exposure Effect: Familiarity Breeds Liking","visual_frame_description":"Participants view random Chinese characters flashed on a screen for 1 millisecond each. Unbeknownst to them, some characters appear 1 time, some 5 times, some 10 times, some 25 times. Later, participants rate the characters they saw most frequently as more pleasant, even though no character was seen long enough for conscious recognition.","target_channel":"Unconscious Familiarity | Liking Without Awareness","question":"How can people develop a preference for stimuli they never consciously perceived?","correct_cue":"Mere exposure effect (Zajonc 1968): repeated exposure to any stimulus increases liking, even without conscious awareness. The brain has a built-in preference for the familiar. The effect is strongest when exposure is subliminal - conscious recognition triggers boredom, but subconscious familiarity feels like intuition. This explains why we prefer our own face in the mirror (familiar orientation), but our friends prefer the reversed photo (their familiar orientation).","distractors":["Participants were guessing and the results are statistically random","Frequent exposure allows more time to analyze meaning and find positive associations","The effect only works for neutral stimuli, not complex social judgments about people"],"underlying_psychology":"Zajonc 1968 mere exposure studies. The effect is robust across cultures, stimuli (faces, words, shapes, sounds), and time frames. It operates without conscious awareness - subliminal exposures are more effective than supraliminal ones. The evolutionary logic: familiar stimuli are usually safe (no predator ate me last time). Novel stimuli require caution. The advertising implication: even disliked ads increase product preference through mere repetition. The interpersonal implication: the person you see every day in class or at work becomes more attractive simply through repeated exposure.","player_mission":"How can people develop a preference for stimuli they never consciously perceived?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["John Thibaut and Harold Kelley argued attraction is partly determined by our comparison level for alternatives - we calculate a person's value by comparing what they bring to us with what we might get from others","College students randomly assigned to dorm rooms named their next-door neighbors about half the time when asked who they liked best, showing proximity fuels friendship","James Brossard analyzed Philadelphia marriage records from the 1930s and found one in three marriages involved people who lived within five blocks, one in six on the same block, and only about one in five involved partners from different cities","The more similar a person seems in attitudes, opinions, and interests, the more you like them: 'Opposites may attract, but they don't stick'","We like people whose attitudes are similar to ours, and if we like someone for an irrelevant reason we assume they share our attitudes about politics, religion, and movies - causality runs in both directions","Hunter Gelbach found that making teachers and students aware of five similarities they shared increased mutual liking and respect, especially among minority students","In problem-solving groups, the participants considered most competent and having the best ideas tend not to be the ones best liked, because people who are too competent may seem unapproachable and make us look bad by comparison","The pratfall effect, demonstrated by Aronson, Willerman, and Floyd: the superior quiz-show candidate who clumsily spilled coffee was rated most attractive, while the average candidate who spilled it was rated least attractive","Abraham Tesser found we bask in the reflected glory of highly competent friends and relatives, but their attractiveness is diminished if their competence in areas that matter to us makes us feel inadequate by comparison","A 1966 analysis of more than 29,000 people found physical attractiveness matters equally to both sexes, contradicting the belief that looks matter more to men","Elaine Walster randomly matched incoming University of Minnesota students for a blind date and found the one determinant of whether they liked each other and repeated the date was physical attractiveness, not intelligence or personality scores","Gregory White found that among young UCLA couples, it was the similarity of the partners' physical attractiveness that determined whether they stayed together over many months"]},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Social Psychology | Attraction | Similarity vs Complementarity","topic":"The Similarity Attraction Debate: Do Opposites Really Attract?","dialogue":[{"speaker":"Alice","line":"I think the reason we work so well is the opposites attract thing. I am spontaneous and chaotic, he is structured and planned. We balance each other out."},{"speaker":"Bob","line":"But the research overwhelmingly says similarity attracts. Byrne 1971 found a linear relationship: the more similar attitudes, the greater the attraction. Rosenbaum 1986 added that dissimilarity repels. There is almost no evidence for complementarity as a primary attraction force."},{"speaker":"Commentator","line":"Bob is correct on the data. Hundreds of studies show that attitude similarity predicts attraction. The law of attraction from Byrne: attraction toward a person equals a function of the proportion of similar attitudes shared. Opposites attract is a cultural myth. What people call complementarity in successful relationships is usually similarity on core values with superficial differences in temperament - both value adventure but one handles logistics while the other generates ideas. Below the surface, the core values match."}],"psychological_breakdown":"Similarity-attraction effect: we like people who share our attitudes, values, and backgrounds. The effect holds across cultures and contexts. Dissimilarity repels even more strongly than similarity attracts. The reciprocity of liking also matters: we like people who like us - and we like people we think will like us (anticipatory liking creates self-fulfilling prophecy). The exception: when people need something we lack, but even then we prefer similar others for long-term committed relationships.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A dating lab with a wall of headshots; a misbehaved-child report slides in with an attractive face and gets excused while the same report with a plain face gets condemned; behind glass, an intercom hums as a woman's voice warms up for a man who believes she is gorgeous.","key_concepts":["John Thibaut and Harold Kelley argued attraction is partly determined by our comparison level for alternatives - we calculate a person's value by comparing what they bring to us with what we might get from others","College students randomly assigned to dorm rooms named their next-door neighbors about half the time when asked who they liked best, showing proximity fuels friendship","James Brossard analyzed Philadelphia marriage records from the 1930s and found one in three marriages involved people who lived within five blocks, one in six on the same block, and only about one in five involved partners from different cities","The more similar a person seems in attitudes, opinions, and interests, the more you like them: 'Opposites may attract, but they don't stick'","We like people whose attitudes are similar to ours, and if we like someone for an irrelevant reason we assume they share our attitudes about politics, religion, and movies - causality runs in both directions","Hunter Gelbach found that making teachers and students aware of five similarities they shared increased mutual liking and respect, especially among minority students","In problem-solving groups, the participants considered most competent and having the best ideas tend not to be the ones best liked, because people who are too competent may seem unapproachable and make us look bad by comparison","The pratfall effect, demonstrated by Aronson, Willerman, and Floyd: the superior quiz-show candidate who clumsily spilled coffee was rated most attractive, while the average candidate who spilled it was rated least attractive","Abraham Tesser found we bask in the reflected glory of highly competent friends and relatives, but their attractiveness is diminished if their competence in areas that matter to us makes us feel inadequate by comparison","A 1966 analysis of more than 29,000 people found physical attractiveness matters equally to both sexes, contradicting the belief that looks matter more to men","Elaine Walster randomly matched incoming University of Minnesota students for a blind date and found the one determinant of whether they liked each other and repeated the date was physical attractiveness, not intelligence or personality scores","Gregory White found that among young UCLA couples, it was the similarity of the partners' physical attractiveness that determined whether they stayed together over many months"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Social Psychology | Attraction | The Dutton and Araron Bridge Study","topic":"Misattribution of Arousal: Love on a Swaying Bridge","subject_baseline":"An attractive female experimenter stops male participants on either a safe low bridge or a terrifying high suspension bridge that sways and bounces over a 230-foot drop. She asks them to fill out a questionnaire and gives her phone number for participants to call if they want more information about the study.","interrogation_transcript":"Participants on the scary high bridge were significantly more likely to call the female experimenter, and the content of their TAT stories contained more sexual imagery. The men on the high bridge experienced physiological arousal (racing heart, sweaty palms) from the bridge fear, but misattributed it as attraction to the woman.","observed_nonverbal_log":["The high bridge induced genuine physiological fear arousal","Participants needed to explain their racing heart","The attractive woman was the most plausible available cause","Arousal from any source can amplify romantic attraction"],"question":"Why did men on the scary bridge show more sexual interest in the female experimenter than men on the safe bridge?","correct_diagnosis":"Misattribution of arousal (Schachter-Singer two-factor theory of emotion): emotion is the product of physiological arousal plus cognitive label. When the high bridge produced arousal, participants needed to label it. The woman was present - I must be excited by her, not scared of the bridge. The arousal from fear was mislabeled as attraction. This explains why dangerous dates (roller coasters, horror movies) can accelerate romantic bonding - the adrenaline from the activity gets misattributed to the person.","distractors":["Men on the high bridge had a death wish or were sensation seekers seeking more intense experiences","The high bridge took longer to cross, giving participants more time to find the woman attractive","The experimenter was more flirty with participants on the high bridge due to experimenter bias"],"diagnostic_breakdown":"Stage 1 - Arousal: Fear-inducing bridge creates physiological arousal. Stage 2 - Attribution Need: Brain seeks explanation for the arousal. Stage 3 - Available Cause: Attractive woman is the most salient possible cause. Stage 4 - Misattribution: I feel aroused, she is here, she must be attractive. This is not love but a labeling error. The practical application: if you want to accelerate attraction, share exciting activities. But be aware that the arousal is from the activity, not from the person.","player_mission":"Why did men on the scary bridge show more sexual interest in the female experimenter than men on the safe bridge?","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A bookkeeper's office where every interaction posts to a ledger with two columns - rewards given and costs paid - while a Franklin-style library book floats overhead and a speed-dating table glows with selectivity meters.","key_concepts":["John Thibaut and Harold Kelley argued attraction is partly determined by our comparison level for alternatives - we calculate a person's value by comparing what they bring to us with what we might get from others","College students randomly assigned to dorm rooms named their next-door neighbors about half the time when asked who they liked best, showing proximity fuels friendship","James Brossard analyzed Philadelphia marriage records from the 1930s and found one in three marriages involved people who lived within five blocks, one in six on the same block, and only about one in five involved partners from different cities","The more similar a person seems in attitudes, opinions, and interests, the more you like them: 'Opposites may attract, but they don't stick'","We like people whose attitudes are similar to ours, and if we like someone for an irrelevant reason we assume they share our attitudes about politics, religion, and movies - causality runs in both directions","Hunter Gelbach found that making teachers and students aware of five similarities they shared increased mutual liking and respect, especially among minority students","In problem-solving groups, the participants considered most competent and having the best ideas tend not to be the ones best liked, because people who are too competent may seem unapproachable and make us look bad by comparison","The pratfall effect, demonstrated by Aronson, Willerman, and Floyd: the superior quiz-show candidate who clumsily spilled coffee was rated most attractive, while the average candidate who spilled it was rated least attractive","Abraham Tesser found we bask in the reflected glory of highly competent friends and relatives, but their attractiveness is diminished if their competence in areas that matter to us makes us feel inadequate by comparison","A 1966 analysis of more than 29,000 people found physical attractiveness matters equally to both sexes, contradicting the belief that looks matter more to men","Elaine Walster randomly matched incoming University of Minnesota students for a blind date and found the one determinant of whether they liked each other and repeated the date was physical attractiveness, not intelligence or personality scores","Gregory White found that among young UCLA couples, it was the similarity of the partners' physical attractiveness that determined whether they stayed together over many months"]},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Social Psychology | Love | Passionate vs Companionate","topic":"The Two Flavors of Love: Passionate vs Companionate","stage_1_observation":"Newlyweds show intense passionate love. After 18-30 months, the passionate intensity fades. Some couples divorce. Others settle into a deeper, calmer bond. Berscheid and Walster distinguished passionate love (intense longing, physiological arousal, idealization) from companionate love (deep friendship, commitment, shared history).","stage_1_question":"If passionate love naturally fades, what determines whether couples transition successfully to companionate love or break apart?","stage_1_correct_answer":"Attributional style. Couples who attribute the fading passion to the relationship (we lost the spark) are at risk. Couples who attribute it to the natural course of relationships (this is normal after two years) accommodate the transition. Also critical: equity - people are most satisfied when the ratio of contributions to benefits is roughly equal for both partners. Inequity creates distress. Finally, self-disclosure - deep, reciprocal sharing of private thoughts and feelings builds intimacy that replaces the intensity of early passion.","stage_2_intervention":"A couple married 6 years comes to you. They describe losing the spark. They used to have passionate sex, talk for hours, feel obsessed. Now they feel like roommates. They ask: Did we marry the wrong person? What do you tell them?","stage_2_correct_answer":"First: Normalize the transition. Tell them the passionate phase biologically lasts 18-36 months. Its absence does not mean a failed marriage. Second: Assess whether they have developed the companionate substitutes - deep self-disclosure, equity, shared meaning, and commitment. Third: Prescribe behavioral interventions that increase positive interactions. Gottman found that happy couples maintain a 5:1 ratio of positive to negative interactions. Small daily acts of appreciation, curiosity about each others inner world, and shared novel experiences rebuild connection. The question is not whether the spark is gone but whether you have built a fire.","boss_battle_explanation":"Layer 1 - Passionate Phase: Intense, short-lived, driven by novelty and uncertainty. Layer 2 - Transition Crisis: Couples who expect passion to last forever are disappointed. Layer 3 - Companionate Phase: Deeper, slower, based on friendship and commitment. Layer 4 - Building Companionate Love: Self-disclosure, equity, shared positive activities, 5:1 positivity ratio. The myth that love should always feel like the first six months destroys many potentially good marriages.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A relationship ark sailing past two whirlpools labeled 'dive in too fast' and 'the high wears off'; the deck holds cards for destiny versus growth beliefs, secure-attachment anchors, a four-horsemen armor set, and two porcupines huddling at a tolerable distance in the snow.","key_concepts":["John Thibaut and Harold Kelley argued attraction is partly determined by our comparison level for alternatives - we calculate a person's value by comparing what they bring to us with what we might get from others","College students randomly assigned to dorm rooms named their next-door neighbors about half the time when asked who they liked best, showing proximity fuels friendship","James Brossard analyzed Philadelphia marriage records from the 1930s and found one in three marriages involved people who lived within five blocks, one in six on the same block, and only about one in five involved partners from different cities","The more similar a person seems in attitudes, opinions, and interests, the more you like them: 'Opposites may attract, but they don't stick'","We like people whose attitudes are similar to ours, and if we like someone for an irrelevant reason we assume they share our attitudes about politics, religion, and movies - causality runs in both directions","Hunter Gelbach found that making teachers and students aware of five similarities they shared increased mutual liking and respect, especially among minority students","In problem-solving groups, the participants considered most competent and having the best ideas tend not to be the ones best liked, because people who are too competent may seem unapproachable and make us look bad by comparison","The pratfall effect, demonstrated by Aronson, Willerman, and Floyd: the superior quiz-show candidate who clumsily spilled coffee was rated most attractive, while the average candidate who spilled it was rated least attractive","Abraham Tesser found we bask in the reflected glory of highly competent friends and relatives, but their attractiveness is diminished if their competence in areas that matter to us makes us feel inadequate by comparison","A 1966 analysis of more than 29,000 people found physical attractiveness matters equally to both sexes, contradicting the belief that looks matter more to men","Elaine Walster randomly matched incoming University of Minnesota students for a blind date and found the one determinant of whether they liked each other and repeated the date was physical attractiveness, not intelligence or personality scores","Gregory White found that among young UCLA couples, it was the similarity of the partners' physical attractiveness that determined whether they stayed together over many months"]}]},{"id":"sa_ch08b","title":"Chapter 8: Liking, Love, and Interpersonal Sensitivity (Part 2)","pages":"315-366","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Social Psychology | Attraction | Gain-Loss","topic":"The Gain-Loss Theory of Attraction: Why Criticism Then Praise Is Most Powerful","visual_frame_description":"Aronson and Linder 1965: Participants overheard a confederate evaluator making one of four patterns of evaluations about them: (1) consistently positive (always praised), (2) consistently negative (always criticized), (3) negative then positive (gain — started critical, became positive), (4) positive then negative (loss — started positive, became critical). The gain condition (criticism turning to praise) produced the highest liking. The loss condition (praise turning to criticism) produced the strongest disliking.","target_channel":"Gain Condition | Evaluative Sequencing","question":"Why does a person who criticizes you and then praises you inspire more liking than someone who praises you consistently?","correct_cue":"The gain effect: a shift from negative to positive evaluation creates a dramatic contrast. The initial criticism creates uncertainty and lowers expectations. When the praise comes, it is more impactful than constant praise because: (1) contrast — the shift is more noticeable than a constant state, (2) credibility — the praise seems more genuine because it was earned (the critic was not just being nice), (3) relief — the reduction of anxiety about the initial criticism is experienced as positive. The loss effect works in reverse: someone who shifts from praise to criticism is especially disliked because the initial praise raised expectations, making the criticism feel like a betrayal.","distractors":["The consistently positive person is liked most because constant affirmation is always the most rewarding","The gain effect works only for strangers — established relationships are not affected by evaluation sequencing","The effect is driven by memory — we remember the most recent evaluation and forget the earlier one"],"underlying_psychology":"The gain-loss theory applies the principles of reinforcement and contrast to interpersonal attraction. The gain condition works because the negative-then-positive sequence creates a more powerful reinforcer than constant positivity — the same way a paycheck after unemployment feels better than a constant salary. The practical implications: (1) first impressions matter less than the trajectory of impressions, (2) a person who starts cold and warms up is liked more than a consistently warm person, (3) a person who starts warm and cools is disliked the most. This is why the brooding hero who gradually opens up is a powerful narrative archetype.","player_mission":"Why does a person who criticizes you and then praises you inspire more liking than someone who praises you consistently?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["John Thibaut and Harold Kelley argued attraction is partly determined by our comparison level for alternatives - we calculate a person's value by comparing what they bring to us with what we might get from others","College students randomly assigned to dorm rooms named their next-door neighbors about half the time when asked who they liked best, showing proximity fuels friendship","James Brossard analyzed Philadelphia marriage records from the 1930s and found one in three marriages involved people who lived within five blocks, one in six on the same block, and only about one in five involved partners from different cities","The more similar a person seems in attitudes, opinions, and interests, the more you like them: 'Opposites may attract, but they don't stick'","We like people whose attitudes are similar to ours, and if we like someone for an irrelevant reason we assume they share our attitudes about politics, religion, and movies - causality runs in both directions","Hunter Gelbach found that making teachers and students aware of five similarities they shared increased mutual liking and respect, especially among minority students","In problem-solving groups, the participants considered most competent and having the best ideas tend not to be the ones best liked, because people who are too competent may seem unapproachable and make us look bad by comparison","The pratfall effect, demonstrated by Aronson, Willerman, and Floyd: the superior quiz-show candidate who clumsily spilled coffee was rated most attractive, while the average candidate who spilled it was rated least attractive","Abraham Tesser found we bask in the reflected glory of highly competent friends and relatives, but their attractiveness is diminished if their competence in areas that matter to us makes us feel inadequate by comparison","A 1966 analysis of more than 29,000 people found physical attractiveness matters equally to both sexes, contradicting the belief that looks matter more to men","Elaine Walster randomly matched incoming University of Minnesota students for a blind date and found the one determinant of whether they liked each other and repeated the date was physical attractiveness, not intelligence or personality scores","Gregory White found that among young UCLA couples, it was the similarity of the partners' physical attractiveness that determined whether they stayed together over many months"]},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Social Psychology | Attachment Theory | Relationships","topic":"Attachment Styles in Adult Relationships: The Internal Working Models of Love","dialogue":[{"speaker":"Person A","line":"My partner says I am too clingy. I need constant reassurance. When they do not text back quickly, I panic and think they are losing interest. I know it is irrational but I cannot stop."},{"speaker":"Person B","line":"I have the opposite problem. My partner says I am emotionally distant. I need a lot of space. When they want to get closer, I feel smothered and pull away. I do not understand why I sabotage good relationships."},{"speaker":"Psychologist","line":"These patterns likely reflect attachment styles formed in childhood. Attachment theory (Bowlby, Ainsworth, Hazan and Shaver) proposes that early interactions with caregivers create internal working models of relationships — expectations about whether others are responsive and whether we are worthy of care. Person A describes anxious attachment: fear of abandonment, need for closeness, hypervigilance to rejection cues. Person B describes avoidant attachment: fear of intimacy, need for independence, discomfort with closeness. The secure style — trust in others and comfort with both closeness and independence — is associated with healthier, more satisfying relationships. The good news: attachment styles can change through corrective emotional experiences in adult relationships."}],"psychological_breakdown":"Hazan and Shaver 1987 applied Ainsworths infant attachment categories to adult romantic love. Secure (about 50% of population): comfortable with intimacy, trusts partners, believes in lasting love. Anxious (about 20%): craves closeness, fears abandonment, obsessed with partner availability. Avoidant (about 30%): uncomfortable with closeness, values independence, doubts romantic love exists. These styles predict relationship satisfaction, communication patterns, conflict resolution, and even partner selection. The styles are moderately stable across the lifespan but can change through significant relationships or therapy.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A dating lab with a wall of headshots; a misbehaved-child report slides in with an attractive face and gets excused while the same report with a plain face gets condemned; behind glass, an intercom hums as a woman's voice warms up for a man who believes she is gorgeous.","key_concepts":["John Thibaut and Harold Kelley argued attraction is partly determined by our comparison level for alternatives - we calculate a person's value by comparing what they bring to us with what we might get from others","College students randomly assigned to dorm rooms named their next-door neighbors about half the time when asked who they liked best, showing proximity fuels friendship","James Brossard analyzed Philadelphia marriage records from the 1930s and found one in three marriages involved people who lived within five blocks, one in six on the same block, and only about one in five involved partners from different cities","The more similar a person seems in attitudes, opinions, and interests, the more you like them: 'Opposites may attract, but they don't stick'","We like people whose attitudes are similar to ours, and if we like someone for an irrelevant reason we assume they share our attitudes about politics, religion, and movies - causality runs in both directions","Hunter Gelbach found that making teachers and students aware of five similarities they shared increased mutual liking and respect, especially among minority students","In problem-solving groups, the participants considered most competent and having the best ideas tend not to be the ones best liked, because people who are too competent may seem unapproachable and make us look bad by comparison","The pratfall effect, demonstrated by Aronson, Willerman, and Floyd: the superior quiz-show candidate who clumsily spilled coffee was rated most attractive, while the average candidate who spilled it was rated least attractive","Abraham Tesser found we bask in the reflected glory of highly competent friends and relatives, but their attractiveness is diminished if their competence in areas that matter to us makes us feel inadequate by comparison","A 1966 analysis of more than 29,000 people found physical attractiveness matters equally to both sexes, contradicting the belief that looks matter more to men","Elaine Walster randomly matched incoming University of Minnesota students for a blind date and found the one determinant of whether they liked each other and repeated the date was physical attractiveness, not intelligence or personality scores","Gregory White found that among young UCLA couples, it was the similarity of the partners' physical attractiveness that determined whether they stayed together over many months"]}]},{"id":"sa_ch09a","title":"Chapter 9: Social Psychology as a Science (Part 1)","pages":"367-396","assets":[{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Research Methods | Social Psychology","topic":"Experimental vs Correlational Design: Causation vs Association","column_a_label":"True Experiment - Random assignment, manipulation of IV, control of extraneous variables. Can establish causation.","column_b_label":"Correlational Study - Measure existing variables as they naturally occur. Can establish association but not causation.","rows":[{"scenario":"Researchers randomly assign participants to watch violent or nonviolent TV, then measure aggression","correct_column":"A","reason":"Random assignment and manipulation allow causal inference that violent TV causes aggression"},{"scenario":"Researchers survey 10,000 teenagers about their TV viewing habits and their aggressive behavior","correct_column":"B","reason":"No manipulation or random assignment - could be that aggressive kids prefer violent TV rather than TV causing aggression"},{"scenario":"A school implements a bullying prevention program in some classrooms and not others, then compares outcomes","correct_column":"A","reason":"Manipulation of the treatment with a control group, allows evaluation of program effectiveness"},{"scenario":"Researchers find a negative correlation between ice cream sales and crime rates","correct_column":"B","reason":"Association only - a third variable (temperature) likely drives both"},{"scenario":"Researchers randomly assign jurors to see different types of evidence presentation and measure verdict decisions","correct_column":"A","reason":"Random assignment to different presentation conditions allows causal conclusions about presentation format effects"}],"explanation":"The fundamental challenge in social psychology: many important variables cannot be manipulated (race, gender, personality). Correlational studies can identify associations but the third-variable problem always lurks. The self-selection problem: people choose their environments, so the direction of causality is ambiguous. True experiments require random assignment and manipulation. Field experiments combine the control of lab experiments with real-world settings, offering the best of both worlds.","player_mission":"","difficulty_level":"Advanced","visual_frame_description":"A film director's storyboard wall where each experiment is a scene with its cover story on a clapperboard; a sign flashes 'Do not tell the actor what the play is about' while a monitoring room shows participants straining to look good.","key_concepts":["The scientific method is the best way we humans have of uncovering lawful relationships among things, whether chemicals, planets, or the antecedents of human prejudice or love","The steps of the scientific process are observation, a guess about why it happens, framing the guess as a testable hypothesis, and designing an experiment that will confirm or disconfirm the hypothesis","As Richard Feynman put it, 'It doesn't matter how beautiful the guess is or how smart the guesser is, or how famous the guesser is; if the experiment disagrees with the guess, then the guess is wrong'","There are no sacred truths in science - if a series of well-designed experiments fails to confirm a hypothesis, we give it up","Social psychologists perform experiments because people are often unaware of why they act in certain ways, so simply asking people to explain their behavior usually will not yield reliable results","When Kennedy's popularity rose after the Bay of Pigs fiasco, Aronson could not experiment on Kennedy himself but designed an experiment on the underlying phenomenon, testing whether a blunder makes a nearly perfect person more attractive","In social psychology we are not studying chemicals in a beaker but adults who have lived in a social world their entire lives, bringing their own ideas and theories about what causes behavior","Pavel Semonov distinguished science and art: as scientists we organize the unknown, as artists we reorganize the known to create something new, and a good social psychology experiment requires skills from both domains","The prison college program case shows scientific skepticism: the prisoners who volunteered for the program may have differed from nonvolunteers in motivation and ability, so the lower recidivism rate proves nothing about the program's effects","The New York State study found male inmates who completed college in prison had a recidivism rate 20 percent lower than average, but the comparison stacked the deck because the groups were not equivalent at the outset","As scientists we must face our biases and preferences, but the scientific method lets us separate the wheat from the chaff to determine which programs are worthy of our time, effort, and money","The science part of an experiment is easier to replicate than the art part, just as a chef's souffle recipe rarely survives the transfer to another kitchen"]},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Research Ethics | Social Psychology","topic":"The Deception Controversy: Is It Ethical to Mislead Participants?","subject_baseline":"Milgram told participants they were in a learning study about punishment and memory. In reality, he was studying obedience to authority. Participants were deceived about the purpose, the shocks, and the learner. Afterward, they were debriefed. Most said they were glad to have participated. A few reported lasting distress from realizing their capacity for destructive obedience.","interrogation_transcript":"Social psychology uses deception frequently - Milgram, Asch, Festinger and Carlsmith, the bridge study, the prison study. The rationale: if participants know the true purpose, demand characteristics would invalidate the data. The ethical critique: deception violates informed consent, may cause distress, and erodes public trust in psychology. The current standard: deception is permitted only when the research is important, no non-deceptive alternative exists, and participants are fully debriefed afterward with the right to withdraw their data.","observed_nonverbal_log":["Deception is used because awareness of the hypothesis changes behavior","Debriefing attempts to restore participants to their pre-experimental state","Some participants in Milgram reported lasting insight, not harm","Zimbardo prison study raised serious ethical concerns for inadequate debriefing"],"question":"Is deception in social psychology research ethically justified, and what safeguards prevent abuse?","correct_diagnosis":"The ethical framework balances potential benefits against participant costs. Deception is justified when: (1) the research question is important and cannot be answered otherwise, (2) the deception is minimal and does not involve highly sensitive topics, (3) participants are fully debriefed about the deception and why it was necessary, (4) participants can withdraw their data after debriefing. Critics argue deception violates the basic ethical principle of respect for persons. Many universities now have stricter IRB limits on deception. The field is moving toward more deception-free paradigms and imaginative alternatives.","distractors":["Deception is never justified because it always violates informed consent regardless of safeguards","Deception is always justified because the scientific value of the knowledge trumps participant discomfort","Milgram study was the only deceptive study in social psychology - the field now uses only non-deceptive methods"],"diagnostic_breakdown":"Stage 1 - Historical Practice: Deception was routine in classic studies without meaningful ethical oversight. Stage 2 - Ethical Evolution: Tuskegee and other scandals led to IRB systems and informed consent requirements. Stage 3 - Current Balance: Deception allowed but regulated with debriefing, right to withdraw, and IRB review. Stage 4 - Ongoing Debate: Some argue even regulated deception damages trust; others argue the insights (obedience, bystander effect) are too important to lose. The field is developing more deception-free paradigms.","player_mission":"Is deception in social psychology research ethically justified, and what safeguards prevent abuse?","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A countryside of observations: a living room where violent TV hours and playground punches rise together, a prison classroom with a recidivism chart, a nursery where babies mirror tongues, and a wedding where happiness and intelligence swirl in a fog of possible causes.","key_concepts":["The scientific method is the best way we humans have of uncovering lawful relationships among things, whether chemicals, planets, or the antecedents of human prejudice or love","The steps of the scientific process are observation, a guess about why it happens, framing the guess as a testable hypothesis, and designing an experiment that will confirm or disconfirm the hypothesis","As Richard Feynman put it, 'It doesn't matter how beautiful the guess is or how smart the guesser is, or how famous the guesser is; if the experiment disagrees with the guess, then the guess is wrong'","There are no sacred truths in science - if a series of well-designed experiments fails to confirm a hypothesis, we give it up","Social psychologists perform experiments because people are often unaware of why they act in certain ways, so simply asking people to explain their behavior usually will not yield reliable results","When Kennedy's popularity rose after the Bay of Pigs fiasco, Aronson could not experiment on Kennedy himself but designed an experiment on the underlying phenomenon, testing whether a blunder makes a nearly perfect person more attractive","In social psychology we are not studying chemicals in a beaker but adults who have lived in a social world their entire lives, bringing their own ideas and theories about what causes behavior","Pavel Semonov distinguished science and art: as scientists we organize the unknown, as artists we reorganize the known to create something new, and a good social psychology experiment requires skills from both domains","The prison college program case shows scientific skepticism: the prisoners who volunteered for the program may have differed from nonvolunteers in motivation and ability, so the lower recidivism rate proves nothing about the program's effects","The New York State study found male inmates who completed college in prison had a recidivism rate 20 percent lower than average, but the comparison stacked the deck because the groups were not equivalent at the outset","As scientists we must face our biases and preferences, but the scientific method lets us separate the wheat from the chaff to determine which programs are worthy of our time, effort, and money","The science part of an experiment is easier to replicate than the art part, just as a chef's souffle recipe rarely survives the transfer to another kitchen"]},{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Research Methods | Bias Detection","topic":"Experimenter Expectancy Effects: The Self-Fulfilling Prophecy in Research","visual_frame_description":"Rosenthal and Jacobson 1968: Elementary school teachers were told that certain students were academic bloomers who would show dramatic intellectual growth (actually randomly selected). At the end of the year, those students showed significantly greater IQ gains than non-bloomers. The teachers expectations created a self-fulfilling prophecy.","target_channel":"Expectancy Contamination | Researcher Bias","question":"How can a researchers expectations unconsciously shape the behavior they are studying?","correct_cue":"Experimenter expectancy effects operate through multiple subtle channels: (1) differential treatment - teachers gave bloomers more attention, more challenging material, more praise; (2) selective reinforcement - teachers unconsciously rewarded bloomer behaviors; (3) confirmation bias - teachers noticed and remembered bloomer successes more than failures. The solution: double-blind procedures where neither participant nor experimenter knows the hypothesis or condition assignment. This is why double-blind drug trials are the gold standard.","distractors":["Teachers consciously favored the bloomers because they wanted to prove the researchers correct","The bloomer effect only occurs in children and not in adult experimental participants","Expectancy effects are small and do not meaningfully influence most research findings"],"underlying_psychology":"Rosenthal 1966 identified four factors through which expectations influence outcomes: climate (warmer emotional environment for expected performers), input (more material taught), output (more opportunities to respond), and feedback (more praise, more specific feedback). The Pygmalion effect: higher expectations lead to better performance. The Golem effect: lower expectations lead to worse performance. In research contexts, experimenters may unconsciously code data differently, interact differently, or interpret results differently based on their hypotheses. This is why double-blind procedures are essential.","player_mission":"How can a researchers expectations unconsciously shape the behavior they are studying?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["The scientific method is the best way we humans have of uncovering lawful relationships among things, whether chemicals, planets, or the antecedents of human prejudice or love","The steps of the scientific process are observation, a guess about why it happens, framing the guess as a testable hypothesis, and designing an experiment that will confirm or disconfirm the hypothesis","As Richard Feynman put it, 'It doesn't matter how beautiful the guess is or how smart the guesser is, or how famous the guesser is; if the experiment disagrees with the guess, then the guess is wrong'","There are no sacred truths in science - if a series of well-designed experiments fails to confirm a hypothesis, we give it up","Social psychologists perform experiments because people are often unaware of why they act in certain ways, so simply asking people to explain their behavior usually will not yield reliable results","When Kennedy's popularity rose after the Bay of Pigs fiasco, Aronson could not experiment on Kennedy himself but designed an experiment on the underlying phenomenon, testing whether a blunder makes a nearly perfect person more attractive","In social psychology we are not studying chemicals in a beaker but adults who have lived in a social world their entire lives, bringing their own ideas and theories about what causes behavior","Pavel Semonov distinguished science and art: as scientists we organize the unknown, as artists we reorganize the known to create something new, and a good social psychology experiment requires skills from both domains","The prison college program case shows scientific skepticism: the prisoners who volunteered for the program may have differed from nonvolunteers in motivation and ability, so the lower recidivism rate proves nothing about the program's effects","The New York State study found male inmates who completed college in prison had a recidivism rate 20 percent lower than average, but the comparison stacked the deck because the groups were not equivalent at the outset","As scientists we must face our biases and preferences, but the scientific method lets us separate the wheat from the chaff to determine which programs are worthy of our time, effort, and money","The science part of an experiment is easier to replicate than the art part, just as a chef's souffle recipe rarely survives the transfer to another kitchen"]}]},{"id":"sa_ch09b","title":"Chapter 9: Social Psychology as a Science (Part 2)","pages":"367-396","assets":[{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Social Psychology | Metascience | Replication","topic":"The Replication Crisis: When Classic Findings Failed to Reappear","subject_baseline":"The Reproducibility Project 2015 attempted to replicate 100 psychology studies published in top journals. Only 36% of replications produced statistically significant results (compared to 97% of original studies). The average effect size in replications was half that of original studies. Classic findings — social priming (elderly words → slow walking), ego depletion (willpower as muscle), power posing (expansive posture → testosterone change) — failed to replicate.","interrogation_transcript":"The replication crisis revealed systemic problems: (1) Publication bias — journals publish positive results, not null results. The file drawer problem means dozens of failed replications are never published. (2) Questionable research practices (QRPs) — p-hacking (running analyses until p < .05), optional stopping (collecting data until significant), selective reporting (reporting only the dependent measures that worked). (3) Low statistical power — many studies had small sample sizes that could only detect large effects, but used them to draw conclusions about small, noisy effects. (4) Researcher degrees of freedom — many defensible analytic choices exist, and choosing the one that produces significance inflates false positives.","observed_nonverbal_log":["Only 36 of 100 high-profile studies replicated","Strong effects in original studies were often much weaker in replications","Questionable research practices were widespread","The incentive structure rewarded interesting findings over rigorous ones"],"question":"If a substantial portion of published social psychology findings are not robust, how can the field claim to have discovered anything reliable?","correct_diagnosis":"The replication crisis is a crisis of confidence, not a complete collapse. Many robust findings do replicate: the fundamental attribution error, cognitive dissonance, conformity (Asch), bystander intervention, stereotype threat, and other core effects have been replicated many times. The crisis affected mostly newer, flashier findings — especially social priming and embodied cognition — that may have been too good to be true. The field is responding with: (1) pre-registration of studies and analysis plans, (2) larger sample sizes and higher statistical power, (3) open data and materials, (4) registered replication reports, and (5) a cultural shift valuing rigor over novelty. The crisis has made social psychology more scientific, not less.","distractors":["The entire field is invalid — no social psychology finding can be trusted","The replication crisis was exaggerated by critics who failed to replicate because they used different methods","Psychology is fundamentally different from the natural sciences and should not be expected to produce replicable findings"],"diagnostic_breakdown":"Stage 1 - Incentive Structure: Publish or perish rewards novelty, not replication. Stage 2 - Publication Bias: Null results go in the file drawer. Stage 3 - Flexing: Researcher degrees of freedom allow p-hacking. Stage 4 - False Positive: A finding that is actually type I error gets published. Stage 5 - Replication Attempt: A team fails to replicate. Stage 6 - Defense: Original authors claim the replications were not exact. Stage 7 - Resolution: The field must reform its practices. The replication crisis is not a death blow to social psychology — it is a painful but necessary correction that will produce a more reliable science.","player_mission":"If a substantial portion of published social psychology findings are not robust, how can the field claim to have discovered anything reliable?","difficulty_level":"Intermediate","visual_frame_description":"A countryside of observations: a living room where violent TV hours and playground punches rise together, a prison classroom with a recidivism chart, a nursery where babies mirror tongues, and a wedding where happiness and intelligence swirl in a fog of possible causes.","key_concepts":["The scientific method is the best way we humans have of uncovering lawful relationships among things, whether chemicals, planets, or the antecedents of human prejudice or love","The steps of the scientific process are observation, a guess about why it happens, framing the guess as a testable hypothesis, and designing an experiment that will confirm or disconfirm the hypothesis","As Richard Feynman put it, 'It doesn't matter how beautiful the guess is or how smart the guesser is, or how famous the guesser is; if the experiment disagrees with the guess, then the guess is wrong'","There are no sacred truths in science - if a series of well-designed experiments fails to confirm a hypothesis, we give it up","Social psychologists perform experiments because people are often unaware of why they act in certain ways, so simply asking people to explain their behavior usually will not yield reliable results","When Kennedy's popularity rose after the Bay of Pigs fiasco, Aronson could not experiment on Kennedy himself but designed an experiment on the underlying phenomenon, testing whether a blunder makes a nearly perfect person more attractive","In social psychology we are not studying chemicals in a beaker but adults who have lived in a social world their entire lives, bringing their own ideas and theories about what causes behavior","Pavel Semonov distinguished science and art: as scientists we organize the unknown, as artists we reorganize the known to create something new, and a good social psychology experiment requires skills from both domains","The prison college program case shows scientific skepticism: the prisoners who volunteered for the program may have differed from nonvolunteers in motivation and ability, so the lower recidivism rate proves nothing about the program's effects","The New York State study found male inmates who completed college in prison had a recidivism rate 20 percent lower than average, but the comparison stacked the deck because the groups were not equivalent at the outset","As scientists we must face our biases and preferences, but the scientific method lets us separate the wheat from the chaff to determine which programs are worthy of our time, effort, and money","The science part of an experiment is easier to replicate than the art part, just as a chef's souffle recipe rarely survives the transfer to another kitchen"]},{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Social Psychology | Metascience | WEIRD","topic":"The WEIRD Problem: Are We Studying All of Humanity or Just American College Students?","visual_frame_description":"96% of psychological participants come from Western, Educated, Industrialized, Rich, Democratic (WEIRD) societies — but these societies represent only 12% of the world population. On many psychological measures, WEIRD people are outliers: more individualistic, less conforming, more analytic in thinking, more focused on harm-based morality. Using WEIRD samples to draw universal conclusions about human nature is like studying penguins and concluding that all birds cannot fly.","target_channel":"Sampling Bias | Generalizability Crisis","question":"Why does the over-reliance on WEIRD samples threaten the validity of social psychology claims about universal human nature?","correct_cue":"WEIRD populations are not representative samples of humanity — they are outliers on many dimensions relevant to social psychology. Henrich, Heine, and Norenzayan 2010 showed that WEIRD people are the most individualistic, analytical, and non-conforming people on the planet. Fundamental findings like the Muller-Lyer illusion, the Ultimatum Game responses, and moral reasoning patterns show substantial variation across cultures. If a finding is based entirely on American college students, we cannot know whether it reflects human nature or American college student nature. The field is increasingly recognizing the need for cross-cultural replication.","distractors":["Psychology studies basic cognitive processes that are universal across all human populations","American college students are more diverse than they were 50 years ago, solving the generalizability problem","Cross-cultural studies show that most social psychology findings replicate across WEIRD countries, proving universality"],"underlying_psychology":"The WEIRD problem is not just a methodological footnote — it has substantive implications. The standard story of moral development (Kohlberg) was based on WEIRD samples and claimed that justice-focused reasoning is the highest moral stage. Cross-cultural research shows that many cultures prioritize loyalty, community, and sanctity over justice. The standard model of the self (independent, autonomous) is WEIRD-specific; most cultures have more interdependent self-concepts. The replication crisis and the WEIRD problem together forced a rethinking of what psychology actually knows. The field is slowly becoming more diverse, collaborative, and humble about universal claims.","player_mission":"Why does the over-reliance on WEIRD samples threaten the validity of social psychology claims about universal human nature?","difficulty_level":"Beginner","key_concepts":["The scientific method is the best way we humans have of uncovering lawful relationships among things, whether chemicals, planets, or the antecedents of human prejudice or love","The steps of the scientific process are observation, a guess about why it happens, framing the guess as a testable hypothesis, and designing an experiment that will confirm or disconfirm the hypothesis","As Richard Feynman put it, 'It doesn't matter how beautiful the guess is or how smart the guesser is, or how famous the guesser is; if the experiment disagrees with the guess, then the guess is wrong'","There are no sacred truths in science - if a series of well-designed experiments fails to confirm a hypothesis, we give it up","Social psychologists perform experiments because people are often unaware of why they act in certain ways, so simply asking people to explain their behavior usually will not yield reliable results","When Kennedy's popularity rose after the Bay of Pigs fiasco, Aronson could not experiment on Kennedy himself but designed an experiment on the underlying phenomenon, testing whether a blunder makes a nearly perfect person more attractive","In social psychology we are not studying chemicals in a beaker but adults who have lived in a social world their entire lives, bringing their own ideas and theories about what causes behavior","Pavel Semonov distinguished science and art: as scientists we organize the unknown, as artists we reorganize the known to create something new, and a good social psychology experiment requires skills from both domains","The prison college program case shows scientific skepticism: the prisoners who volunteered for the program may have differed from nonvolunteers in motivation and ability, so the lower recidivism rate proves nothing about the program's effects","The New York State study found male inmates who completed college in prison had a recidivism rate 20 percent lower than average, but the comparison stacked the deck because the groups were not equivalent at the outset","As scientists we must face our biases and preferences, but the scientific method lets us separate the wheat from the chaff to determine which programs are worthy of our time, effort, and money","The science part of an experiment is easier to replicate than the art part, just as a chef's souffle recipe rarely survives the transfer to another kitchen"]}]},{"id":"sa01","title":"Chapter 1: What Is Social Psychology?","pages":"19-31","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Cue Scrubber: The Dispositional Blind Spot Scanner","visual_frame_description":"A wall of frozen vignettes hangs in a dark lab, each scene ringed by two glowing buttons labeled DISPOSITION and SITUATION, while a truth-meter swings whenever a correct classification snaps the frame into focus.","player_mission":"Scan ten vignettes from Chapter 1 - Sam at the senatorial debate, Steve's risky driving, Kaya's math hatred, Edgar Welch at Comet Ping Pong - and classify each explanation as dispositional or situational. Name the situation pressing on each person and decide whether the 'crazy' label reveals the cause or just our need to feel in control of events. Then flag every prediction that reeks of the hindsight bias.","key_concepts":["Social psychology is the scientific study of the influence of the real, imagined, or implied presence of others upon our thoughts, emotions, beliefs, and behavior, and of how we influence others","Aristotle was the first serious thinker to call our species 'the social animal'","As diverse as the chapter's opening examples seem, they all contain a common factor: social influence","The hindsight bias is our tendency to overestimate our powers of prediction once we know the outcome of a given event","Social psychological findings seem far more obvious to students who have been told what happened in a study than to students asked to predict what will happen","Aronson's first law states that people who do crazy things are not necessarily crazy","The dispositional view assumes that people who do crazy things have a personality disposition to be crazy, and that people who do stupid things must be stupid","Dispositionalism is often a mistake and an oversimplification, and we pay a price for it by feeling smug about our own invulnerability to pressures","The dispositional view moves the focus off improving situations and onto a narrow approach to fixing people","Most people, particularly those from Western cultures, assign personality traits like 'psychotic' or 'evil' spontaneously, without intention or conscious awareness","Some situations can cause a surprisingly large proportion of 'normal' adults to behave in unexpected, unappetizing, and sometimes abnormal ways","Helping us appreciate the situational view of human behavior is the central contribution of social psychology"],"difficulty_level":"Beginner"},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Dialogue: The Senator's Sincerity Cross-Examination","visual_frame_description":"A dorm lounge glows blue under a television screen; Sam sits mid-center while four silhouettes lean in, their unanimous verdict floating in a speech bubble that slowly drains Sam's orange 'sincere' meter toward gray.","player_mission":"Take the chair as Sam, watching a senatorial candidate you think is sincere while four acquaintances denounce her as a hypocrite. Decide whether to agree aloud, stay silent, or hold your ground, then switch roles and play the friends pressing for unanimity. Trace what social influence does to a stated judgment when private doubt collides with public pressure.","key_concepts":["Sam was favorably impressed by the female candidate, but when all his acquaintances agreed she was a hypocrite, he mumbled that she didn't come across as sincere as he would have hoped","Steve drives cautiously when alone in the car but hits the accelerator through yellow lights whenever two or three of his buddies are with him","Kaya went from a confident, excited student of mathematics to someone who dreaded it in a single year","Charlie ignored the vivacious woman he had been daydreaming about and began an earnest conversation with her less appealing companion","Rejection is among the most painful experiences for human beings, and it can cause all kinds of self-defeating and destructive behaviors, from overeating to violence","George Woods, Aronson's close African American friend in the 1940s, felt inferior partly because radio and television portrayed black adults as naive children","Everyone is an amateur social psychologist, but the casual tests amateurs run lack the rigor and impartiality of careful scientific investigation","Conventional wisdom is usually derived from shrewd observations that have stood the test of time, but sometimes it leads us astray","People who are threatened with mild punishment develop a dislike for the forbidden activity, while people who are severely threatened are, if anything, even more drawn to it","We tend to like someone who says nice things about us, but we like that person even more if some of the overheard remarks are anything but nice","Professional social psychologists do not need to wait for things to happen; they can conduct experiments in which everything is held constant except the factors being investigated","People are prone to explain unpleasant behavior by assigning personality traits to the perpetrator, such as 'psychotic,' 'sadistic,' or 'evil'"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Audit: The Monday Morning Quarterback Log","visual_frame_description":"A sports-broadcast studio desk buried in ledger sheets; each audited prediction gets stamped in red reading HINDSIGHT 20-20 or in green reading BEFORE THE FACT as a screen reveals the experiment outcomes.","player_mission":"Spend a session auditing twelve predictions about social life - who will obey, who will help, who will cheat - and mark each one for hindsight bias after you learn the outcome. Record whether the 'obvious' verdict existed before the fact or only after, and apply the chapter's distinction between amateur observation and controlled experiment to every entry.","key_concepts":["When you read research results, you may think 'That's obvious,' but you are likely acting on the hindsight bias","It is easy to be a Monday morning quarterback because, as we say, 'Hindsight is twenty-twenty'","Numerous studies have demonstrated the hindsight bias, from predicting election outcomes to predicting how a given social psychology experiment will turn out","It seems reasonable that people threatened with punishment for forbidden behavior should stop, but when tested empirically that assumption turns out to be dead wrong","Severe punishment does not make children stop wanting the forbidden activity; it is those mildly threatened who develop a dislike for it","Nearly all the data presented in this book are based upon experimental evidence","Labels like 'crazy' are often just name-calling; they do little to increase our understanding of human behavior","It is much more useful to try to understand the nature of the situation, the often complex processes operating to produce the behavior we observe","Before these people moved to Guyana, none of their neighbors, friends, or relatives would have thought of them as anything other than ordinary, sensible people","The Jonestown massacre is a reminder of the often powerful yet frequently hidden role of social influence in determining what human beings think, feel, and do","Aronson is not saying that psychosis doesn't exist; some personality traits and mental illnesses do affect people's behavior","Nor is Aronson saying all people are the same; what he is saying is that some situations cause large proportions of normal adults to behave abnormally"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Discrimination Matrix: The Disposition-Situation Grid","visual_frame_description":"A giant coordinate grid is projected on a warehouse wall; each vignette card flies to its cell, where a personality label fades out and a situational force diagram glows in its place.","player_mission":"Drop twelve Chapter 1 behaviors into a two-axis grid, dispositional explanation against situational explanation, from the Wheaties girl and the Montana shopkeeper to the ISIS recruit and the shooters at Columbine. For each cell, write the personality label people reach for and the situational force Aronson points to instead, then mark which explanation changes what we would do about the problem.","key_concepts":["The Montana shopkeeper was not born with an unflattering stereotype of Muslim people in his head; somebody, somehow, put it there","The 10-year-old girl avidly consumed two bowls of Wheaties each morning because Michael Phelps was pictured on the box","Thousands of apparently ordinary young men and women from Western countries have left their homes to fight and die in Syria and Iraq as Islamic jihadists","Understanding people's social motivations can be used for good, for evil, for winning reality TV competitions, for anything in which social influence matters","Douglas McCain was remembered as a 'goofball' and a 'really good person' before he joined ISIS and died waging war against his own country","The dispositional view is appealing because it helps us mentally separate those bad people who do bad things from the rest of us 'nice people'","This belief may comfort us because, as nice people, we would never do such a thing","Zimmerman saw a possibly armed-and-dangerous criminal in Trayvon Martin, not a normal high school student walking home with iced tea and a bag of Skittles","George Wood's grandchildren live in a different world than he did, but racial prejudice continues, from 'shopping while black' to 'driving while black'","The presence and example of other people, in the flesh or in movies like Schindler's List, can inspire positive emotions and generosity","Joshua, moved by Schindler's List, gave all the bills in his pocket to a panhandler, contrary to his habit of ignoring people who ask for handouts","Social psychology tackles disturbing questions from racism to the success of terrorist groups in recruiting young people"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Boss Battle: The Jonestown Verdict Chamber","visual_frame_description":"A courtroom materializes in the jungle clearing of Jonestown; the judge's bench glows with the words ARONSON'S FIRST LAW, and each time you offer a dispositional label the prosecutor's gavel strikes a flashing red stamp reading NAME-CALLING.","player_mission":"Face the final boss of Chapter 1: the Jonestown massacre. Decide whether the 613 parents were crazy, evil, or the product of a lethal situation, and defend your verdict against a prosecutor who keeps demanding personality labels. Use Aronson's first law, the disposition-situation distinction, and the hindsight bias to reach a verdict that explains behavior without name-calling.","key_concepts":["The Reverend Jim Jones, a charismatic leader of the People's Temple in San Francisco, convinced his followers to move to Guyana to establish a humanistic paradise","When the group's existence was threatened by a congressional investigation in 1978, Jones asked his followers to perform a 'revolutionary act': killing their children and then themselves","Vats of poison were prepared, and amid only scattered shouts of protest, mothers and fathers administered the fatal mixture to their infants, drank it themselves, and laid down to die","When one person kills their own child, it is reasonable to ask if they are mentally ill","When 613 parents murder their children in one place, we are forced to look beyond mental conditions and personality traits and consider the nature of the situation","People who do crazy things are not necessarily crazy, and understanding the situation is more useful than name-calling","Edgar Welch fired three shots in Comet Ping Pong on December 4, 2016, believing a conspiracy theory; he later said he regretted what he did but continued to believe the theory","The owner of Comet Ping Pong said the fabricated conspiracy theory put the restaurant under constant assault","People cling to 'insane' conspiracy theories despite solid evidence to the contrary, a tendency examined throughout this book","Dispositionalism can make us smug about our own invulnerability to pressures that could induce us to behave stupidly, crazily, or cruelly","It is of paramount importance to understand what is going on in situations that can produce unpleasant or destructive behavior","The examples that opened this chapter all illustrate social psychological situations containing a common factor: social influence"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"}]},{"id":"sa02","title":"Chapter 2: Social Cognition","pages":"31-75","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Cue Scrubber: The Heuristic Detection Lab","visual_frame_description":"A busy convenience store rendered as a holographic lab; each product and decision freezes under a scanning beam that tags it with REPRESENTATIVENESS, AVAILABILITY, or AFFECT in pulsing neon.","player_mission":"Scan twelve everyday decisions - the wine list, the cereal box, the shark-attack fear, the easy-to-read font - and name the heuristic steering each one: representativeness, availability, or affect. Say which shortcut misfires and which condition (no time, overload, emotion) makes the mind fall back on it, then decide whether to override the intuition.","key_concepts":["Social cognition is the study of how people come to believe what they do and why these processes so frequently produce errors","Much of the time we use reason, facts, and critical analysis not to form our opinions but to confirm what we already see, feel, or believe","Most of us are cognitive misers who seek ways to conserve cognitive energy and simplify complexity, taking shortcuts and using rules of thumb","When we focus on surface similarities to make inferences, we are using the representativeness heuristic, so the expensive wine is assumed to be better","Consumer Reports found that young rats thrived on a diet of Lucky Charms, whereas a diet of 100 Percent Natural Granola actually stunted their growth","The availability heuristic predicts the likelihood of an event based on how easy it is to bring examples to mind","The overwhelming majority of people wrongly believe more Americans die from shark attacks and fires than from falling airplane parts and drowning","People who consume crime dramas and television news tend to vastly overestimate the crime rate, and the more they watch, the more fearful they become","Statements that rhyme or roll off the tongue are judged to be truer than statements that say the same thing less felicitously","The affect heuristic taps our feelings to shape evaluations, so a bad mood makes you likely to evaluate a job candidate more negatively","The mad cow study showed beef consumption fell when the alarming label was used but stayed the same when the technical disease names were used","We are more likely to rely on heuristics when we don't have time, are overloaded with information, the issue is unimportant, we lack knowledge, or emotions get in the way"],"difficulty_level":"Beginner"},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Dialogue: The Primed Donald Interview","visual_frame_description":"A split-screen lab: on one side a memory wall scrolls trait lists in gold, on the other a man named Donald freezes mid-skydive while two glowing verdicts, 'adventurous' and 'reckless,' hover over his head.","player_mission":"Play the student who must describe Donald after being primed with positive or negative trait lists, and choose between 'adventurous' and 'reckless' for every ambiguous action. Then switch to the experimenter's seat, pick the priming words for the next participant, and predict how a racial prime biases the judgment of a teen accused of a crime.","key_concepts":["Schemas are mental models of the world; they can be stereotypes, categories, expectations, attitudes, and mindsets","Schemas can be activated through priming, the use of subtle cues that direct our thinking","In the Higgins, Rholes, and Jones study, students primed with negative traits characterized the fictitious Donald in negative terms and saw him as less likeable","The paragraph about Donald was ambiguous, describing acts that could be interpreted as either adventurous or reckless, self-confident or conceited","Police and juvenile probation officers primed with words related to black people judged the teen as older, more culpable, and more likely to get into trouble","Simply activating a racial schema set these professionals up to be biased in regarding the teenager as a troublemaker","The mass media may not be successful much of the time in telling people what to think, but it is stunningly successful in telling them what to think about","As Anais Nin put it, 'We don't see things as they are; we see them as we are'","The confirmation bias makes us notice, remember, and accept information that confirms what we already believe","In Snyder and Swann's experiment, students who expected an extrovert asked questions like 'What do you do to liven up a party?'","Whatever the respondent answers, the questioners' hypothesis about the person is likely to be confirmed","The confirmation bias helps explain why people cling tenaciously to debunked beliefs"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Audit: The Bias Blind Spot Expense Ledger","visual_frame_description":"A banker's ledger floats above a crowded quad where every student wears a glowing spotlight; each audit entry the player files stamps the ledger with the bias's name in a different colored ink.","player_mission":"Audit a week of your own judgments and log every bias you catch: the spotlight effect worries, the Barnum effect flattery you accepted, the loss you mourned more than the gain you celebrated. For each entry, note whether you believed the bias applied to everyone else but you, the bias blind spot, and classify the reasoning as automatic or controlled.","key_concepts":["The bias blind spot is the belief that we are more objective and less biased than most other people; we are biased to think we aren't biased","Naive realism is the propensity to believe that our subjective interpretation of reality is reality","The egocentric bias makes people remember new information better when they can apply it to themselves than when it only affects other people","Thomas Gilovich and his associates named the spotlight effect: people feel as though the social spotlight shines more brightly on them than it actually does","In the Barry Manilow T-shirt experiment, participants imagined about half of their peers noticed the shirt, but in reality only about 20 percent had even noticed it","The cloak of invisibility illusion makes us feel that we notice and observe others more than they notice and observe us","The Barnum Effect refers to the fact that when people are given vague, all-purpose descriptions of themselves, they usually say 'Incredible! That's me exactly!'","The Barnum Effect helps explain why so many people mistakenly believe in the accuracy of astrology, fortune telling, and some pop-psych personality tests","The negativity bias makes us quicker to find angry faces in a crowd than smiling ones, and bad news is shared more readily than good news","Roy Baumeister and his colleagues found that negative events typically have more power than positive ones; it is evolutionarily adaptive for bad to be stronger than good","The distress people feel when they lose money tends to be greater than the joy they feel gaining the same amount, a phenomenon known as loss aversion","In the home-insulation field experiment, homeowners told how much money they were losing every day were twice as likely to insulate as those told how much they could save"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Discrimination Matrix: The Attribution Choice Grid","visual_frame_description":"A courtroom-style grid stretches across the floor; each behavior card drops into either the PERSONALITY column glowing red or the SITUATION column glowing blue, and two policy maps unfurl beneath the winning explanations.","player_mission":"Sort a dozen explanations of human behavior - Margaux's kiss, the failing student, the smart questioner and the dim contestant - into dispositional and situational cells, then explain what the fundamental attribution error costs us in each case. Mark which policies follow from each attribution, from more prisons to better schools, and judge who the 'bloomers' really were.","key_concepts":["The fundamental attribution error is the tendency to overestimate the importance of personality or dispositional factors relative to situational or environmental influences","When explaining why a classmate has stopped studying, we reach for 'She's gotten lazy' rather than an external problem such as a parent's illness","In the quiz show experiment by Ross, Amabile, and Steinmetz, observers felt the questioners were far more knowledgeable than the contestants","Even though the observers knew the participants had been randomly assigned to their roles, they failed to acknowledge the impact of the roles on their judgments","A dispositional attribution about a thoughtless partner ('We need to break up') versus a situational one ('We need a vacation') leads to very different relationship happiness","Attributing crime to an inherently evil criminal leads us to support policies of spending more money on prisons and doling out longer, crueler sentences","Perceiving the causes of crime as largely unemployment, poor role models, and illiteracy leads to increased spending for better schools and tax credits for poor areas","People with a pessimistic explanatory style think the cause of their troubles permeates their lives, can't be changed, and will haunt them forever","The self-fulfilling prophecy occurs when we act on our initial attribution of our behavior and then behave in a way to confirm it","In Rosenthal and Jacobson's study, teachers were told that randomly chosen students were 'bloomers' on the verge of significant intellectual gains","At the end of the year, the children labeled 'bloomers' made significantly larger IQ gains than the children not labeled bloomers","The bloomer effect was primarily found in first and second grades, and for minority students the impact of teacher expectations was larger and spanned more grades"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Boss Battle: The Memory Reconstruction Trial","visual_frame_description":"A courtroom screen replays the accident film in flickering frames; every question the lawyers ask re-renders the cars' speed and the glass on the asphalt, and the witness's confidence meter fills even as the memory meter drains.","player_mission":"Defend or attack a witness's memory of a multiple-car accident in a courtroom where the prosecution uses 'smashed' and the defense uses 'hit.' Rule on whether broken glass existed at the scene, decide whether a planted shopping-mall memory counts as truth, and render a verdict on confidence as a guide to authenticity.","key_concepts":["Remembering is a reconstructive process; our memories are most strongly influenced by what we are thinking about those events in the present","In Loftus's experiment, those asked how fast the cars were going when they 'smashed into each other' estimated significantly higher speeds than those asked when they 'hit' each other","A week after the film, those asked about smashing cars were more likely to state, erroneously, that there was broken glass at the accident scene","Students asked whether the 'blue car that drove past the accident' had a ski rack were more likely to claim incorrectly that they had seen a blue car","A simple word change in the question had altered their memories","We construct our autobiographical memories to fit the picture we have of ourselves, aided by the confirmation bias","When people have a narrative of their childhoods as unhappy, events that violate that picture are more difficult to recall than events that support it","Loftus showed it is possible, indeed relatively easy, to plant false memories of childhood experiences by having a close relative talk about these events as fact","Within a few days of hearing such a story, most people incorporate the planted false memory, embroider it with details, and are absolutely certain it happened","In study after study, confidence in a memory turns out to be a poor guide to its authenticity","Students predicted that being assigned to an undesirable dorm would wreck their happiness, but one year later everyone had nearly identical levels of happiness","We fail to recognize our powers of adjustment when we mentally construct our futures, so we mispredict how events will make us feel"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"}]},{"id":"sa03","title":"Chapter 3: Self-Justification","pages":"75-127","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Cue Scrubber: The Rationalization Detector","visual_frame_description":"A stage lit by a single spotlight; each performer's inner speech scrolls above their head as a detector ring scans for the moment a rationalization snaps into place and the dissonance meter drops.","player_mission":"Scan ten self-justifying performances - the hypnotized Sam in his raincoat, Sally the smoker, the tobacco executives before Congress - and name the dissonance-reducing move in each: changing a cognition, adding a consonant cognition, disparaging the evidence, or minimizing the habit. Flag which justifications are conscious lies and which are people believing their own lies.","key_concepts":["People are motivated to justify their own actions, beliefs, and feelings; that is the essence of self-justification","Sam, hypnotized into buying bourbon and wearing rain gear on a sunny day, invented sensible-sounding reasons because it was difficult for him to accept that he had behaved nonsensically","Leon Festinger described cognitive dissonance as a state of tension that occurs when an individual simultaneously holds two cognitions that are psychologically inconsistent","Two cognitions are dissonant if the opposite of one follows from the other, and because the experience is unpleasant, people are motivated to reduce it","We reduce dissonance by changing one or both cognitions to make them more compatible or by adding cognitions that help bridge the gap","In the 1964 survey after the Surgeon General's report, 40 percent of heavy smokers doubted a link between smoking and cancer had been proven, while only 10 percent of nonsmokers doubted it","Smokers who smoked one to two packs a day reduced dissonance by convincing themselves that they were 'moderate' smokers","Heavy smokers who relapsed after a smoking cessation clinic reduced dissonance by convincing themselves that smoking isn't as bad as they originally thought","At the 1994 congressional hearings, the top executives of every major tobacco company admitted they were smokers and argued cigarettes are no more harmful than eating Twinkies","James J. Morgan of Philip Morris said cigarettes are not pharmacologically addictive, comparing his habit to liking gummy bears","People like Morgan begin to believe their own lies, in an effort to deceive themselves","The process of reducing dissonance is largely unconscious; people don't sit down and say, 'I guess I will reduce some dissonance now'"],"difficulty_level":"Beginner"},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Dialogue: The Saying-Is-Believing Debrief","visual_frame_description":"A cluttered experiment room where a tray of spools and a board of screws sit under a work lamp; two cash envelopes, one holding a single bill and one a thick stack, hover before the player while an interviewer's clipboard waits.","player_mission":"Take the seat of the student who spent an hour packing spools and turning screws, then decide what to tell the waiting participant about the task. Choose whether to accept the $1 or the $20 for your lie, then sit for the interviewer and rate how enjoyable the task really was, knowing your answer reveals whether external justification insulated you from belief.","key_concepts":["Festinger and Carlsmith asked college students to do a boring and repetitive series of tasks, packing spools in a tray and turning screws, for a full hour","Some students were offered $20 and others only $1 for telling a young woman that the task was interesting and enjoyable","Students paid $20 for lying rated the spool packing and screw turning as dull, but students paid only $1 rated the task as enjoyable","People who received abundant external justification for lying told the lie but didn't believe it","Those who told the lie in the absence of external justification moved in the direction of believing what they said, a result called the 'saying is believing' paradigm","If you want to get a person to change an attitude, get them to do or say something counter-attitudinal and underpay them for it","In Arthur Cohen's Yale experiment, the less students were paid to write essays in support of the police, the more liking for the police they expressed later","The smaller the bribe used to get you to give a speech, the more likely you are to justify it by convincing yourself that the things you said were true","External justification includes monetary gain, forced requirements, praise, and the desire to please","In Zimbardo's grasshopper experiment, reservists found the grasshoppers significantly tastier when the officer who asked them to eat one acted like a jerk rather than in a friendly manner","Zimbardo's high-dissonance shock participants reported feeling less pain, showed a less intense galvanic skin response, and were objectively less bothered by the pain","Brehm's food and water deprivation experiments found high-dissonance participants said they were less hungry or thirsty and consumed less afterward"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Audit: The Post-Decision Commitment Ledger","visual_frame_description":"A betting-window counter at a racetrack where bettors stream past holding tickets; the audit ledger lights up each entry with IRREVOCABLE stamps and a comparison meter showing chosen versus rejected appeal.","player_mission":"Audit six decisions you have made - a purchase, a bet, a commitment, a relationship choice - and log how your attitude toward the chosen and rejected options shifted after the decision. For each entry, mark the irrevocability of the choice and predict whether the racetrack, the photography lab, or the car dealership pattern applied to you.","key_concepts":["After a decision, especially a difficult or costly one, people almost always experience dissonance because their choice is seldom entirely positive and the rejected alternatives are seldom entirely negative","In Brehm's experiment, women who chose an appliance later rated its appeal somewhat higher and decreased the rating of the appliance they had rejected","To reduce dissonance, people cognitively spread apart the alternatives, emphasizing the positive attributes of the chosen option","In Johnson and Rusbult's study, the more heavily committed students were to their current romantic partners, the less attractive they found potential new partners","In Simpson's experiment, committed people found available others less attractive but did not lower their ratings of those too old or of the same sex; no threat, no dissonance","Knox and Inkster found that bettors who had just placed their $2 bets gave their horses a much better chance of winning than those who were about to place their bets","Nothing had changed between the two groups of bettors except the finality of the decision","In Gilbert's photography experiment, students whose first choice was final liked their photograph more than those who could exchange it within five days","Once a decision is irrevocable, people get busy making themselves feel good about the choice they made","Cialdini's lowballing ploy worked because signing a check created an implicit commitment and triggered the anticipation of a pleasant experience","New-car owners read advertisements selectively, reading more ads about their own car after the purchase and steering clear of ads for other makes","Because dissonance reduction is unconscious, we do not realize that it will protect us from pain in the future"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Discrimination Matrix: The Justification Strength Grid","visual_frame_description":"A chessboard-like grid glows on the floor, its columns reading ABUNDANT EXTERNAL JUSTIFICATION to NONE; each scenario card lands in a cell and its outcome meter shows whether the attitude moved or froze.","player_mission":"Drop a dozen discipline scenarios onto a grid that sorts them by the strength of external justification: $20 lies versus $1 lies, severe threats versus mild threats, harsh initiations versus easy ones. For each cell, predict whether attitudes change or stay rigid, then mark which side of the grid produces lasting values that survive when the punisher leaves the room.","key_concepts":["A child under a severe threat has a ready-made answer for why he is not beating up his sister: the giant over there will punish me","A child under a mild threat lacks complete justification, so he must convince himself that he really doesn't like beating up little kids","The less severe the threat, the less external justification; the less external justification, the greater the need for internal justification","In the Aronson-Carlsmith forbidden toy experiment, five-year-old children threatened with mild punishment found the forbidden toy less attractive afterward","Children threatened with severe punishment continued to rate the forbidden toy as highly desirable, and some found it even more desirable than before","In Freedman's study, one mild threat kept children away from the battery-powered robot as much as nine weeks later, while the severe threat failed to inhibit later behavior","Allowing people the opportunity to construct their own internal justification can help them develop a permanent set of values","If you want someone to do something once while you are watching, deliver the largest incentive or the most severe punishment you can; if you want lasting values, offer the smallest reward that brings about the behavior","In Mills's experiment with sixth-graders, those who cheated for a small reward softened their attitude about cheating more than those who cheated for a large reward","Freedman and Fraser found 17 percent of homeowners agreed to an ugly 'Drive Carefully' sign, but more than 55 percent complied after first signing a safe-driving petition","Pliner and her associates found that people induced to wear a lapel pin the day before were approximately twice as likely to donate to the American Cancer Society","Students asked not to 'be a cheater' claimed far less unearned money than students asked simply not to cheat, because cheating would threaten their self-concept of honesty"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Boss Battle: The Initiation Gauntlet","visual_frame_description":"A vaulted initiation hall where three gated corridors glow, NO ORDEAL, MILD WORDS, and SEVERE ORDEAL, while a one-way mirror shows the dull group discussion that every new member must rate.","player_mission":"Face the Chapter 3 endgame: recruit women into a psychology-of-sex discussion group and choose the severity of their initiation, none, mild, or a severe ordeal of reciting obscene words aloud. Then watch them rate the painfully tedious discussion, and decide whether to trust the severe-initiation members' glowing reviews or the effortless members' boredom, before applying effort justification to your own hardest-won commitments.","key_concepts":["In the Aronson-Mills experiment, women who went through a severe initiation succeeded in convincing themselves that the discussion was interesting and worthwhile","Women who made little or no effort to get into the group did not enjoy the discussion much and saw it as a dull and boring waste of time","The women actually listened to a prerecorded discussion designed to be as tedious and bombastic as possible","Gerard and Mathewson found that participants who underwent severe electric shocks to join a group liked that group better than those who underwent mild shocks","If a person goes through a difficult or painful experience in order to attain some goal or object, that goal or object becomes more attractive, a process called the justification of effort","If a flowerpot fell on your head on your way to a discussion group you would not like the group better, but if you volunteered to be hit by it in order to join, you would","Sapolsky described aged gentlemen paying fortunes for painful daily injections of testicular extract and reporting wondrous results, one big placebo effect","Conway and Ross found students in a useless study-skills course misremembered how bad their skills were before the course, a technique they call 'getting what you want by revising what you had'","In Brehm's vegetable experiment, children led to believe they would inevitably eat the vegetable again convinced themselves that it was not so bad","In Darley and Berscheid's experiment, women who believed they would inevitably interact with a young woman found her much more appealing than those who expected the other woman","In the hypocrisy paradigm, men who made a video advocating condoms after being made mindful of their own failures were far more likely to purchase condoms","Women in shower rooms who were made mindful of their own failures to take short showers took significantly shorter showers afterward"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"}]},{"id":"sa04","title":"Chapter 4: Conformity","pages":"127-171","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Cue Scrubber: The Line Judgment Lab","visual_frame_description":"A stark 1950s classroom where a white card holds line X and three comparison lines; six identical silhouettes answer 'Line A' in unison while your chair pulses with the words YOUR TURN.","player_mission":"Take the second-to-last seat in Asch's line-judgment room and judge twelve trials where six confederates answer first, most of them wrong. Decide each time whether to trust your eyes or the unanimous majority, then re-run the trials with an ally present, a different wrong answer, or a private response pad and watch conformity collapse.","key_concepts":["Conformity is a change in a person's behavior or opinions as a result of real or imagined pressure from another person or group of people","Asch's line judgment task was so easy that when people judged the lines alone, they made almost no errors","When faced with their fellow students agreeing on incorrect responses, approximately three-quarters of Asch's participants conformed at least once","Over the entire spectrum of judgments, an average of 35 percent of responses conformed to the incorrect judgments","Asch firmly believed there would be little, if any, yielding to group pressure, but his prediction was wrong","Anthony Pratkanis repeated the experiment on national television with college students who considered themselves nonconformists, and the results were almost identical to Asch's","Bond and Smith's analysis of 133 experiments in 17 countries found conformity is more prevalent in collectivist societies than in individualistic ones","Berns's fMRI replication showed participants who resisted group pressure displayed heightened activity in the amygdala, a region associated with pain, fear, and emotional discomfort","People conform to group pressure about as often when the unanimous group consists of only 3 other people as when it consists of 16","When unanimity is broken, so is the pressure to conform; even a single ally who sees it your way releases you from that pressure","Even a dissenter who gives a different wrong answer sharply reduces conformity, and the participant is likely to give the correct response","It only takes one dissenter to seriously diminish the power of the group to induce conformity"],"difficulty_level":"Beginner"},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Dialogue: The 3:00 a.m. Window Watch","visual_frame_description":"A row of apartment windows frames a dim street; each window holds a lit silhouette while a phone receiver waits in your hand, and a small ticker counts the other watchers who do nothing.","player_mission":"Stand at your Queens window at 3:00 a.m. while a woman is attacked on the street below and 37 other windows glow with silent watchers, then decide whether to call the police, mirroring the Kitty Genovese case. Next, play the student alone in a room when a woman's chair crashes in the next room, and discover whether one stranger in the room is enough to stop you from helping.","key_concepts":["In 1964, Kitty Genovese was stabbed to death near her home in Queens, New York, and news reports said 38 of her neighbors watched from their windows for 30 minutes without helping or calling the police","The Genovese story turned out to be exaggerated for the sake of sensational headlines, and interviews revealed the bystanders were horrified, not nonchalant","One reason for failing to intervene is conformity: the victim is less likely to get help if many people are watching, because nonintervention conforms to how others define the situation","The informational motive for conformity is that other people are sources of valuable information, so people become convinced their own opinions are wrong","The normative motive is that people go along with the crowd while inwardly believing their initial judgments are correct, in order to be accepted or avoid being disliked","Asch's participants gave their honest opinions when allowed to respond privately, suggesting their public yielding was largely a matter of going along to avoid exclusion","As physical reality becomes increasingly uncertain, people rely more and more on social reality, other people's behavior as the best available guide","In Latané and Rodin's experiment, 70 percent of students who were alone offered to help the woman after her staged fall, but only 20 percent offered help when sitting with a stranger","In Darley and Latané's epilepsy experiment, those who believed they were the only listener were far more likely to help, and as the number of listeners increased, help decreased","In Darley and Batson's study, only 10 percent of seminary students who thought they were late for their appointment offered help to a slumped victim","In Piliavin's subway experiments, people spontaneously rushed to aid a collapsed victim in more than 95 percent of trials, and helping was unaffected by the number of bystanders","The subway and the campground had two things in common: a feeling of sharing a common fate and a face-to-face situation with no immediate escape"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Audit: The Norm Enforcement Field Ledger","visual_frame_description":"A split-screen field logbook: on one side a shower room with a soaping silhouette, on the other a parking lot where a model picks up a fast-food bag while a ticker counts the fliers dropped on the ground.","player_mission":"Run four field experiments and log the results: the shower-room soap-up model, the parking-lot litter model, the mailbox with the €5 bill, and the hotel-towel sign. For each entry, classify the norm as descriptive or injunctive, record the exact conformity percentages, and note which environmental cue most powerfully enforced or eroded the norm.","key_concepts":["A descriptive norm reflects our knowledge about what most people do in a given situation, while an injunctive norm specifies what people should do, often through explicit directives","In Aronson's shower experiment, 49 percent of students conformed to a single role model who turned off the shower while soaping up, and conformity zoomed to 67 percent with two models","In Cialdini's parking lot experiment, 37 percent of drivers threw the flier on the ground in the control condition, but only 7 percent did when an accomplice picked up a discarded bag","Drivers were least likely to litter when a single piece of paper lay on the ground nearby, which reminded them of the injunctive norm, than when the lot was completely clean","In Keizer's mailbox experiment, 27 percent of passersby stole the envelope with the €5 bill when graffiti and litter were present, versus 13 percent when the mailbox was clean","The mailbox study is a test of the broken windows theory: when the environment sends the message that people don't care, the disorder spreads to human behavior","Signs urging hotel guests to reuse towels work better when they explicitly say that most other guests in the room have reused theirs","Informing hikers that very few people broke the rules against collecting petrified wood reduced the incidence more than signs that only asked them not to remove it","In Sherif's autokinetic effect experiments, each student's judgments moved in the direction of the group average, and the change stuck when they were tested again","Sherif could reliably shape the group norm by having confederates give arbitrary estimates, so long as the deviation from the actual norm wasn't too large","Pluralistic ignorance is the collective belief in a false norm created by the ambiguous behavior of others, as when no student raises a hand in a confusing lecture","Miller and Prentice found that giving students accurate information about their peers' true feelings about binge drinking reduced conformity and lowered the rate of drinking"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Discrimination Matrix: The Conformity Levels Grid","visual_frame_description":"A three-tiered temple grid rises in the room, its tiers labeled COMPLIANCE, IDENTIFICATION, and INTERNALIZATION; each scenario card drops onto a tier and its permanence meter spins up or down.","player_mission":"Drop twelve acts of conformity into the three-level grid: the speed-limit driver, the uncle-and-aunt beliefs, the popular classmate's marijuana opinions, and the Asch line answer. For each act, decide whether it runs on reward and punishment, admiration of a role model, or the desire to be right, then mark which levels survive when the watcher leaves the room.","key_concepts":["Compliance is conformity motivated by the desire to gain reward or avoid punishment, and the behavior is only as long-lived as the promise of reward or the threat of punishment","Remove the threat of punishment or the benefits of riches, and the citizens will cease showing allegiance to the dictator","Identification is a level of conformity brought about by an individual's desire to be like the group or role model they admire","In identification we come to believe in the opinions and values we adopt, although we may not believe in them very strongly","The good-old-Uncle-Harry-and-Aunt-Harriet phenomenon describes incorporating the political beliefs of beloved relatives because you want to be like them","Internalization is the most permanent level of conformity, motivated by the desire to be right","Once we have internalized a belief and made it our own, it becomes independent of its source and extremely resistant to change","Most people obey the speed limit to avoid a penalty, which is compliance; some because an admired father stressed the law, which is identification; and some because they are convinced speed laws are good, which is internalization","In Cohen and Prinstein's chat room study, teenagers were far more likely to adopt the opinions of classmates they believed were popular and admired","The influence of the admired classmates was durable and was evident even when participants gave their opinions about marijuana in private","Continuous reward or punishment is not necessary for identification; you just need to want to be like that person","Crandall found that the more sorority women hewed to the group's ideal body type and bingeing behavior, the more popular they were"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Boss Battle: The Obedience Shock Trial","visual_frame_description":"A laboratory table bears the 30-switch Shock Generator, its dial glowing from 'Slight Shock' to 'Danger: Severe Shock' to 'XXX'; a technician in a lab coat stands behind you repeating, 'The experiment requires that you continue.'","player_mission":"Climb into the teacher's chair at the Shock Generator and run the full Milgram procedure, delivering 15 volts up to the XXX level while the learner pounds the wall and begs to be released. Choose to obey or defy at each milestone, then face the variations: the Bridgeport office, the phone-issued orders, the learner in the room, and the two defiant teachers, and discover who obeys when authority loses prestige or proximity.","key_concepts":["Milgram's initial forty participants were a random sample of businessmen, professionals, and white-collar and blue-collar workers in Connecticut","The learner was an accomplice who never received any shock, but the teacher had experienced a sample shock and heard the learner's anguished reactions","Forty psychiatrists predicted that most participants would quit at 150 volts and that fewer than 1 in 100 would administer the highest shock","The psychiatrists were completely wrong: some 67 percent of the participants continued to administer shocks to the maximum level","Milgram and Elms discovered no differences in personality tests between those who were fully obedient and those who successfully resisted","The obedient individuals were not sadistic; some protested, and many sweated, trembled, stuttered, and burst into nervous laughter, but they continued to obey","Replications in Australia, Jordan, Spain, Germany, and the Netherlands produced much the same results, and women are just as obedient as men","Jerry Burger's 2007 replication showed that modern Americans are every bit as susceptible to being led to shock an innocent victim as participants nearly 60 years earlier","When Milgram moved the study from Yale to offices in industrial Bridgeport, full obedience dropped from 67 percent to 48 percent","When a non-authoritative substitute replaced the scientist, only 20 percent delivered the full set of shocks, and telephone orders from out of the room dropped obedience below 25 percent","When two fellow teachers defied the experimenter's orders, the proportion of fully obedient people dropped to just 10 percent","Milgram's unwelcome message was that an astonishingly large proportion of people will cause pain to other people when an authority figure orders them to obey"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"}]},{"id":"sa05","title":"Chapter 5: Mass Communication, Propaganda, and Persuasion","pages":"171-219","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Cue Scrubber: The Who-Says-What-to-Whom Sweep","visual_frame_description":"A news studio frozen mid-broadcast; the communicator's face, the words of the message, and the faces of the audience are each ringed in a different colored scanner light while a paradigm meter tallies which factor is driving the shift.","player_mission":"Scan twelve persuasion scenes and tag each one by the three factors that make a communication effective: the source (who says it), the nature of the communication (how it is said), and the characteristics of the audience (to whom it is said). For each scene, name the factor doing the work and say whether the target is being moved through an opinion or a deeply held attitude.","key_concepts":["Persuasion occurs when communication from one person changes the opinions, attitudes, or behavior of another person","An opinion is what a person believes to be true and is primarily cognitive and transient, while an attitude is an opinion that includes an emotional and evaluative component and is extremely difficult to change","The three key factors in any persuasive attempt are the source of the communication, the nature of the communication, and the characteristics and mindset of the audience","The news media influence opinions simply by selecting which events get exposure and how much exposure they get, based largely on entertainment value, so 'if it bleeds, it leads'","A Gallup poll found 60 percent of Americans believed crime was on the rise even though crimes of all kinds had been steadily declining for 25 years, a distortion fed by violent media coverage","Selective emphasis puts the media in a position not only of reporting events but of determining subsequent ones, as when heavy news coverage of suicides increased teen suicides (David Phillips's studies)","More than 90 percent of preschool children asked for toys or food they saw advertised on television, and by the tenth grade only 4 percent of children believed ads told the truth most of the time","Robert Zajonc showed that, all other things being equal, the more familiar an item is, the more attractive it is, even a silly nonsense word","Northwestern Mutual jumped from thirty-fourth to third in name familiarity after two weeks of $1 million worth of advertising, and A&W Root Beer's market share rose from 15 to 50 percent after six months of TV ads","The Willie Horton ads of the 1988 Bush-Dukakis campaign showed that when people are scared and angry, facts alone (like Dukakis's furlough statistics) are neither reassuring nor convincing","Eli Pariser calls the personalized universe of information created by algorithms and social networks the filter bubble, which reinforces our beliefs invisibly so we think we are seeing reality","The more educated and skeptical viewers believe they are immune to persuasion, but our sense of immunity can make us more susceptible to persuasion of all kinds"],"difficulty_level":"Beginner"},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Dialogue: The Central Versus Peripheral Cross-Examination","visual_frame_description":"A courtroom splits down the middle: the left half shows scales weighing argument strength while the right half flashes peripheral cues - a glowing glove, a rhymed slogan, a celebrity smile - and a cursor labeled 'which route?' hovers between them.","player_mission":"Play two jurors at once: one who weighs arguments systematically about reducing prison recidivism and one who gets scared and angry by a mug shot. For each pitch, decide whether it persuades by strong logic (central route) or by simple, irrelevant cues like a rhymed slogan or a familiar face, and then argue both sides of why the phrase 'If it doesn't fit, you must acquit' swayed a jury.","key_concepts":["Richard Petty and John Cacioppo argued there are two ways people are persuaded: the central route, weighing arguments and considering relevant facts systematically, and the peripheral route, responding to simple, often irrelevant cues","People who consider the strength of arguments about reducing prison recidivism are taking the central route; those who get scared and angry by the image of Willie Horton are taking the peripheral route","Johnnie Cochran's rhyme 'If it doesn't fit, you must acquit' was persuasive not because of its logic but because people judging an argument can be influenced by the way it is phrased","Matthew McGlone found college students were more persuaded by unfamiliar aphorisms that rhyme ('woes unite foes') than by the same ideas in nonrhyming form ('woes unite enemies')","Frank Luntz's research showed people could be turned against an estate tax if politicians called it a 'death tax', which conjures the image of being unfairly penalized for dying","Far more Americans approved of the Affordable Care Act than of Obamacare, although it was the same program","ABC News's renaming of a USDA-approved meat additive as 'pink slime' cost producers their customers, showing words conjure powerful images that can overwhelm consideration of the facts","If your aim is to persuade, you need strong, logical arguments for audiences that are knowledgeable and motivated, and high-quality peripheral cues for audiences that do not care or cannot comprehend","Persuasion that gets people to process arguments systematically is much more likely to endure than persuasion based solely on peripheral cues","Harold Hartmann's early study of voting preferences showed that individuals who received a primarily emotional message voted for the endorsed candidate more often than those who received a primarily logical message","In the fluoridation referendum, opponents used an emotional leaflet picturing an ugly rat saying 'Don't let them put rat poison in your drinking water' and soundly defeated the logical pro-fluoridation campaign"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Audit: The Source Credibility Field Log","visual_frame_description":"A broadcast studio with a walk of fame: each communicator steps onto a pedestal as a credibility meter swings between expert, trustworthy, self-interested, and persuasive, while a ledger auto-fills with audience impact scores.","player_mission":"Spend an hour rating communicators on trustworthiness and expertise: a Nobel physicist, a communist party newspaper, a prize-winning engineer, a dishwasher, a mobster arguing for stricter courts, and a beautiful woman pitching an opinion. For each entry, note whether the person argues against their own self-interest or seems unaware they are being overheard, and log the credibility verdict.","key_concepts":["Carl Hovland and Walter Weiss found that almost everyone who believed a claim about nuclear submarines came from J. Robert Oppenheimer changed their opinions, while very few shifted when the identical claim was attributed to Pravda","A communicator is credible when he or she is both expert and trustworthy, the concept Hovland and Weiss substituted for Aristotle's 'good men'","In the Aronson and Golden experiment, sixth-graders heard a speech on arithmetic from a prize-winning engineer or a dishwasher; prejudiced children were less influenced by the black engineer and unprejudiced children were more influenced by him than by the identical white engineer","Joe 'The Shoulder' Napolitano, a fictional mobster and hit man, was totally ineffective arguing for lenient courts but extremely effective arguing for stricter, more powerful courts - as effective as a respected public official","When people argue against their own self-interest, we infer that the truth of the issue is so compelling that they sincerely believe what they are saying","Patrick Reynolds, heir to the R. J. Reynolds Tobacco fortune, took a strong public stand against smoking, and no one disputed his sincerity","Elaine Walster and Leon Festinger found participants' opinions changed significantly more when they believed graduate students were unaware of their presence in the next room and were not trying to influence them","Judson Mills and Aronson showed a beautiful woman could have a major impact on an audience's opinions on a topic wholly irrelevant to her beauty, and her impact was greatest when she openly told the audience she wanted to influence them","Alice Eagly and Shelly Chaiken replicated the finding that more likable communicators are more persuasive and showed that we expect beautiful people to think the way we do","People trust the confident speaker rather than one who hems and haws, but extreme confidence can rouse our suspicions if we suspect flimflam or an ulterior motive","The same communicator may be regarded by some people as possessing high credibility and by others as possessing low credibility","Celebrities and athletes can influence our cereal choices and opinions on trivial issues, but are unlikely to influence us on issues where we strongly disagree, such as gun control or abortion"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Discrimination Matrix: Fear, Forewarning, and Inoculation Trials","visual_frame_description":"A laboratory grid glows with three axes - fear dosage, audience self-esteem, and instruction specificity - as needle gauges for compliance swing; a weakened-virus vial labeled 'inoculation' sits beside a megaphone labeled 'forewarning'.","player_mission":"Run a matrix of persuasion defenses: sort appeals by fear level (low, moderate, high), audience self-esteem, and the presence of specific instructions, then choose whether to inoculate the audience with a mild attack or warn them it is coming. Track which cells yield attitude change and which yield resistance.","key_concepts":["The overwhelming weight of experimental data suggests that, all other things being equal, the more frightened a person is by a communication, the more likely he or she is to take preventive action","Howard Leventhal's smoking and chest X-ray studies showed those who were most frightened were also most eager to stop smoking and most likely to get X-rays","People with high self-esteem are most likely to take immediate action when frightened, while people with low self-esteem act if there is a delay and they know they can take action later","Fear-arousing messages containing specific instructions about how, when, and where to take action are much more effective than generalized alarms with no recommendations","In the tetanus shot campaign, 28 percent of students who were frightened and given specific instructions got the shots, while only 3 percent of those without instructions or high fear did","Daniel Gilbert argues our brains evolved to be frightened by clear and present dangers, not gradual ones, so people are more afraid of terrorism and identity theft than of global warming","Matthew McGlone's swine flu pamphlets showed people who read 'Swine flu may kill thousands of people this year' were significantly more likely to see the flu as frightening and to schedule a flu shot than those who read that people contract the flu","William McGuire's inoculation effect: people who receive a brief communication they can refute are 'immunized' against a subsequent full-blown presentation of the same argument","Inoculating people with the facts about 9/11 increased their resistance to subsequent conspiracy theory propaganda about that tragedy","Jack Brehm's theory of reactance: when our sense of freedom is threatened, we attempt to restore it, so blatant or coercive messages trigger resistance","Jonathan Freedman and David Sears found teenagers who were forewarned 10 minutes before a talk on why teenagers should not drive were less convinced than those who got no forewarning","Madeline Heilman found the more intense the attempts to prevent people from signing a petition, the more likely they were to sign it"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Boss Battle: The Propaganda Siege of the Media Citadel","visual_frame_description":"A walled city where citizens scroll glowing feeds; hostile banners - 'fake news', 'death panels', 'America Under Attack' - batter the gates while defenders arm a broadcast tower with inoculation speeches and a 'more speech, not enforced silence' banner unfurls.","player_mission":"Defend a city of citizens against a 24/7 propaganda siege: fake news and filter bubbles on one front, emotional contagion on another, and identity appeals and moral outrage on the last. For each wave, deploy the correct countermeasure - inoculation, two-sided refutation, reactance triggers, or free inquiry - before the citizens vote on a war they never wanted.","key_concepts":["Propaganda is defined as 'the systematic propagation of a given doctrine' and education as 'the act of imparting knowledge or skill', yet whether a message counts as one or the other depends largely on the viewer's moral values and ideology","Fake news demonstrates that because it comes from so many unknown sources and is easy to package as real news, people rely on their existing ideas and social networks to decide what to believe","Emotional contagion occurs when one person's emotional behavior triggers similar emotions and behaviors in observers, as with the 1982 Tylenol poisonings, where false alarms outnumbered verified cases of product tampering by seven to one","The more coverage major television networks devoted to suicide, the greater the subsequent increase in suicides among teenagers, and suicides spike especially when a celebrity commits suicide","Tweets containing words associated with moral emotions, such as outrageous or disgusting, tend to be re-tweeted more often than tweets with non-moral emotion words","Moral elevation - witnessing virtue in others, such as in videos of real-life kindness - makes people many times more likely to say yes to a request for help or money","Adam Grant nearly tripled donations from volunteer callers by arranging a five-minute meeting between them and a past recipient of the fellowship they were soliciting for","Aronson's energy conservation study found auditors who used vivid language, like comparing cracks to a basketball-sized hole in the living room wall, increased homeowner improvements from 15 to 61 percent","Christopher Bryan showed people are much more likely to vote if asked 'How important is it for you to be a voter?' than 'How important is it for you to vote?', and children help more when asked 'Who wants to be a helper?'","The Texas 'Don't Mess With Texas' slogan, tapping into frontier pride and identity, cut litter on Texas highways roughly 72 percent between 1986 and 1990","Well-informed audiences are more persuaded by a two-sided argument that refutes the opposition, while uninformed audiences are persuaded by whichever one side they hear","John Stuart Mill argued in 'On Liberty' that we must allow the expression of ideas we dislike, and Justice Brandeis wrote that the remedy for fallacies and falsehoods is 'more speech, not enforced silence'"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"}]},{"id":"sa06","title":"Chapter 6: Human Aggression","pages":"219-269","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Cue Scrubber: The Intentional Harm Detector","visual_frame_description":"A street frozen mid-action with each punch, insult, and gunshot trailed by a red intention-tag that glows only for deliberate harm; a biology chart of chimps and bonobos hangs in the background while an instinct-versus-learning meter wavers.","player_mission":"Scan twelve violent scenes and decide for each one whether it is aggression: check the actor's intention, whether harm is physical or verbal, and whether the act succeeded or missed. Then classify the source - instinct, culture of honor, frustration, or learned model - and flag any scene where the target is actually being dehumanized.","key_concepts":["Aronson defines aggression as an intentional action aimed at doing harm or causing physical or psychological pain, so an angry friend throwing a beer bottle that misses is still aggression but a drunk driver's accident is not","Thomas Hobbes in Leviathan argued humans are brutes in their natural state, while Rousseau's noble savage held that a restrictive society forces gentle creatures to become hostile","Freud theorized human beings are born with an instinctual drive toward life (eros) and an instinctual drive toward death (thanatos) leading to aggressive actions","Zing Yang Kuo raised a kitten in the same cage with a rat and found the cat refused to attack its buddy or any other rats, showing aggressive instincts can be curbed by early experience","Rats raised in isolation still attack a fellow rat introduced into the cage using the same pattern of threat and attack as experienced rats, so in some species aggression needn't be learned","Our closest genetic relatives are the aggressive chimpanzee and the peaceful bonobo, the 'make love not war' ape that defuses conflict with communal sex","The Iroquois lived in peace for hundreds of years but became ferocious warriors when trade with Europeans brought competition with the Hurons over furs, showing aggression is an optional strategy shaped by circumstances","Richard Nisbett showed homicide rates for white Southern males are substantially higher than for white Northern males, but only for honor-related killings and only in areas with a herding economy","In the culture of honor experiments by Dov Cohen and Richard Nisbett, Southern white males responded to an insult with more aggression, spiking cortisol and testosterone, while Northerners tended to shrug it off","Cultures of honor have rates of honor-related homicide five times higher than other regions, more than twice as many school shootings per capita, and higher rates of domestic violence","The number one predictor of violence is gender: boys engage in more rough-and-tumble play, and in a cross-cultural study by Dane Archer and Patricia McDaniel, young men in all 11 countries showed a greater tendency to end conflict stories with violence","James Dabbs found naturally occurring testosterone levels are significantly higher among prisoners convicted of violent crimes, but Robert Sapolsky argues testosterone does not cause aggression since aggression continues even after castration"],"difficulty_level":"Beginner"},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Dialogue: The Thwarted-Goal Interrogation","visual_frame_description":"A playroom bisected by a wire screen: on one side children press against the mesh watching the toys, on the other children play freely; a rage thermometer rises only on the frustrated side while a monkey hurls a cucumber at a researcher in the corner.","player_mission":"Interrogate the frustration-aggression connection by playing the experimenter in the roomful of toys: frustrate one group of children behind a wire screen while letting another play freely, then watch how each group handles the toys. Then switch sides and run the line-cutting field study, the charity phone call, and the capuchin monkey grape exchange to pin down when goals within reach, high expectations, and unfairness turn frustration into rage.","key_concepts":["Roger Barker, Tamara Dembo, and Kurt Lewin frustrated young children by showing them a roomful of appealing toys kept out of reach behind a wire screen; when finally allowed to play, the frustrated group smashed and threw the toys while the non-frustrated group played joyfully","Mary Harris's field study found people standing behind a student who cut into the second place in line became much more aggressive than those behind an intruder who cut in front of the twelfth person, since frustration is high when a goal is within reach","James Kulik and Roger Brown found charity phone callers with high expectations spoke more harshly and hung up more forcefully when refused, and callers became more aggressive when refusals sounded arbitrary and illegitimate rather than affordable","Sarah Brosnan and Frans de Waal found capuchin monkeys rewarded with cucumber slices became angry and even threw the cucumber when a nearby monkey received a grape for the same token, showing the perceived unfairness effect is so fundamental it appears in monkeys","Frustration is most pronounced when the goal is within reach, when expectations are high, when the rule of fairness has been violated, and when the goal is blocked without a compelling reason","Frustration is often the result of relative deprivation, the deprivation we feel when we see others enjoying a better situation or when we are deprived relative to our expectations","Katherine DeCelles and Michael Norton found the presence of a first-class cabin produced four times as many aggressive 'air rage' incidents among economy passengers, especially when they had to walk through first class to board","Most social revolutions are started not by the most oppressed but by people who have recently lifted their faces out of the mud and noticed others doing better, as Tocqueville observed about evils that become intolerable once escape seems possible","The most intense African American riots of the 1960s took place not in the areas of greatest poverty but in Los Angeles (Watts) and Detroit, where conditions were better but the gap relative to whites was visible","Shabaz Mallick and Boyd McCandless found frustrated third-graders directed much less aggression at a thwarting child when told he had been 'sleepy and upset'","Hopeless people are apathetic people: Ugandans under Idi Amin and South African blacks under apartheid did not revolt while they were prevented from hoping for anything better","The pain we receive always feels more intense than the pain we inflict: English neurologists found participants could not retaliate exactly in kind and always escalated pressure, making escalation 'a natural by-product of neural processing'"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Audit: The Situational Trigger Field Log","visual_frame_description":"A summer city street where every aggressive act is traced back to a glowing cause: thermometers over rooftops, a glowing rifle on a desk, cocktail glasses in a bar, and a voodoo doll bristling with pins in a kitchen window.","player_mission":"Walk through a heatwave city and log every situational trigger of aggression you find: a 90-degree testing room, a voodoo doll stabbed at low blood sugar, a rifle left lying on a table, a full bar fight, and a driver honking in traffic. For each entry, identify the trigger category - heat, pain, hunger, alcohol, weapons, rejection - and record the experimental evidence that proves it.","key_concepts":["Leonard Berkowitz showed students who underwent the pain of having their hand immersed in excruciatingly cold water showed a sharp increase in committing aggressive acts against other students","Brad Bushman's voodoo doll study of 107 married couples found spouses with the lowest blood sugar stuck more than twice as many pins in the doll and were more likely to blast their spouse with awful noises, the toxic combination of anger and hunger - being 'hangry'","In the 'long, hot summer of 1967', 159 race riots erupted, and Merrill Carlsmith and Craig Anderson found riots were far more likely on hot days than cold days, while heat did not increase burglary or property crimes","William Griffitt and Roberta Veitch found students who took a test in a 90 degree F room reported feeling more aggressive and expressed more hostility toward a stranger than students in a normal-temperature room","In major league baseball, significantly more batters are hit by pitched balls when the temperature is above 90 degrees, and Phoenix drivers without air-conditioning honk more in traffic jams","Alcohol serves as a disinhibitor that makes drinkers less cautious and impulsive, and it disrupts information processing so drunk people respond to the earliest, most obvious aspects of a situation and miss subtleties","In the 'think-drink' effect study of 116 men, the actual quantity of alcohol the men drank was related less to their aggressive behavior than their expectations: the more alcohol they believed they were drinking, the more aggressively they behaved","The weapons effect: college students angered in a room where a rifle was left lying around administered more electric shocks than those angered in a room with a badminton racket, and people drive more aggressively with a gun on the passenger seat","Mark Leary's analysis of over 15 school shootings found acute or chronic rejection - ostracism, bullying, or romantic rejection - was present in all but two of the incidents","Jean Twenge and Roy Baumeister found college students led to believe nobody wanted to work with them expressed far more intense hostility, even against neutral individuals, than students who had not been excluded","Philip Zimbardo showed female students made anonymous with robes and hoods administered longer and more severe shocks, and Brian Mullen found the larger the mob in 60 lynchings between 1899 and 1946, the more heinous the atrocities","Deborah Davis and Elizabeth Loftus found men who are drunk are less likely to interpret nonconsent messages accurately and women who are drunk convey less emphatic signs of refusal"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Discrimination Matrix: Media Violence and Catharsis Sorting Grid","visual_frame_description":"A courtroom evidence wall covered with cards and video stills: a child punching a Bobo doll, a man slugging a punching bag, a gory boxing match, and a violent video game screen; a verdict banner asks 'Does venting reduce or increase hostility?' while the pointer swings to increase.","player_mission":"Sort a wall of evidence into five columns: catharsis studies, imitation studies, desensitization effects, hostile attribution bias, and the fear-of-crime magnifier. Decide for each card whether the finding supports venting anger or shows that venting backfires, and place the Bobo doll, the punching bag, the voodoo finger-pressure game, and the lambs-versus-lemmings game in their correct cells.","key_concepts":["The belief in catharsis comes from Freud's hydraulic analogy that aggressive energy must be drained off lest it build up and explode, but abundant evidence indicates the 'hit a pillow' solution simply does not work","Brad Bushman found participants who slugged a punching bag while thinking about the student who insulted them felt the most angry afterward, and later blasted their tormentor with the loudest, longest noise, while those who sat still felt least angry","Arthur Patterson measured football players' hostility before, during, and after the season and found a significant increase in hostility over the course of the season, contradicting the idea that intense physical activity drains aggression","Russell Geen found participants who had previously shocked a confederate delivered more frequent and more intense shocks the second time around, contrary to the catharsis hypothesis","Laid-off technicians who were allowed to verbalize their hostility against their ex-bosses were much nastier in their subsequent descriptions of them than those who had not vented","The more people ventilate anger by behaving aggressively, the angrier they remain and the more aggressive they become, and people who believe in catharsis act out more aggressively because they expect it to make them feel better","Michael Kahn found students who vented hostility about a derogatory technician to his employer subsequently disliked him more and expressed greater hostility toward him than those not given the opportunity to vent","In Albert Bandura's Bobo doll experiments, children who watched an adult knock around a plastic doll imitated the aggression hit for hit and kick for kick and invented their own new aggressive behaviors","A meta-analysis of 98 studies with nearly 37,000 participants found both violent video games and prosocial video games have direct effects on their players","Bushman and Anderson found participants who played a violent video game took five times longer to respond to a victim groaning in the next room, and people who had just seen a violent movie took longer to help a woman struggling with her crutches","A longitudinal study of 430 elementary school children found that consumption of media violence early in the school year predicted higher rates of verbal, relational, and physical aggression and less prosocial behavior later in the year","Gentile and Gentile found children who played many different violent games were more likely to develop a hostile attribution bias, interpreting the ambiguous behavior of others in a hostile manner"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Boss Battle: The Violence Reduction Summit","visual_frame_description":"A school auditorium summit table surrounded by glowing strategy cards - punishment severity dials, empathy training videos, mindfulness bells, and cooperative game designs - while a city skyline beyond the windows dims as each intervention succeeds.","player_mission":"Take charge of a violence prevention summit and deploy each proven intervention: mild threats of punishment that produce internalization, rewarding nonaggressive alternatives, providing nonaggressive models, empathy training, and mindfulness. A crisis counselor will test you with the paradox cases - the smacking mother, the graffiti wall, the horn-honking drivers, and the bullying school - and you must choose the strategy the research actually supports.","key_concepts":["States that have the harshest punishment - the death penalty for murder - have higher murder rates than states without it, and a natural experiment from the Gideon v. Wainwright ruling found prisoners who served their full terms were twice as likely to return to prison as those released early","Severe punishment, if administered promptly, frequently results in compliance but rarely produces internalization, and it teaches children 'so that's what I should do when I'm mad at someone: yell at them or hit them'","Threats of mild punishment are far more effective than threats of severe punishment: with minimal external justification, the child internalizes a counteraggressive value and convinces himself that hitting someone is neither desirable nor fun","Dan Olweus curtailed bullying in Norwegian schools by as much as 50 percent by training teachers to be vigilant and to take swift but moderate punitive action","Seeing an aggressive model punished neither increases nor decreases a child's aggressive behavior, while seeing an aggressor rewarded increases it, so it is just as effective not to expose children to aggressive models at all","Paul Brown and Rogers Elliot found nursery school teachers who ignored aggressive acts but were attentive when children shared and cooperated produced a noticeable decline in aggressive behavior","Joel Davitz found children trained to behave constructively played far more constructively and less aggressively after frustration than children rewarded for aggressive, competitive behavior","Robert Baron and Richard Kepner found participants who witnessed a person delivering intense shocks to their tormentor delivered more intense shocks, and those who saw mild shocks delivered milder shocks, showing aggression is a form of conformity","In the horn-honking experiment, almost 90 percent of second-car drivers honked aggressively when the light turned green, but the percentage dropped dramatically when the pedestrian crossing was on crutches and evoked empathy","Norma and Seymour Feshbach demonstrated a negative correlation between empathy and aggression in children, and Norma Feshbach's 30-hour empathy-training program made children more empathic, higher in self-esteem, more generous, and less aggressive","Kenichi Obuchi found Japanese students administered much milder shocks to victims who first disclosed something personal about themselves, opening the door to empathy","Lara Aknin's three-method research pinned down that behaving altruistically produces greater feelings of happiness, and even brief mindfulness training reduces mind-wandering and aggressive responses to provocation"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"}]},{"id":"sa07","title":"Chapter 7: Prejudice","pages":"269-319","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Cue Scrubber: The Three-Component Prejudice Detector","visual_frame_description":"A mindscape gallery where each person's head is split into three panels: one panel fills with a sketchy 'little picture' of the group, one glows red with gut feeling, and one shows an action of unfair treatment; an accuracy meter grades each stereotype.","player_mission":"Scan twelve scenes and split each one into its three components: the cognitive stereotype (the little picture in the head), the emotional gut feeling, and the behavioral discrimination. For each scene, name the category used - race, gender, weight, sexual orientation - and decide whether the stereotype is accurate, misleading, or a rationalization for hatred.","key_concepts":["Aronson defines prejudice as a negative attitude toward all members of a distinguishable group, based solely on their membership in the group, and it is partly cognitive, partly emotional, and partly behavioral","Categorization, the grouping of people by key characteristics, is fundamental to cognition and can be the first step toward prejudice, and the fundamental category for social animals is us or them","Newborns show no preference for faces of one race or another, but by three months of age babies in a mono-racial setting prefer faces of their own race, showing we are predisposed to like people like us and be wary of people who are different","Walter Lippmann, the first to use the term stereotype, described the difference between reality - the 'world out there' - and the 'little pictures in our heads'","Contrary to conventional wisdom, stereotypes are often accurate and can be an adaptive, shorthand way of dealing with complexity; Lee Jussim showed stereotype accuracy is among the strongest and most reliable findings in social psychology","A stereotype that is accurate as a group average can be woefully incomplete and misleading when applied to an individual, as with the 'model minority' myth that torments a Chinese male who hates math","Jeff Stone's experiment: students who believed basketball player 'Mark Flick' was African American rated him as having more natural athletic ability and playing a better game, while those who thought he was white rated him as having greater hustle and basketball smarts","Birt Duncan showed people interpreted the same shove differently depending on who did it - aggression if a black man shoved a white man, playful if a white man shoved a black man - even when the interpreters were themselves black","James Pennebaker wired up men and women with voice recorders and found both sexes used about 16,000 words per day, so the girls-talk-more stereotype is simply false","Peter Glick and Susan Fiske found sexism worldwide takes two forms - hostile sexism, holding negative stereotypes of women, and benevolent sexism, holding positive but patronizing stereotypes that idealize women as needing protection","In the hurricane naming study, raters thought male-named hurricanes were more intense than female-named ones and said they'd be more likely to evacuate for Hurricane Christopher than for identically described Hurricane Christina","Oliver Wendell Holmes Jr. compared the mind of a bigot to the pupil of an eye: 'The more light you pour on it, the more it contracts', and as Allport put it, 'Defeated intellectually, prejudice lingers emotionally'"],"difficulty_level":"Beginner"},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Dialogue: The Us-Them Interrogation","visual_frame_description":"A camp at Robbers Cave splits into the Eagles and the Rattlers squaring off over tug-of-war rope while a scapegoat runs through the woods; beside it a coal mine splits into an integrated shaft and a segregated town, and a demagogue's checklist floats over a rally stage.","player_mission":"Interrogate four causes of prejudice in turn: economic and political competition, displaced aggression and scapegoating, maintenance of status and self-image, and conformity to social norms. Play the experimenter in the Robbers Cave camp, the demagogue studying the 1949 speech analysis, the low-status sorority member, and the West Virginia coal miner to decide which cause each case actually demonstrates.","key_concepts":["Prejudice often results from economic and political competition: John Dollard documented that prejudice against newly arrived Germans in a small industrial town increased as jobs became scarce","Attitudes toward Chinese immigrants in nineteenth-century America fluctuated with economic competition - 'depraved and vicious' while competing for gold, 'sober, industrious, and law-abiding' while building the railroad, and criminal and conniving again once jobs grew scarce","In a 1970s survey, most anti-black prejudice was found in groups just one rung above black people socioeconomically, especially where whites and blacks competed closely for jobs","In Sherif's Robbers Cave experiment, healthy, well-adjusted 12-year-old boys randomly assigned to the Eagles or the Rattlers became hostile and riotous after a series of competitions with prizes, and simply stopping the competition did not eliminate the hostility","Scapegoating describes blaming innocent and powerless others for our troubles, tracing back to the ancient Hebrew ritual of placing the sins of the people on a goat that was sent into the wilderness","Laboratory experiments suggest frustrated individuals displace aggression onto groups that are disliked, visible, and relatively powerless, but the form the mistreatment takes depends on what the ingroup approves of","An analysis of demagogues' speeches throughout history found striking regularities: 'You've been cheated', 'there is a widespread conspiracy', 'our enemies are low animals', and 'behold, I am your leader'","Self-justification preserves the self-image but intensifies hostility: if we can convince ourselves a group is subhuman or stupid, we feel free to enslave or aggress against it without questioning our own morality","Jennifer Crocker found college women in low-status sororities expressed more prejudice and disparagement of other sororities than members of higher-status sororities, and low or declining social status predicts prejudice","Steven Fein and Stephen Spencer found anti-Semitic students became especially biased against a woman they thought was Jewish if they received a low score on a supposed intelligence test, showing threats to self-esteem increase prejudice","Thomas Pettigrew concluded the West Virginia coal miners' pattern - complete integration underground and complete segregation above ground - demonstrates that prejudice is often simply conformity to the prevailing norms of one's community","Jeanne Watson found people who moved to a large city and came into contact with anti-Semitic people became more anti-Semitic themselves, while Pettigrew found Southerners entering the army became less prejudiced against blacks"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Audit: The Hidden Bias Field Log","visual_frame_description":"A job market diorama: identical resumes float onto desks and get shuffled into a yes pile or a no pile, an orchestra auditions behind a screen, a woman in a fat suit drinks a milkshake in a department store, and an eye-tracking heat map shows teachers staring at a black boy at play.","player_mission":"Go undercover and log discrimination that hides in plain sight: resumes with black names versus white names, identical science-lab resumes with male versus female names, blind orchestra auditions, teachers watching children at play, and salespeople facing a fat-suit shopper with a diet soda or a milkshake. For each entry, identify the bias mechanism and judge whether the IAT would or would not have predicted it.","key_concepts":["African Americans are much more likely than whites to be arrested, convicted, and incarcerated for the same crime, and possession of crack cocaine carries more severe penalties than powder cocaine even though both are the same chemical","Field experiments show applicants with black names like DeShawn or Queenisha are less likely to get called for interviews than applicants with names like Emily or Greg despite identical resumes","Devah Pager sent matched pairs of well-groomed college graduates to 350 Milwaukee employers and found whites with a clean record were called back three times as often as black applicants with a clean record, and employers even preferred white convicts over black applicants with a clean record","When science professors at leading universities evaluated resumes with randomly assigned male or female names, both male and female professors judged the male applicant as more capable, more worthy of hiring, and more deserving of a higher salary","In 2014 the typical American woman earned about 77 cents for every dollar earned by a man, with African American women earning 64 cents and Latina women earning 56 cents, and the gap remains even after factoring in education and experience","When symphony orchestras began blind auditions using a screen, the number of women hired shot up, and most orchestras are now evenly divided between male and female musicians","The Implicit Association Test (IAT), developed by Mahzarin Banaji and Anthony Greenwald, measures the speed of people's positive and negative associations to a target group, and more than 15 million people have taken versions of it","Banaji herself 'failed' the racial IAT, revealing antiblack associations she consciously repudiates, and writer Malcolm Gladwell was shocked by his own results, though the test's originators disagree about what it measures","The evidence linking IAT scores with overt behavior is weak, and Adam Hahn found people were 'surprisingly accurate' in predicting their own responses, casting doubt on the claim that IAT results reflect unconscious attitudes","When white students were told they would shock a black 'learner', they initially gave lower intensity shocks, but after overhearing the learner insult them they administered higher levels of shock than did students working with a white learner","Christian Crandall and Amy Eshleman suggest people are attracted to justifications that let them express prejudice without feeling like bigots, avoiding the dissonance of being a fair person who dislikes a group","In Eden King's fat suit study, salespeople treated an obese woman just as nicely as a thin one when she drank a diet soda, but smiled less, made less eye contact, and spoke more abruptly when she drank a milkshake"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Discrimination Matrix: Stereotype Threat and Attribution Grid","visual_frame_description":"A testing hall where identical exams float under different banners - 'measuring your intellectual ability' versus 'testing the test' - while anxiety meters rise and fall; beneath them, essays about personal values glow green as buffers against the threat.","player_mission":"Run a grid of stigma experiments: black students told a hard verbal test measures intelligence versus 'testing the test', women taking a math test after affirming a value, white male engineers told about Asian math superiority, and minority students writing self-affirmation essays. Place each in the correct cell of threat versus buffer and predict the performance outcome.","key_concepts":["Attributional ambiguity is the extra layer of complexity that makes members of minority groups unsure whether feedback about their work reflects their performance or the evaluator's prejudice","Kent Harber found white evaluators gave overly positive feedback on poorly written essays when they believed the writer was black, being so eager to appear unprejudiced that they withheld the honest feedback students need to improve","Marlene Turner and Anthony Pratkanis found women told they were selected for a job because the company needed more women later denigrated their own abilities and stopped trying as hard as women selected on the basis of merit","Claude Steele and Joshua Aronson's stereotype threat research: black students who believed a difficult GRE verbal test measured their intellectual ability performed about half as well as black students who believed the investigators were merely testing the test","White students performed equally well regardless of whether they believed the test measured intelligence, showing the evaluative framing itself activated stereotype threat for black students","Stereotype threat also afflicts women working on math problems, Latinos on verbal ability tests, and elderly people on memory tests, and even white male engineering majors with near-perfect math SATs performed worse when told the test explored why Asians excel at math","Matthew McGlone and Joshua Aronson found that reminding women about to take a spatial ability test that they were students at a highly selective university completely eliminated the male-female gap","Exposing black test-takers to images of successful African American role models like W.E.B. Du Bois and Neil deGrasse Tyson eases their performance anxiety, and women who affirmed a personally important value scored as well as men under stereotype threat","Geoffrey Cohen's school interventions found minority students improved their grades after a few writing assignments explaining why certain values were important to them","Melvin Lerner showed people attribute personal responsibility for inequitable outcomes: if two people work equally hard and a coin flip gives one a reward, observers rate the unlucky person as having worked less hard","Blaming the victim - 'If the Jews have been victimized, they must have been doing something wrong' - is motivated by the desire to see the world as fair and just, and negative attitudes toward the poor are more prevalent among those who believe most strongly the world is just","Janet Swim and Lawrence Sanna analyzed more than 50 experiments and found a man's success is attributed to natural ability while a woman's is attributed to hard work, and a man's failure to bad luck while a woman's failure to not having what it takes"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Boss Battle: The Desegregation Campaign","visual_frame_description":"A schoolyard where the lunchtime clusters of black, Latino, and white students slowly dissolve into mixed groups holding puzzle-piece cards; in a classroom, a nervous Mexican American boy recites his paragraph while other children learn to ask gentle, probing questions.","player_mission":"Command a desegregated school under siege by hostility and self-segregated lunchtime clusters. Deploy the contact hypothesis, equal-status contact, superordinate goals, and the jigsaw classroom: divide the class into diverse six-person groups where each child holds one-sixth of Joseph Pulitzer's biography, and defuse the taunting of Carlos before the exam. Defeat the final wave by showing how cooperation changes 'those people' into 'us people'.","key_concepts":["The contact hypothesis predicted that bringing black and white children into direct contact would replace stereotypes with real human beings, and Morton Deutsch and Mary Ellen Collins found white residents of integrated housing projects reported greater positive attitude change than residents of segregated projects","Mere contact did not work in desegregated schools: schoolyards showed almost no integration, tension and turmoil rose, and Walter Stephan found no studies showing significant increases in self-esteem among black children while 25 percent showed significant drops","For contact to reduce prejudice it must take place with equal status, since throughout history most white contact with blacks occurred in menial roles as porters, dishwashers, and domestic workers that perpetuated stereotyping","Sherif reduced the Eagles-Rattlers hostility at Robbers Cave not by removing competition but by creating superordinate goals: the boys had to cooperate to repair the water-supply system and to pull the broken camp truck up a steep hill","The key factor in reducing hostility is interdependence in reaching mutual goals - a situation in which individuals need one another to succeed","Aronson invented the jigsaw classroom in 1971 to defuse the crisis of Austin, Texas, desegregation: each child in a diverse six-person group held one-sixth of a biography and needed the others to learn the material for the exam","In the jigsaw classroom, when Mary ridiculed Carlos's accent the assistant told her that taunting would not help her learn about Joseph Pulitzer's young adulthood with the exam in less than an hour, shifting the reinforcement contingencies","Children in jigsaw classrooms performed better on objective exams, liked each other better, developed greater liking for school and self-esteem, and formed friendships across ethnic and racial barriers, with absenteeism dropping","Diane Bridgeman found 10-year-olds from jigsaw classes could take the perspective of the letter carrier who did not know why a boy cried over a toy airplane, while traditional-classroom children assumed everyone knew what they knew","The jigsaw method works partly because cooperation breaks down ingroup-outgroup perceptions and creates a sense of 'oneness', and Leippe and Eisenstadt showed people who benefit others come to feel more favorable toward them - 'If I'm helping them, it must be because they deserve it'","Jay Van Bavel and William Cunningham found white participants assigned to a mixed-race team showed more positive automatic evaluations of black ingroup members than white outgroup members, driven by ingroup bias more than outgroup denigration","John McConahay called cooperative learning the single most effective practice for improving race relations in desegregated schools, yet educational systems resist change and the competitive classroom remains the norm"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"}]},{"id":"sa08","title":"Chapter 8: Liking, Loving, and Connecting","pages":"319-367","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Cue Scrubber: The Attraction Factor Detector","visual_frame_description":"A party where every pair of guests is linked by a glowing thread labeled with its factor - 'same dorm hall', 'both love birdwatching', 'she spilled coffee', 'he complimented her' - while a matchmaker's ledger tracks which threads hold and which fray.","player_mission":"Scan twelve couples at a party and tag what drew each pair together: proximity, attitude similarity, competence with a pratfall, physical attractiveness, being liked, or the paradox of choice. For each scene, name the factor, predict whether the pair will stick, and flag any couple where a gain or loss of esteem is doing the work.","key_concepts":["John Thibaut and Harold Kelley argued attraction is partly determined by our comparison level for alternatives - we calculate a person's value by comparing what they bring to us with what we might get from others","College students randomly assigned to dorm rooms named their next-door neighbors about half the time when asked who they liked best, showing proximity fuels friendship","James Brossard analyzed Philadelphia marriage records from the 1930s and found one in three marriages involved people who lived within five blocks, one in six on the same block, and only about one in five involved partners from different cities","The more similar a person seems in attitudes, opinions, and interests, the more you like them: 'Opposites may attract, but they don't stick'","We like people whose attitudes are similar to ours, and if we like someone for an irrelevant reason we assume they share our attitudes about politics, religion, and movies - causality runs in both directions","Hunter Gelbach found that making teachers and students aware of five similarities they shared increased mutual liking and respect, especially among minority students","In problem-solving groups, the participants considered most competent and having the best ideas tend not to be the ones best liked, because people who are too competent may seem unapproachable and make us look bad by comparison","The pratfall effect, demonstrated by Aronson, Willerman, and Floyd: the superior quiz-show candidate who clumsily spilled coffee was rated most attractive, while the average candidate who spilled it was rated least attractive","Abraham Tesser found we bask in the reflected glory of highly competent friends and relatives, but their attractiveness is diminished if their competence in areas that matter to us makes us feel inadequate by comparison","A 1966 analysis of more than 29,000 people found physical attractiveness matters equally to both sexes, contradicting the belief that looks matter more to men","Elaine Walster randomly matched incoming University of Minnesota students for a blind date and found the one determinant of whether they liked each other and repeated the date was physical attractiveness, not intelligence or personality scores","Gregory White found that among young UCLA couples, it was the similarity of the partners' physical attractiveness that determined whether they stayed together over many months"],"difficulty_level":"Beginner"},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Dialogue: The Beauty-and-Blunder Interrogation","visual_frame_description":"A dating lab with a wall of headshots; a misbehaved-child report slides in with an attractive face and gets excused while the same report with a plain face gets condemned; behind glass, an intercom hums as a woman's voice warms up for a man who believes she is gorgeous.","player_mission":"Interrogate the power of looks by running the classic studies yourself: pair blind-date students at random and ask which trait predicted a second date, show photos and predict future happiness ratings, grade classroom misbehavior reports with photos attached, and tape the intercom conversation where the man believes his unseen partner is beautiful. Then switch sides and decide whether the beautiful woman who criticizes you is liked or loathed.","key_concepts":["Karen Dion showed college students photos of attractive, average, and unattractive people and found the attractive were assigned the most desirable traits on all 27 ratings and given the greatest prognosis for future happiness","Karen Dion also found women evaluating classroom disturbance reports blamed less attractive children more, inferring the incident was typical of their everyday behavior, while attractive children's misbehavior was excused as a situational aberration","In the Aronson and Sigall experiment, a woman made to appear unattractive was liked about the same whether she gave favorable or unfavorable evaluations, but when beautiful she was liked a lot for favorable evaluations and disliked more than in any other condition when she criticized the men","Mark Snyder, Elizabeth Decker Tanke, and Ellen Berscheid found that when men believed their unseen phone partner was beautiful, independent observers rated the woman's side of the conversation as more attractive, confident, animated, and warm - a self-fulfilling prophecy that treating someone as attractive brings out desirable qualities","Attractive workers are often paid better and promoted more often, and the 'pro-beauty bias' begins at a very young age","Female high school students who watched 15 beauty commercials ranked beauty-oriented attributes as more important than did a control group that watched unrelated commercials","In sensitivity-training groups where people got acquainted by touch and voice with eyes closed, participants reported their prior stereotypes had been shattered by discovering that the 'funny-looking guy' was the warm, gentle person who had impressed them","Evolutionary arguments that beauty preferences are hardwired are controversial because the round face and plump body that are gorgeous in Tonga may be unattractive in Tallahassee","What is beautiful is good - but what is good becomes beautiful: people we dislike come to look less beautiful to us as we get to know them","Jon Freeman points out that we bring tremendous psychological baggage to face perception, so evaluating mates mainly with our eyes on Tinder may make us miss wonderful people whose beauty requires more than a quick glance","Half of all marriages end in divorce after about eight years, the question that opens the chapter's exploration of why some couples stay together till death like Annie Oakley and Frank Butler","The Harvard study of 75 years of Boston men concluded 'Good relationships keep us happier and healthier. Period.', and the happiest men were those who had prioritized their relationships, not the most accomplished or wealthy"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Audit: The Liking Ledger Field Log","visual_frame_description":"A bookkeeper's office where every interaction posts to a ledger with two columns - rewards given and costs paid - while a Franklin-style library book floats overhead and a speed-dating table glows with selectivity meters.","player_mission":"Keep a ledger of liking experiments: log what happens when someone is told you like them, when praise arrives with an ulterior motive, when an insecure student rates her admirer, when a coffee shop confederate is plain or pretty, and when a favor is done for you versus done by you. For each entry, enter the gain or loss on the scale and note whether the Dale Carnegie playbook actually worked.","key_concepts":["Rebecca Curtis and Kim Miller found people led to believe another person liked them disclosed more, disagreed less, and behaved more warmly, and were in fact subsequently liked, while those told they were disliked were disliked - another self-fulfilling prophecy","Elaine Walster found female students who received negative personality test feedback liked the smooth young man who had made a date with them more than students who received positive feedback: the more insecure we feel, the more we like someone who likes us","Sara Kiesler and Roberta Baral found male students led to feel insecure after a poor test performance showed more romantic interest toward a plain confederate, while secure students showed more interest toward an attractive one","Paul Eastwick and Eli Finkel's speed-dating study found people who seemed likely to say yes to everyone were perceived as less desirable and having less chemistry, while those perceived as selective were more readily reciprocated","Teresa Amabile found college students considered a negative novel reviewer considerably more intelligent, competent, and expert than a positive reviewer - but less likable","Jason Lawrence found black female students who were praised with 'Great job!' on a test rated the white evaluator as significantly less polite, suspecting the praise revealed surprise about their performance","Edward Jones found liking dropped sharply when an evaluator's praise came with a possible ulterior motive: 'flattery will get you somewhere - but not everywhere'","Favors are rewards, but not if they come with strings attached that threaten the freedom of the receiver, as when a borderline student presents an expensive gift just before grading","Benjamin Franklin won over a political enemy by asking to borrow a scarce, curious book from his library, and Jon Jecker and David Landy's experiment confirmed it 230 years later: students cajoled into returning their winnings as a special favor liked the experimenter best","Tolstoy wrote in War and Peace, 'We do not love people so much for the good they have done us, as for the good we have done them'","Aronson's gain-loss theory predicts we like someone whose liking for us increases over time more than someone who has always liked us, and dislike someone whose esteem decreases more than someone who always disliked us","In the Aronson and Linder experiment, women in the gain condition (negative evaluations that became positive) liked the confederate significantly better than those in the all-positive condition, and a true gain requires an integrated, gradual change of heart"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Discrimination Matrix: The Choice and Exchange Sorting Grid","visual_frame_description":"A supermarket aisle where one table overflows with 24 gleaming jam jars and another holds six; beside it a relationship split-screen shows one couple splitting the dinner check to the penny while another partner watches a light panel to see if her loved one needs help.","player_mission":"Sort dating profiles and relationships into a matrix: jam tables with 24 versus 6 varieties, Derek dismissing a beautiful Red Sox fan, exchange relationships keeping score, communal relationships responding to needs, and prenuptial agreements. For each card decide whether it belongs to the paradox of choice column or the exchange versus communal column, and predict whether the shopper buys, the couple sticks, and the friend pays the bill.","key_concepts":["Barry Schwartz's paradox of choice: people always say they want more options, but too many choices can impede us from making any decision at all and leave us less happy with the choices we finally make","Sheena Iyengar's jam study: 60 percent of passersby stopped for the 24-jam display versus only 20 percent for the six-jam display, but only 3 percent of those with the wide array bought jam while 30 percent of those with the narrow array did","Online dating turns every searcher into 'Derek', who deleted a beautiful, witty woman with a great job because she liked the Red Sox - an impossibility for the Derek of 1993, showing choice breeds pickiness and paralysis","As Barry Schwartz concluded, unlimited choice leaves us comparing our current choice to a fantasy, 'and nobody can compete with a fantasy'","Margaret Clark and Judson Mills distinguished exchange relationships, where people are concerned about reciprocity and equity, from communal relationships, where neither partner is keeping score and each gives in response to the other's need","In the Clark and Mills light-monitoring experiment, participants paired with a stranger watched the lights more when a signal meant a possible reward, while participants paired with a close friend watched more when the signal meant the friend might need help","Clark and Mills suggested prenuptial agreements are more likely to undermine than enhance the intensity of feelings, and someone who counts every penny at dinner is telling the other person they want an exchange rather than a communal relationship","In an exchange relationship with a major imbalance, the person getting less feels angry or depressed while the person getting more feels guilty","Partners in communal relationships are not totally unconcerned with equity but are relaxed about it, having faith that over the long haul things will even out","The lonely widower's story and the Harvard study's George Vaillant capture the chapter's thesis: 'Connection is the whole shooting match'","Lonely people have been found to have less efficient sleep patterns and higher blood pressure, and when wounded they heal more slowly than people who are not lonely","Living single is perfectly healthy; half of American adults choose to live alone, and what causes problems is feeling alone and being isolated, not being single"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Boss Battle: The Companionate Love Keep","visual_frame_description":"A relationship ark sailing past two whirlpools labeled 'dive in too fast' and 'the high wears off'; the deck holds cards for destiny versus growth beliefs, secure-attachment anchors, a four-horsemen armor set, and two porcupines huddling at a tolerable distance in the snow.","player_mission":"Defend a relationship through the two danger points: the passionate peak when couples dive in headfirst, and the fade when the dopamine high wears off after a year to 18 months. Deploy the right weapons at each wave - implicit theories of love, secure attachment, straight talk against Gottman's four horsemen, authentic disclosure, and the porcupine's dilemma balance - before the final boss: the Dotings' plateau of stagnant compliments.","key_concepts":["Love researchers distinguish passionate love, characterized by strong emotions, unquenchable sexual desire, and intense preoccupation with the beloved, from companionate love, a milder, more stable experience marked by mutual trust, dependability, and warmth","Passionate love floods the brain with dopamine, the same neurotransmitter released by cocaine, and the high wears off after about a year to 18 months, when companionate love must arise to take its place","Jonathan Haidt identifies two danger points: moving in or marrying too quickly at the peak of passion, and giving up when the initial phase fades, as couples who believe only obsessive love is real become repeatedly disappointed","Spike W. S. Lee and Norbert Schwarz found that recalling conflicts made people with a 'soul mate' unity frame of love feel less satisfied with their relationship, but people who framed love as a journey were unaffected","C. Raymond Knee's longitudinal study found 'destiny' believers' satisfaction depended on meeting ideal standards and they used avoidance coping, while growth believers remained more satisfied and worked to repair fraying seams","Phillip Shaver, Cindy Hazan, and Mario Mikulincer describe adult attachment styles: securely attached lovers are rarely jealous, anxious lovers are clingy and fear abandonment, and avoidant lovers distrust intimacy and keep partners at a distance","Attachment theory holds that children form internal working models of relationships from their parents' care, and the Minnesota Longitudinal Study found poorly attached children become the less-committed partners in adult relationships, creating a self-fulfilling prophecy of rejection","Insecurely attached adults can be helped by a securely attached partner, and Eli Finkel's research with Ximena Arriaga found that the ability to trust the partner predicts a reduction in avoidant tendencies over time","The porcupine's dilemma, from Schopenhauer's parable, is the desire to achieve deep intimacy while remaining invulnerable to hurt, and people who fear rejection place self-protection over self-revelation, losing the warmth of connection","John Gottman identified four destructive forms of communication that predict marriage failure: hostile criticism, defensiveness, contempt (the most devastating sign), and stonewalling","Fincham and Bradbury studied 130 newly married couples and found those who made dispositional attributions about their spouses became increasingly unhappy, while those who used straight talk and made situational attributions became increasingly happy","Shelley Gable found people who received enthusiastic responses from partners when describing a triumph were happier with their relationship months later than those who received flat responses"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"}]},{"id":"sa09","title":"Chapter 9: Social Psychology as a Science","pages":"367-484","assets":[{"asset_type":"CUE_SCRUBBER_STATION","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Cue Scrubber: The Scientific Method Workbench","visual_frame_description":"A laboratory workbench where a wagon with a rolling rubber ball and a smiling waiter's tip jar are fed through four glowing stations - observe, guess, hypothesize, experiment - while a gauge stamped with Feynman's quote rejects beautiful guesses that fail the test.","player_mission":"Scan twelve claims about the social world and process each through the four steps of the scientific method: observe the phenomenon, make a guess about the lawful relationship, frame a testable hypothesis, and design an experiment that can confirm or disconfirm it. For each claim, decide whether it can be tested experimentally or whether, like Kennedy's Bay of Pigs popularity spike, you must experiment on the underlying phenomenon instead.","key_concepts":["The scientific method is the best way we humans have of uncovering lawful relationships among things, whether chemicals, planets, or the antecedents of human prejudice or love","The steps of the scientific process are observation, a guess about why it happens, framing the guess as a testable hypothesis, and designing an experiment that will confirm or disconfirm the hypothesis","As Richard Feynman put it, 'It doesn't matter how beautiful the guess is or how smart the guesser is, or how famous the guesser is; if the experiment disagrees with the guess, then the guess is wrong'","There are no sacred truths in science - if a series of well-designed experiments fails to confirm a hypothesis, we give it up","Social psychologists perform experiments because people are often unaware of why they act in certain ways, so simply asking people to explain their behavior usually will not yield reliable results","When Kennedy's popularity rose after the Bay of Pigs fiasco, Aronson could not experiment on Kennedy himself but designed an experiment on the underlying phenomenon, testing whether a blunder makes a nearly perfect person more attractive","In social psychology we are not studying chemicals in a beaker but adults who have lived in a social world their entire lives, bringing their own ideas and theories about what causes behavior","Pavel Semonov distinguished science and art: as scientists we organize the unknown, as artists we reorganize the known to create something new, and a good social psychology experiment requires skills from both domains","The prison college program case shows scientific skepticism: the prisoners who volunteered for the program may have differed from nonvolunteers in motivation and ability, so the lower recidivism rate proves nothing about the program's effects","The New York State study found male inmates who completed college in prison had a recidivism rate 20 percent lower than average, but the comparison stacked the deck because the groups were not equivalent at the outset","As scientists we must face our biases and preferences, but the scientific method lets us separate the wheat from the chaff to determine which programs are worthy of our time, effort, and money","The science part of an experiment is easier to replicate than the art part, just as a chef's souffle recipe rarely survives the transfer to another kitchen"],"difficulty_level":"Beginner"},{"asset_type":"DYNAMIC_DIALOGUE_SIM","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Dialogue: The Aronson-Mills Initiation Trial","visual_frame_description":"A theater of the absurd: women recite 12 obscene words behind a curtain, then listen through headphones to a recorded discussion of spider mating dances while a rating scale collects their verdicts; a judge's gavel hovers over 'the dependent variable'.","player_mission":"Play the experimenter behind the group-discussion screen: randomly assign sixty-three college women to a severe initiation, a mild initiation, or no initiation, let each eavesdrop on the same excruciatingly dull tape-recorded discussion of secondary sex characteristics, and collect their ratings. Then argue the case for why random assignment - not the fraternity survey - is the only way to know that severity of initiation causes liking.","key_concepts":["In the Aronson-Mills experiment, sixty-three college women who volunteered for discussions on the psychology of sex were randomly assigned to a severe initiation (reciting obscene words and explicit passages), a mild initiation, or no initiation at all","The severity of initiation was the independent variable - the cause the experimenter manipulates - and the women's ratings of how interesting and worthwhile the discussion was constituted the dependent variable","All participants heard the same incredibly dull tape-recorded discussion of the secondary sex characteristics of lower animals, yet women who suffered a severe initiation rated it as really exciting while mild- and no-initiation women rated it as dull","The greatest advantage of the laboratory experiment is not control but random assignment: each participant has an equal chance of being in any condition, so uncontrolled variables are distributed randomly and cannot systematically affect results","The hypothetical intelligent-wife study shows why correlation is not causation: happy men may attract intelligent women, or a third factor like wealth may cause both happiness and marrying an intelligent woman","From survey data alone we cannot conclude severe initiations cause liking, because an elite group might require severe initiations, or a third variable like sadomasochistic tendencies could cause both severity and mutual liking","The random assignment of children to watch a violent video or an athletic event neutralized personality differences across conditions, strongly suggesting watching violence can lead to violence","To evaluate the prison college program properly, researchers should randomly select half of twice as many volunteers for the program and place the other half in the control condition","The experimental method eliminates third-variable explanations in one fell swoop: sadomasochists have just as much chance of being assigned to the no-initiation condition as to the severe-initiation condition","A survey showing severe-initiation fraternity members like each other more provides evidence of a positive correlation, but says nothing about cause and effect","An experiment must have impact on participants as well as control, and the difficulty is that these two crucial factors often work in opposite directions - as one increases the other tends to decrease","Merrill Carlsmith and Aronson distinguished experimental realism, whether the experiment has impact and forces participants to take it seriously, from mundane realism, whether the laboratory situation resembles everyday life"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DECEPTION_AUDIT_FILE","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Audit: The Correlation-Versus-Causation Field Log","visual_frame_description":"A countryside of observations: a living room where violent TV hours and playground punches rise together, a prison classroom with a recidivism chart, a nursery where babies mirror tongues, and a wedding where happiness and intelligence swirl in a fog of possible causes.","player_mission":"Walk through a field of natural observations - TV violence and childhood aggression, prison education and recidivism, smart wives and happy husbands, newborn imitation - and audit each one for confounds. For every entry, propose the hidden third variable and then redesign the study with random assignment so the causal arrow can finally be trusted.","key_concepts":["The correlation between children's TV violence watching and their aggressive problem-solving does not prove causation, because aggressive children may simply like to watch aggression","The experiment that randomly assigned children to a violent video or an athletic event found those who watched violence behaved more aggressively afterward, showing the causal link","Replication, in which the original study is repeated by other investigators in other labs, clarifies for whom and under what conditions a finding applies and whether it is robust or a fluke","If scientists are unable to duplicate original results, either they did not replicate accurately or the original investigators' conclusions were limited or wrong","A well-done 1970s study seemed to show newborn infants imitate adults' facial expressions, but a much better-controlled recent experiment found this imitation skill is learned, beginning at about six months of age","The minority-student values essay intervention did not replicate everywhere - it works only in schools where minority students feel a great deal of prejudice against them","Several viral findings failed replication: age-related words making young people walk slowly, hand-washing reducing guilt, watching eyes increasing honesty, oxytocin increasing empathy, and warm coffee making people feel more loved","The alleged 'power pose' that went viral through a TED talk failed most replications, and Joseph Simmons and Uri Simonsohn's statistical analysis of 33 studies concluded the evidence was too weak to advocate power posing","Pop social psychology findings that fail replication do not mean the basic methods of experimentation are at fault - science is moving along as it should, correcting its mistakes","The public should be wary of sensational, improbable, or simplistic findings and ask, 'But have they been replicated?'","A failed replication sometimes catches a mistake in the original experimenters' procedures or analysis, and sometimes reveals a missing ingredient that was crucial to the original research","Milgram's obedience experiment was low in mundane realism - we are rarely asked to deliver shocks in everyday life - but high in experimental realism, as his twitching, stuttering participants demonstrated"],"difficulty_level":"Intermediate"},{"asset_type":"DISCRIMINATION_MATRIX","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Discrimination Matrix: The Deception and Realism Sorting Grid","visual_frame_description":"A film director's storyboard wall where each experiment is a scene with its cover story on a clapperboard; a sign flashes 'Do not tell the actor what the play is about' while a monitoring room shows participants straining to look good.","player_mission":"Sort every study on the board by realism type and deception status: Milgram's shock lab, the Asch line judgment, the Aronson-Mills embarrassment test, the eavesdropping discussion, and the staged intercom conversations. For each card, check the cover story cell, the participant-knows-the-truth cell, and the 'looking good' cell, then decide where the deception was ethically required for valid data.","key_concepts":["Deception is frequently necessary because participants, who are always amateur social psychologists, will behave in ways consistent with their own hypotheses if they know the true purpose of the study","Cover stories like the Aronson-Mills 'test for embarrassment' increase experimental realism by letting participants act naturally without knowing which behavior is being studied","Participants who know an experiment's purpose either try to perform in a way that puts themselves in a good light or try to help the experimenter make the study come out as they think it should - both outcomes are disastrous","When told a particular outcome indicated a good personality trait, participants worked to produce that outcome far more often than when told it reflected a negative trait","The Asch conformity experiment used deception because almost no one would conform if told in advance they were being tested for conformity, since conformity is considered a weak and unattractive act","In Milgram's obedience experiments around 65 percent of average citizens administered intense shocks, yet only about 1 percent of students predict they would obey - evidence that people, given half a chance, will try to look good","Without deception we would get a picture of how people would like to be rather than a picture of how people are","Deception presents three ethical problems: it is simply unethical to lie to people, it frequently leads to an invasion of privacy when participants cannot give informed consent, and procedures often entail pain, boredom, anxiety, or embarrassment","Robyn Dawes, Jeanne McTavish, and Harriet Shaklee's social dilemma experiments seemed innocuous with no deception, yet one man telephoned 24 hours later to return his $190 winnings because he felt miserable about defecting, showing no code of ethics can anticipate all problems","Social psychologists work on an ethical dilemma between two values they subscribe to: free scientific inquiry and the dignity of humans with their right to privacy","Aronson advocates a cost-benefit analysis: compare the benefits to science and society against the costs to the participants before deciding whether a procedure is ethical","Leonard Bickman and Matthew Zarantonello found that people who read that a high proportion of Milgram's participants had been fully obedient rated the procedure as more harmful and less ethical than those told hardly anyone had obeyed"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"},{"asset_type":"BOSS_BATTLE","domain":"Social Psychology | Situational vs Dispositional","topic":"Boss Battle: The Ethics of the Science Council","visual_frame_description":"A marble hearing chamber where a projector plays clips of twitching, stuttering participants and a defector returning his winnings; five golden guidelines engraved on the wall glow one by one as each case is acquitted, and a scientist's oath against misuse of findings scrolls beneath the bench.","player_mission":"Stand before the ethics council defending five experiments - Milgram's obedience lab, the Asch line study, the initiation embarrassment test, the Dawes social dilemmas, and Aronson's cheating at cards - against charges of lying, invasion of privacy, and discomfort. Win each round by applying Aronson's five guidelines: avoid intense pain, offer the real option to quit, seek alternatives to deception, debrief thoroughly to restore dignity, and never run a study 'just for the hell of it'.","key_concepts":["Aronson's five guidelines: avoid procedures that cause intense pain or discomfort, provide participants the real option to quit, be alert to alternative procedures to deception, debrief participants carefully to protect their dignity and leave them feeling good, and never undertake an experiment relying on deception or discomfort just for the hell of it","During debriefing, experimenters should carefully explain the true purpose of the experiment and the reasons for the deception, going out of their way to avoid making participants feel stupid or gullible for having fallen for it","Milgram worked hard after the experiment to make the experience useful and exciting for participants: several weeks later 84 percent were glad they had taken part, 15 percent felt neutral, and only 1 percent were sorry","A university psychiatrist who interviewed a random sample of Milgram's participants a year later found no evidence of injurious effects, with the typical response being that participation was instructive and enriching","Milgram confided that he believed much of the ethical criticism was fueled by the results he obtained rather than by the actual procedure he employed","The ethics of any experiment seem less problematic when the results tell us something pleasant or flattering about human nature than when they tell us something we would rather not know","Milgram's obedience experiment taught us a great deal about human behavior, and Aronson concludes he is glad Milgram went ahead with it despite the controversy","A social psychologist's decision to run an experiment depends on an assessment of its potential costs and benefits, even though we can never be absolutely certain of either in advance","Many participants have argued that self-discovery, such as Asch's participants learning they would conform, has therapeutic or educational benefit, but this does not in itself justify the procedures","The knowledge described in this book rests on the cooperation of tens of thousands of individuals who allowed their behavior to be studied, and our understanding rests on techniques that are well controlled and influential without violating the essential dignity of participants","The mechanisms of self-persuasion are powerful because the persuaded never know what hit them, and they can be used to get people to floss their teeth or to vote for particular candidates","Aronson names his own responsibilities: to educate the public about how social-psychological knowledge might be used for the greater good, to remain vigilant against its abuse, and to promote good research aimed at furthering our understanding of us social animals"],"difficulty_level":"Advanced"}]}]};
